{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Franken FDK AAC\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Franken FDK AAC is the Fraunhofer FDK AAC encoder (libfdk-aac 2.0.3, mstorsjo/fdk-aac) with the nu774/fdkaac 1.0.2 frontend, extended with switches that expose decisions FDK normally makes on its own: how stereo is coded, how the high band is rebuilt, where bands may be cut to zero, how transients are handled, how bits are distributed over time. It is meant for engineers who want to hear and measure what those decisions do, especially at the extremes: very low bitrates, forced wide bands, speech, streaming.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Author: Micha\u322 ? Dziwisz. Subject-matter consultant: Patryk Faliszewski. Built on open source: libfdk-aac (Fraunhofer IIS) and the nu774/fdkaac frontend.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Without any of the new switches the encoder produces exactly what stock FDK produces, byte for byte. Every switch is opt-in, and every one of them can also make things worse: they let you go where FDK's own tuning deliberately does not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Download\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Prebuilt Windows binaries are published as GitHub Releases and download without an account:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://github.com/michaldziwisz/franken-fdk-aac/releases/latest"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
https://github.com/michaldziwisz/franken-fdk-aac/releases/latest
}}}
}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 franken-fdk-aac-x64-vX.Y.Z.zip} is 64-bit, the {\f1 x86} one is 32-bit. Each zip contains the static encoder (no DLLs) and the full documentation. The repository itself holds sources only; building is described at the end of this document.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 First steps\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 All original frontend options work as before ({\f1 -p}, {\f1 -b}, {\f1 -m}, {\f1 -w}, {\f1 -a}, {\f1 -s}, {\f1 -f}, tagging); {\f1 fdkaac-franken-x64.exe --help} lists them together with the new ones.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 1.\tx360\tab Encode once with {\f1 --verbose} and read what the encoder actually set up:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi0 \f1 fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 2 -b 128000 --verbose -o out.m4a in.wav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 2.\tx360\tab Change one switch at a time and check in {\f1 --verbose}, under {\f1 franken overrides applied}, that it took effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 3.\tx360\tab Listen, and where it matters, measure: decode both versions and compare. The tools in {\f1 tools/} in the repository do this for holes, pre-echo and stereo error.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Recipes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Each of these is a tested starting point, not a preset to apply blindly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 # LC stereo at a low bitrate with a wide band: fewer holes, less pre-echo\line
fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 2 -b 32000 -w 17000 --hole-ladder 2 --avoid-holes 1 --window-shape 1 -o out.m4a in.wav\line
\line
# The same at 40 kbps, with a calmer attack detector for percussive material\line
fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 2 -b 40000 -w 18000 --hole-ladder 2 --avoid-holes 1 --window-shape 1 --attack-decay 0.9 -o out.m4a in.wav\line
\line
# More stereo width at the expense of the centre\line
fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 2 -b 96000 --side-bias 6 -o out.m4a in.wav\line
\line
# Quasi-VBR around 128 kbps: CBR engine with room to breathe\line
fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 2 -b 128000 --peak-bitrate 160000 --vbr-reservoir 6000 -o out.m4a in.wav\line
\line
# HE-AAC with a softer, less metallic high band\line
fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 5 -b 32000 --sbr-softness 4 -o out.m4a in.wav\line
\line
# HE-AAC v2 with a wider core than FDK picks\line
fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 29 -b 48000 --core-cutoff 7500 -o out.m4a in.wav\line
\line
# Stream that a listener tuning in decodes with SBR at once\line
fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 5 -b 48000 --sbr-header-period 1 -f 2 -o out.aac in.wav\line
\line
# AAC-LD with a wider core and more TNS against pre-echo (LD needs LOAS, not ADTS)\line
fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 23 -b 64000 --core-cutoff 12000 --tns-thresh 0.7 -f 10 -o out.loas in.wav\line
\line
# Extremely low HE-AAC v2: 8000 bps stereo\line
fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 29 -b 8000 --unlock-bitrate -o out.m4a in48k.wav\line
\line
# Full 42 kHz core from a 96 kHz source\line
fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 2 -b 320000 --uncap-bandwidth --core-cutoff 40000 -o out.m4a in96k.wav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The last one reports {\f1 core bandwidth: 42000 Hz} in {\f1 --verbose}: the requested 40000 Hz is snapped up to the next scalefactor band edge.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Switch reference\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the reference. Every switch gets its own card, and every card is built the same way, so you can read only the card you need.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 How a card reads\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The heading is the switch as you type it, with its argument. Under it:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab A single bold sentence: what the switch is for, in terms of sound or behaviour.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Applies to}: the profiles and situations where the switch has an effect. If a switch does nothing in a profile, it says so here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Values}: the legal range, the unit, and the default. The default is always the value that means "stock FDK decides".\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Input}: whether the switch takes a whole number, a decimal, a flag or text.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Then the explanation: the problem first, the mechanism in plain words, then what happens as you move the value up and down.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Examples}: for a numeric switch with room to move, a mild, a strong and a deliberately exaggerated setting in each direction. Enumerated switches get a list of every value instead.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Works with / against}: interactions, and which other switch masks this one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Measured}: only real measurements, with the material and conditions. The line is missing when there is no measurement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Under the hood}: one or two lines for the curious. Skip it freely.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Shared conventions\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These hold for every card and are not repeated in each one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Off = stock.} Every Frankenstein switch is off unless you give it. With none of them on the command line, the output is byte identical to stock FDK. "Off" is spelled {\f1 -1} for almost all of them (the sentinel meaning "leave FDK's own decision"), and {\f1 0} for {\f1 --core-cutoff}, {\f1 --side-bias}, {\f1 --side-knee} and {\f1 --mask-slope}, where {\f1 -1} would be a legal working value.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Decimal input.} Decimal switches accept {\f1 .} or {\f1 ,}, up to three decimal places. The value is rounded half away from zero, and the range is then checked on the rounded value. Anything else is an error: {\f1 1e1}, {\f1 nan}, {\f1 3x}, embedded spaces. Whole number switches reject fractions, so {\f1 128,5} where an integer is expected is an error, not a silent truncation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Units.} Four conventions appear throughout:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 dB}: a plain decibel offset, positive or negative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 x100}: an integer that is a hundred times the real value, so {\f1 100} means 1.00, {\f1 90} means 0.90, {\f1 400} means 4.00.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 Q8}: a fixed point integer where {\f1 256} means 1.0. So {\f1 128} is 0.5, {\f1 384} is 1.5, {\f1 512} is 2.0, {\f1 4096} is 16.0. Q8 is FDK's own internal scale, which is why it leaks into the command line.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 SFB}: a scalefactor band number, counted from {\f1 0} at the bottom of the spectrum upwards. A scalefactor band is a group of spectral lines that share one scalefactor, roughly following critical bandwidth: narrow at the bottom, wide at the top. How many bands exist depends on profile, sample rate and the coded bandwidth, so an SFB number is only meaningful once you know that count. {\f1 --verbose} prints it, for example {\f1 active SFBs (long): 49} at AAC-LC 48 kHz stereo.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Checking what took effect.} {\f1 --verbose} prints the effective, initialised encoder parameters before encoding starts, plus a line {\f1 franken overrides applied} listing the switches that actually deviate from stock in this run. If a value you set does not show up there, it did not take effect, and no amount of listening will tell you that faster.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 How {\f1 -b} is read\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Three conventions live side by side, and mixing them up is the most common way to get a surprising file size.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Normal operation}: {\f1 -b} is bits per second, but the frontend accepts the short form too. Small numbers are read as kilobits, so {\f1 -b 128} and {\f1 -b 128000} both mean 128 kbps, and {\f1 -b 1152} means 1152 kbps.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b With {\f1 --unlock-bitrate}}: {\f1 -b} is read literally as bits per second, with no kilobit shortcut. {\f1 -b 6000} is 6000 bps. {\f1 -b 1152} in this mode is 1152 bps, which is practically nothing; the program warns when a value looks like that mistake.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b With {\f1 --dab}}: {\f1 -b} is kilobits per second, in the range 8..192 and a multiple of 8. The encoder multiplies by 1000 itself, so {\f1 -b 96} is 96 kbps. If you combine {\f1 --dab} with {\f1 --unlock-bitrate}, {\f1 -b} goes back to being literal bps.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The upper ceiling is not a switch and cannot be lifted: an AAC frame holds at most 6144 bits per channel. At 48 kHz that is roughly 576 kbps per channel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Transport note for LD and ELD\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 AAC-LD ({\f1 -p 23}) and AAC-ELD ({\f1 -p 39}) work in this encoder, but they cannot be written into ADTS. Asking for {\f1 -f 2} with those profiles ends in "encoder initialization failed", and the failure is the transport format, not the profile. Use M4A ({\f1 -f 0}, the default), LATM ({\f1 -f 6} or {\f1 -f 7}) or LOAS ({\f1 -f 10}). LOAS is the practical choice when you want a bare stream to measure or to feed somewhere, because it is a self contained byte stream the way ADTS is for LC and HE-AAC.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Map of the groups\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The switches are grouped in {\f1 --help} roughly from easy to geeky, and this reference follows the same order.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b A. Start here}: the consumer knobs. Diagnostics, intensity stereo in one slider, speech tuning, and the two opt-in flags that remove built-in limits.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b B. Stereo coding}: mid/side, intensity stereo in detail, parametric stereo.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b C. Bandwidth and SBR}: the high band, its detectors, estimators and grid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b D. Masking, noise shaping and detail}: the psychoacoustic thresholds, the SNR the encoder demands per band, TNS and PNS.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b E. Core transients, pre-echo and spectral holes}: the LC, LD and ELD core under bit pressure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b F. Block switching and bitrate control}: the long/short decision, the bit reservoir and the quasi-VBR knobs.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b G. DAB+ output}: digital radio superframes.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 A. Start here\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Five switches. One of them only looks at the encoder, two of them tune it, and two remove limits that stock FDK enforces. If you are new to this build, read {\f1 --verbose} first and use it after every other switch you try.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 {\f1 --verbose}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Prints the encoder's real, initialised settings to stderr before encoding, so you can see what actually took effect.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: every profile and every transport. Changes nothing in the output stream.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: flag, no argument; default: off\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: flag\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The encoder makes a large number of decisions from your command line, and most of them are not the values you typed. Bandwidth gets snapped to a scalefactor band boundary. Bitrate selects a row in a tuning table that in turn sets PNS, intensity stereo and the SBR grid. A switch you spelled correctly may still be ignored because the profile has no such mechanism. {\f1 --verbose} is the only way to see the result of all that before you spend time listening.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What it prints: the profile (AOT), bitrate and bitrate mode, sample rate, channel mode, the effective core bandwidth in Hz together with its source ({\f1 from -w}, {\f1 from --core-cutoff}, or {\f1 auto}), afterburner state, transport and signaling, and the state of the coding tools the encoder picked for itself, that is TNS, PNS, intensity stereo and MS stereo on or off. When SBR is active it adds the SBR ratio, the effective start and stop frequency indices, the frequency scale, noise bands, amplitude resolution and, for the whole band, a {\f1 final BW (AAC+SBR)} estimate. It prints the reservoir budget block, which tells you the average bits per frame and the ceilings, and it prints {\f1 active SFBs (long)}, which is the number you need before setting any SFB based switch. At the end comes the list {\f1 franken overrides applied}, naming only the switches that really deviate from stock in this run.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Three readings repay attention. The core bandwidth is SFB anchored, so a typed {\f1 -w 17300} can come back as {\f1 17915 Hz}: the encoder cannot cut inside a band. Under SBR the {\f1 -w} value is ignored but still labelled {\f1 [from -w]}, which is confusing until you remember that {\f1 --core-cutoff} is the switch that works there. And {\f1 active SFBs (long)} is the ceiling for {\f1 --msbands}, {\f1 --isbands} and every {\f1 <sfb>} argument: asking for band 48 when only 40 are coded is not an error, it simply does nothing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Values:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 --verbose}: print the dump. There is no off switch, since omitting it is off.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Example in context:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 fdkaac -p 5 -b 64000 --verbose -o out.m4a in.wav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: nothing. It is the diagnostic for everything else in this reference, and the fastest answer to "did my switch do anything at all".\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Measured: at AAC-LC 48 kHz stereo it reports {\f1 active SFBs (long): 49}. At HE-AAC v2 48 kHz the default core cutoff comes out at 8613 Hz.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 {\f1 --is-aggression <0..100>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b One slider for how eagerly the encoder collapses upper bands to intensity stereo, trading stereo separation at the top for bits.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: profiles with a real stereo core, so AAC-LC, AAC-LD and AAC-ELD, and the core of HE-AAC. No effect on mono. Under HE-AAC v2 the stereo image is carried by parametric stereo on a mono core, so intensity stereo has nothing to act on.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 0..100}, where {\f1 0} is stock FDK; default: {\f1 -1}, off (identical to stock)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Intensity stereo (IS) replaces separate left and right spectra in the upper bands with one shared energy envelope plus a direction. The ear localises high tones poorly, so this is cheap: you drop half the data in those bands and pay in stereo separation at the top. On material with genuinely different highs in the two channels, for instance cymbals placed hard to one side or wide spatial effects, you pay audibly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Stock FDK is extremely shy about IS, which is why people conclude the switch does nothing. There are four gates, and a band needs to pass all of them at once: an admission gate that only considers IS when bitrate per band is below a threshold, a correlation threshold of about 0.95 between the channels, a minimum run of 6 consecutive bands, and a stable direction. Loosening one gate usually changes nothing, because another still blocks. {\f1 --is-aggression} moves all of them together, coherently: it drops the bitrate and bandwidth gate and loosens the correlation, direction and region requirements as the number rises.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Higher means more bands coded as IS, fewer bits spent on the top of the spectrum, and weaker stereo separation up there. {\f1 100} is the real maximum: gate removed, correlation loosened to roughly 0.475, a region of a single band is enough, and the direction tolerance is wide. Low values are not useful as a fine trim, because a small loosening often still leaves another gate binding; {\f1 1} changed nothing on our test material. The useful working window starts around 30.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is only one direction to travel, since {\f1 0} is already stock and stock is close to "never".\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild, more IS: {\f1 --is-aggression 30}. Bands start going to IS on correlated material. Image at the top narrows slightly, a few percent of bits freed for the rest.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong, more IS: {\f1 --is-aggression 50}. Clearly more bands in IS. Audible narrowing on cymbals and reverb tails, a real bit saving at 48..96 kbps.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated, more IS: {\f1 --is-aggression 100}. Almost anything that can go to IS does. The top of the image glues to the centre and panned highs drift. Useful at 32 kbps and below, and as a caricature to hear what IS sounds like when you let it run.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: the explicit {\f1 --is-*} switches in group B override whatever the slider set, so use the slider first and only reach for {\f1 --is-corr-thresh}, {\f1 --is-min-sfbs} and {\f1 --is-lr-ratio} if you need precision. {\f1 --is 0} disables intensity stereo outright and makes this switch irrelevant. {\f1 --is-force-lo} and {\f1 --is-force-hi} bypass every gate, so they do not need this slider at all. {\f1 --verbose} prints the effective correlation threshold and minimum band count, which is how you confirm the slider landed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Measured: on mix48 at AAC-LC 64 kbps, {\f1 1} produced no stream change, while {\f1 30}, {\f1 50} and {\f1 100} each changed the stream.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 {\f1 --speech}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Switches the SBR layer to its speech tuning: different inverse filtering, a different noise floor, and no parametric coding of the high band.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: HE-AAC and HE-AAC v2, that is wherever SBR is running, including ELD with SBR. No effect on AAC-LC, which has no separate speech mode anywhere in FDK.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: flag; default: off (music tuning)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: flag\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 SBR rebuilds the high band from the coded low band, and it needs to know how tonal the source is to decide how much to whiten the patched band and how much noise to fill in. Music and speech have very different answers. Speech at low bitrate suffers a specific artifact when the music tuning is used: the reconstructed top gurgles, because the estimator keeps deciding the patch is tonal when it is really a moving formant plus breath.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The flag changes several things at once: the inverse filtering decision table switches to the speech table, the noise floor weighting goes to the speech value, and parametric coding of the high band is disabled. For a podcast, an audiobook or dialogue at 24..48 kbps the result is a more natural top with less gurgling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If you want only part of what the flag does, the pieces are available separately: {\f1 --sbr-invf-table 1} gives just the speech decision table, {\f1 --sbr-nf-weight 1.0} just the speech noise weight, and {\f1 --sbr-softness 4} both of them, in each case without touching the crossover and without disabling parametric coding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For speech in plain AAC-LC there is no equivalent, and this reference will not pretend otherwise. The LC psychoacoustic model is the same for speech and music. What helps there instead is narrowing the band, since speech has little content high up, with {\f1 --core-cutoff 12000} to {\f1 14000}, and at very low bitrate turning on noise substitution with {\f1 --pns 1 --force-pns}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Values:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab omitted: music tuning (stock).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 --speech}: speech tuning.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Example in context:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 fdkaac -p 5 -b 32000 --speech -o speech.m4a podcast.wav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: the explicit SBR estimator switches in group C win over it, so {\f1 --sbr-invf} forces a fixed inverse filtering level regardless. {\f1 --sbr-invf-table} and {\f1 --sbr-nf-weight} follow {\f1 --speech} when left at {\f1 -1}, which is how a single flag can move both.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Measured: no effect at all on AAC-LC; changes the HE-AAC stream.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 {\f1 --uncap-bandwidth}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Removes FDK's hard 20 kHz limit on core bandwidth so {\f1 --core-cutoff} can reach up to Nyquist.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: any profile with a core you can widen, in practice AAC-LC at high bitrate from a high sample rate source. Meaningless without {\f1 --core-cutoff}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: flag; default: off (the 20 kHz cap applies)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: flag\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 FDK clamps core bandwidth to {\f1 min(20000, sr/2)}. The reasoning is sound for normal material: almost nobody hears above 20 kHz, and the bits are better spent lower. The consequence is that a 96 kHz source encoded at a generous bitrate still codes nothing above 20 kHz, no matter what you ask for, and the extra sample rate buys you only a gentler antialiasing slope.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This flag lifts the clamp. On its own it does nothing at all: it only raises the ceiling, it does not move the bandwidth. The switch that moves the bandwidth is {\f1 --core-cutoff <hz>}, and with the cap lifted that switch can go up to half the sample rate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Be clear about what you are buying. Content above 20 kHz costs bits that come out of the audible band unless your bitrate has real headroom, and streams with bandwidth beyond the usual range sit outside typical practice, so some decoders may balk. This is a deliberate opt-in, which is why it is a separate flag rather than a side effect of {\f1 --core-cutoff}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Values:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab omitted: core bandwidth clamped at 20 kHz (stock).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 --uncap-bandwidth}: clamp lifted, {\f1 --core-cutoff} free up to {\f1 sr/2}.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Example in context:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 fdkaac -p 2 -b 320000 --uncap-bandwidth --core-cutoff 40000 --ath-scale 200 \\\line
    -o out.m4a in96k.wav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: requires {\f1 --core-cutoff}. Under SBR, {\f1 -w} is ignored and {\f1 --core-cutoff} is the switch that works, so the pair is also the way to widen a core sitting under SBR. Pairs naturally with {\f1 --ath-scale} below 256 when you have bitrate to spare, since there is no point coding 40 kHz of band at thresholds tuned for scarcity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Measured: 96 kHz input, AAC-LC 400 kbps, broadband noise. Without the flag, {\f1 --core-cutoff 40000} gives {\f1 20000 Hz} in {\f1 --verbose} and essentially zero energy above 20 kHz. With the flag, {\f1 --verbose} reports {\f1 40000 Hz} and the decoded file carries about 10 percent of its energy in 20..24 kHz, about 20 percent in 24..32 kHz and about 20 percent in 32..44 kHz. This encoder has no 96 kHz test signal checked in, so treat that as a prior measurement rather than one you can reproduce from the shipped material.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 {\f1 --unlock-bitrate}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Removes the lower bitrate floors that every profile enforces, and makes {\f1 -b} literal bits per second.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: every profile. The floors removed differ by profile, see below.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: flag; default: off (all floors enforced)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: flag\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Stock FDK refuses to encode below a floor, and the floor is different in each profile and is often mistaken for a format limit. It is not. The common claim that HE-AAC cannot go below 16 kbps describes the lowest row of the SBR tuning table for 48 kHz stereo, nothing more. Ask for less and stock FDK quietly raises your bitrate to that row.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The flag removes five separate floors:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab the 40 bit per channel core minimum;\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab the LD and ELD rule of 8 kbps per channel, and the 208 bit transport reserve those profiles guess at (the real header overhead is still checked against the real transport encoder);\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab the start of the LD and ELD bandwidth table, so those profiles can use a band narrower than the table's first row;\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab the SBR and PS tuning table floor: HE-AAC, HE-AAC v2 and ELD with SBR below the table reuse its lowest row, keeping the same SBR grid while your bitrate is honoured and the AAC core simply gets fewer bits;\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab the rule that a frame unable to fit its own side information aborts the encode. Such frames overshoot instead, and the reservoir absorbs it over the following frames.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What remains is the transport header, 56 bits per frame for ADTS, and the upper ceiling of 6144 bits per channel, which no switch touches. Below the SBR payload estimate, about 2.5 kbps per channel plus parametric stereo, the core would have no bits at all and the encoder stops with a clean "encoder initialization failed" rather than an assertion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The flag removes floors. It does not guarantee any particular bitrate. At these values dense passages routinely come out above what you asked for, because headers and side information alone cost more than the budget, so judge the result by the file size and not by the number in {\f1 --verbose}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Mind the {\f1 -b} change. In unlock mode the number is bits per second, literally. {\f1 -b 6000} is 6000 bps. {\f1 -b 6} is 6 bps and will not encode anything useful, and {\f1 -b 1152} typed in the belief that it means 1152 kbps gives you 1152 bps. Always write the full number of bits here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Values:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab omitted: all floors enforced, {\f1 -b} read with the kilobit shortcut (stock).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 --unlock-bitrate}: floors removed, {\f1 -b} literal bps.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Example in context:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 fdkaac -p 29 -b 6000 --unlock-bitrate -f 2 -o out.aac in48k.wav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: {\f1 --vbr-reservoir} and {\f1 --peak-bitrate} become more interesting here, since overshoot is the normal case in this mode and the reservoir is what pays for it. With {\f1 --dab}, the DAB+ kilobit convention for {\f1 -b} is replaced by literal bps. Nothing in group D or F restores the floors.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Measured: real bitrate computed from the size of the ADTS or LOAS stream, with no container overhead:\par}
