{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Where the real bitrate exceeds {\f1 -b}, those are dense frames overshooting on headers and side information alone. The ffmpeg 6.1 column needs reading with care: it reports the same errors on the stock encoder in these modes, one {\f1 noise_facs_q is invalid} on HE-AAC v2 at 12..14 kbps, which is inside the stock tables, and many errors on every ELD or LD LOAS stream at any bitrate. ffmpeg does not implement LD and ELD fully, so judge those streams with the Fraunhofer decoder.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 B. Stereo coding\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Three different machines in this encoder turn two channels into fewer bits, and they work on different parts of the signal, with different costs. Before reaching for any switch in this group, get clear on which of the three you are actually talking about.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b MS (mid/side)} is a lossless rotation of the channel pair. Instead of left and right, the encoder stores mid (L+R) and side (L-R) for a given scalefactor band. Nothing is thrown away: the decoder rotates back and gets L/R exactly. On correlated music the side signal is small, so it quantises to far fewer bits than a second full channel would. MS is a decision about bit economy, not about sound, and it is taken per band and per frame. Available in the AAC core, which means LC, LD, ELD, and the core under HE-AAC when that core is still stereo.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b IS (intensity stereo)} is lossy and directional. Over a range of upper bands the encoder stops sending two spectra and sends one energy envelope plus a direction, so the decoder paints both channels from the same spectrum with a pan coefficient. That is cheap and the ear tolerates it up high, where it localises fine spectral detail poorly. It also genuinely removes information: anything that differed between left and right in those bands, cymbals placed hard to one side, spatial effects, out-of-phase content, collapses toward a single steered source. IS lives in the core too, and the encoder is cautious with it by default.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b PS (parametric stereo)} is the HE-AAC v2 machine. The core becomes mono: one AAC spectrum, one SBR high band, and the stereo image rides along as a handful of parameters per frame (level differences, coherence, optionally phase). Below roughly 32 kbps that buys you a usable stereo picture where real two-channel coding would be starving. PS is not a refinement of MS or IS, it replaces them: with {\f1 -p 29} there is no side channel to bias and no intensity bands to place, so every {\f1 --ms*} and {\f1 --is*} switch in this group is inert. Conversely {\f1 --ps*} does nothing outside HE-AAC v2.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In practice: at 96 kbps and up MS is doing almost all the work and you tune the mid/side bit split ({\f1 --side-bias}, {\f1 --side-knee}). Between about 40 and 80 kbps intensity stereo starts to earn its keep in the top bands ({\f1 --is-aggression}, then the {\f1 --is*} placement switches). Below about 32 kbps stereo, HE-AAC v2 and the PS parameters are the sane route, and the interesting knobs there are the resolution ones ({\f1 --ps-env-reduce}, {\f1 --ps-bands}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Reading SFB numbers.} Every band switch here counts scalefactor bands from 0 at the BOTTOM of the spectrum. Band 0 is bass, the highest number is the top of the coded range. How many bands exist depends on profile and sample rate: at LC 48 kHz stereo {\f1 --verbose} prints {\f1 active SFBs (long): 49}, so legal band numbers run 0..48. Always read that line before setting a range, because the count changes with the configuration and a number you remembered from another encode may be out of the spectrum entirely. Short blocks are numbered the same way, from 0 inside each window group, and a short frame has far fewer bands (around 14 at 44.1 and 48 kHz); a range that reaches above the short band count simply leaves short frames in plain L/R.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Why a range above the coded bandwidth does nothing.} The SFB count is the full table for the profile, but only bands up to the coded bandwidth carry anything. Bands above the cutoff are zero, and a stereo decision about a band of zeros changes no bits at all. This is a real trap with the high-band examples: at the default LC bandwidth of about 15.75 kHz, {\f1 --msbands-lo 44 --msbands-hi 48} produces exactly the same stream as {\f1 --msmask 0}, because bands 44..48 sit above the coded band and MS is therefore switched off everywhere that matters. Force the band open with {\f1 -w 20000} and the two stop being equal. So check your range against the coded bandwidth, not just against the SFB count.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 All switches in this group are off unless you give them. Off means stock: the sentinel {\f1 -1}, and {\f1 0} for {\f1 --side-bias}, {\f1 --side-knee} and {\f1 --mask-slope}, leaves FDK's own decision alone. {\f1 --side-bias}, {\f1 --side-knee} and {\f1 --mask-slope} take decimals, with {\f1 .} or {\f1 ,} and up to 3 decimal places; the rest are whole numbers and reject fractions.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 MS\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 MS costs nothing in sound quality, so there is no "MS damages the image" tradeoff to manage. What you can manage is where MS is allowed and how the bit budget is split between the mid and the side channel once a band is in MS. Two of these switches are the real control ({\f1 --msmask}, and the band range), two are honest curiosities ({\f1 --ms-bias}, {\f1 --ms-precision}), and three are the quality knobs that repay study ({\f1 --side-bias}, {\f1 --side-knee}, {\f1 --mask-slope}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --msmask <n>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Force the mid/side decision globally instead of letting the encoder choose per band.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: stereo AAC core (LC, LD, ELD, and the stereo core under HE-AAC). No effect in HE-AAC v2, where the core is mono.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 -1} auto (default, FDK decides per band), {\f1 0} off (pure L/R everywhere), {\f1 1} on (MS in every band); default {\f1 -1}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The encoder normally tests each band both ways and keeps whichever packs smaller. That is close to optimal for file size, and because the transform is lossless it is also close to irrelevant for sound. Two situations make the forced settings worth having anyway.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --msmask 0} guarantees that the coder never mixes your channels. Quantisation noise then stays inside the channel that caused it, instead of being shared between mid and side and redistributed to both outputs on reconstruction. That matters if the file will later be put through center cancellation, vocal removal, or any other subtraction of one channel from the other: with pure L/R the residual is your material plus its own noise, with MS it is your material plus noise that was fitted to the sum and difference. It costs bits, and on correlated music it costs real bits.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --msmask 1} is the opposite extreme, MS forced even where the encoder judged L/R cheaper. Useful as a reference point and for strongly correlated material, slightly wasteful on material with genuinely independent channels, for instance two unrelated mono sources hard panned.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Values:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 -1}: stock. Per band decision from the encoder's own cost comparison.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 0}: pure L/R in every band. Nothing is mixed between channels.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 1}: MS in every band.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 fdkaac -p 2 -b 128000 --msmask 0 in.wav -o lr.m4a\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: {\f1 --msmask 0} makes the whole mid/side family inert, including {\f1 --msbands}, {\f1 --side-bias}, {\f1 --side-knee} and {\f1 --mid-bias}, because no band is in MS. {\f1 --msbands}/{\f1 --msbands-lo}/{\f1 --msbands-hi} narrow where the chosen mask may apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Measured: on a 2 second sample the difference between {\f1 --msmask 0} and {\f1 --msmask 1} is about 900 bytes of payload, which puts the whole MS decision in perspective next to {\f1 --ms-bias} at about 2 bytes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --msbands <n>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Allow MS only in the N lowest scalefactor bands and leave everything above in L/R.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: stereo AAC core. No effect in HE-AAC v2.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 -1} no limit (default), or {\f1 0..N} where N is the active SFB count; {\f1 n} means bands {\f1 0..n-1} may use MS\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the "from the bottom up to" form of the band limit. It is counted from the bass upward, always, so {\f1 --msbands 6} means bands 0 to 5 may be coded mid/side and bands 6 and above stay strictly L/R. {\f1 --msbands 0} allows MS nowhere, which is the same thing as {\f1 --msmask 0}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Why restrict MS at all, given that it is lossless? Because the bit split follows the mask. Inside MS bands the encoder allocates against the mid and side thresholds, and the side channel of a band is only as good as the bits the budget allows it. Keeping the top of the spectrum in L/R keeps the channel separation up there independent of that negotiation, which is a defensible choice on material whose high content really is uncorrelated. It also pairs well with high band experiments: L/R up top and MS down low is a different balance of risks from the encoder's own per band choice.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The difference from {\f1 --msbands-lo}/{\f1 --msbands-hi} is only the shape of the region: this switch always starts at band 0, the pair can start anywhere.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild restriction: {\f1 --msbands 40}. Only the very top bands are pushed back to L/R; on most material the stream barely moves, because those bands often carried little.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong restriction: {\f1 --msbands 12}. MS confined to roughly the bass and low mids. Expect a measurably larger file at the same quality target, or coarser quantisation at the same bitrate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated restriction: {\f1 --msbands 2}. MS on essentially nothing. A caricature: you are paying nearly the full price of independent stereo while still signalling MS in two bands.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: equal to {\f1 --msbands-lo 0 --msbands-hi n-1} ({\f1 --msbands 6} and {\f1 --msbands-lo 0 --msbands-hi 5} give the same stream). Inert under {\f1 --msmask 0}. A limit above the active SFB count is the same as no limit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --msbands-lo <n>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Lowest scalefactor band of the range in which MS is allowed.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: stereo AAC core. No effect in HE-AAC v2.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 -1} off (default), or {\f1 0..N} within the active SFB count; pair it with {\f1 --msbands-hi}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Together with {\f1 --msbands-hi} this defines a from..to window: MS may be used in bands {\f1 lo..hi} inclusive and nowhere else. Unlike {\f1 --msbands}, the window does not have to touch the bottom of the spectrum, which is the only way to put MS exclusively high up, for instance to let the encoder merge correlated noise and air at the top while the bass and mids stay in fully independent L/R.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Give both halves of the pair. {\f1 --msbands-lo} on its own leaves the upper edge unset, which is not a range.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild, window starting low: {\f1 --msbands-lo 2 --msbands-hi 48}. Almost everything may use MS, the two lowest bands are excluded. Near stock in size.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong, window in the middle: {\f1 --msbands-lo 10 --msbands-hi 30}. MS only across the mids. Bass and top are L/R, so the file grows and the top keeps full separation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated, window at the very top: {\f1 --msbands-lo 44 --msbands-hi 48} with {\f1 -w 20000}. MS on five bands of air only. A caricature of the band range feature, and the clearest demonstration of why the coded bandwidth matters: without {\f1 -w 20000} these bands are above the default 15.75 kHz cutoff and the setting degenerates into {\f1 --msmask 0}.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: must be read against {\f1 --verbose} {\f1 active SFBs} and against the coded bandwidth. Inert under {\f1 --msmask 0}. Overlaps {\f1 --msbands}, which is the same feature anchored at band 0.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --msbands-hi <n>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Highest scalefactor band, inclusive, of the range in which MS is allowed.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: stereo AAC core. No effect in HE-AAC v2.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 -1} off (default), or {\f1 0..N} within the active SFB count; pair it with {\f1 --msbands-lo}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The upper edge of the from..to window described above, and inclusive: {\f1 --msbands-hi 5} includes band 5. Bands above it go to plain L/R.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In short blocks the number means the same frequency region, counted from 0 inside each window group, and short frames have only about 14 bands at 44.1 and 48 kHz. A range whose upper edge sits above the short band count leaves short frames in L/R rather than failing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --msbands-lo 0 --msbands-hi 44}. Top four bands excluded from MS. Small change.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --msbands-lo 0 --msbands-hi 20}. MS up to roughly the presence region, L/R above.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --msbands-lo 0 --msbands-hi 0}. MS in exactly one band, the bass. Functionally independent stereo with one band of bookkeeping.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: {\f1 --msbands-lo} is the other half; without it there is no range. A value above the active SFB count is the same as no upper limit. Inert under {\f1 --msmask 0}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --ms-bias <n>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Nudge the L/R versus MS cost comparison toward MS.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: stereo AAC core. No effect in HE-AAC v2.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 -1} off (default, stock comparison), {\f1 0} also stock, {\f1 1..255} in Q8 where 256 would be 1.0 in FDK's internal log units, so 128 is +0.5\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Be honest with this one: it is a binary nudge dressed as a slider, and it is the weakest switch in the group. Measurement says so plainly. {\f1 0} gives the stock stream; every value from 1 to 255 gives one and the same different stream. There is no gradient to ride, so there is nothing to tune.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason is structural. MS is lossless, so this threshold does not decide how a band sounds, only which of two encodings is cheaper to write. The encoder's own comparison is already close to optimal, so a shift of the decision line can only flip bands that were nearly tied, and nearly tied bands cost nearly the same either way. The net effect on file size is a fraction of a percent, and the channel correlation after decoding moves in the fourth decimal place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Use it to confirm that the decision point exists, not to shape sound. For real control over MS, {\f1 --msmask 0} or {\f1 --msmask 1} and the band range switches do the job properly, and {\f1 --side-bias} is the knob that actually changes what you hear.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Values:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 -1} or {\f1 0}: stock cost comparison.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 1..255}: one single alternative stream, slightly more eager to pick MS.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 fdkaac -p 2 -b 128000 --ms-bias 128 in.wav -o biased.m4a\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: pointless together with {\f1 --msmask 0} or {\f1 --msmask 1}, which remove the decision entirely. Superseded for any practical purpose by {\f1 --msmask} and {\f1 --side-bias}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Measured: mix48 at 128 kbps and tonal48/sparse48 at 96 kbps, values 1..255 all produce an identical stream that differs from {\f1 0} only. Size effect under 0.1 percent, on the order of 2 bytes on a 2 second sample.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --ms-precision <n>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Scale the quantisation precision of MS bands as a whole, mid and side together.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: stereo AAC core, MS bands only. No effect in HE-AAC v2.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: Q8 multiplier from {\f1 256} (no change) upward, {\f1 384} is about 1.5x, {\f1 512} about 2x, no upper limit; values below 256 are rejected; {\f1 -1} off (default)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the closest thing here to a LAME style global quality lever for the stereo pair: raise it and the thresholds in MS bands get tighter, which means shallower spectral holes and finer quantisation in both mid and side at once. It is blunt, because it moves the mid and the side in the same direction and by the same factor, so it cannot shift the balance between them, which is usually what you actually want.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Its reach is also limited in two ways worth knowing before you spend time on it. Above roughly 600 to 800 the thresholds run into FDK's own hard floor and further increases stop doing anything. And under CBR the bit budget does not grow, so a tighter threshold in MS bands mostly moves bits from somewhere else in the same frame rather than adding detail. The effect flattens out into a reshuffle.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Reach for {\f1 --side-bias} instead when the goal is stereo quality: it acts per channel, at the decision that actually drops or keeps a side band.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --ms-precision 320}. Slightly tighter MS thresholds, roughly 1.25x. Small reduction in zeroed bands inside MS.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --ms-precision 512}. About 2x. Noticeably fewer holes in MS bands; under CBR expect the gain to be paid for elsewhere in the frame.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --ms-precision 2048}. Deep into the saturated region, where the threshold floor has already taken over. A caricature: the number looks dramatic and the sound stops tracking it. There is no other direction: the switch only tightens. To make MS bands coarser on purpose, raise the side threshold with a negative {\f1 --side-bias}, or the mid threshold with {\f1 --mid-bias}.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: inert under {\f1 --msmask 0}. Overlaps {\f1 --mid-bias} and {\f1 --side-bias}, which move one channel at a time; if you are using those, leave this at default.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Under the hood: a Q8 multiplier applied to the MS band thresholds, hence 256 meaning 1.00.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --mid-bias <n>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Deliberately raise the mid channel threshold so the freed bits can go to the side.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: stereo AAC core, MS bands. No effect in HE-AAC v2.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: Q8, {\f1 256} = off, above 256 raises the mid threshold, no upper limit; {\f1 -1} off (default)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 After the MS rotation the mid channel carries the sum, which on normal music is most of the loudness and most of the information people listen to. This switch raises its masking threshold on purpose, which makes the mid cheaper to code and leaves more of a fixed budget for the side. It is a legitimate way to buy stereo width, and a risky one, because you are paying with the channel that carries the melody.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Use it sparingly, and prefer {\f1 --side-bias} for the same job. Both pull on the same mid/side budget, but {\f1 --side-bias} pulls from the side end, where the measurement is cleaner and where the effect of overdoing it shows up as a narrower image rather than a duller lead vocal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --mid-bias 288}. Mid threshold raised slightly, a little headroom handed to the side. Hard to fault on dense material.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --mid-bias 384}. About 1.5x. The side gets a real share; the mid starts to lose definition at low bitrate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --mid-bias 1024}. The mid is given away to feed the difference signal. A caricature: a wide, hollow image with a soft centre, which is almost never what anyone wants from a music encode.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: inert under {\f1 --msmask 0}, and pointless in combination with a large positive {\f1 --side-bias}, since both spend the same budget from opposite ends. Overlaps {\f1 --ms-precision}, which moves mid and side together instead.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --side-bias <dB>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Shift the side channel masking threshold on MS bands: the main stereo width versus centre quality lever.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: stereo AAC core, MS bands. No effect in HE-AAC v2, and nothing to do under {\f1 --msmask 0}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 -24.0..50.0} dB, {\f1 0} = off (default, stock threshold)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: decimal, up to 3 places, {\f1 .} or {\f1 ,}\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the switch to learn in this group. The encoder decides, per scalefactor band, whether the side signal is worth coding by comparing its energy against a masking threshold; below the threshold the band is dropped to zero. That one comparison is where your stereo width lives. Moving the threshold moves the line.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A positive value lowers the effective bar for the side channel: fewer side bands are zeroed, the survivors are quantised more finely, and what you hear is cleaner width, reverb tails that decay instead of stopping, and ambience that stays put instead of collapsing to the centre. It is not free. The bit budget is fixed, so those bits come out of the mid channel, and at low bitrate you can hear the centre give them up. That is the tradeoff working as designed, not a fault. The measurement below shows exactly this shape: the side error improves steadily from +1 to +9 while the mid error degrades steadily over the same span.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A negative value does the reverse and raises the bar, so side bands fall out and the image narrows toward mono. That is a deliberate tool for very low bitrate work, or for an effect, and it has one hard structural feature you need to know about: it does not narrow smoothly all the way down.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b The step at about -0.97 dB.} From 0 to -0.95 dB the side error moves smoothly, which is the gentle narrowing you would expect. At about -0.97 dB the raised side threshold crosses a minimum SNR line that the encoder keeps inside its hole avoidance logic, and most side bands drop out at once. Past that point the knob is finished: -1, -4 and -12 all give the same result, because the side has already been cut. This is a property of the encoder, a real discontinuity in its decision chain, not a rounding artefact of the command line: the decimals are read to 3 places and the value you type is the value used. The practical rule follows directly. For gentle narrowing stay inside 0 to -0.95, and treat anything at or beyond -0.97 as a single setting that means "drop the side".\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In the positive direction the useful span ends around +9. Beyond that, measurement shows both channels getting worse: the side stops improving and the mid keeps degrading, which is a bad trade in both directions at once.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild, more bits to the side: {\f1 --side-bias 1}. A touch more side detail, mid essentially untouched. Safe on anything.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong, more bits to the side: {\f1 --side-bias 6}. Clearly wider and more spacious, with the mid starting to pay for it at low bitrate. This is the normal working value.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated, more bits to the side: {\f1 --side-bias 24}. Past the useful span. The side gains nothing further and the mid is measurably damaged, so the image gets wide and the centre gets thin. A caricature that shows where the budget went.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild, narrowing: {\f1 --side-bias -0.3}. Slightly tighter image, a few marginal side bands given up. The fine control lives in this region.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong, narrowing: {\f1 --side-bias -0.9}. Noticeably narrower, still inside the smooth region and still just short of the step.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated, narrowing: {\f1 --side-bias -4}. Past the -0.97 dB step, so the side is simply gone and the result is near mono. A caricature, and identical to -1 or -12: there is nothing left to remove.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: {\f1 --side-knee} shapes the edge of the same decision and combines cleanly with this. {\f1 --mid-bias} pushes on the same budget from the mid end, so using both hard is double counting. {\f1 --msmask 0} removes the side channel entirely and makes this switch inert. {\f1 --msbands}/{\f1 --msbands-lo}/{\f1 --msbands-hi} limit which bands it can touch.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Measured: dense48, LC stereo 40 kbps, Fraunhofer decoder, error energy relative to the source per channel. Default band (9.75 kHz): side error -0.53, -0.58, -0.62, -0.63, -0.66 dB at 0, +1, +3, +6, +9, while mid error goes -8.62, -8.53, -8.27, -7.08, -6.43 dB. At +24 side gets worse again (-0.42 dB) and mid keeps losing (-5.81 dB). Negative direction with the band forced to 18 kHz ({\f1 -w 18000}): side error -1.13 dB at 0, -1.03 at -0.5, -0.93 at -0.9, -0.92 at -0.95, then -0.31 at -0.97 and the same at -1, -4 and -12. Which quantity shows the step depends on material and bandwidth: at the default band on the same signal the step shows in the mid error instead (-8.72 to -9.04 dB at -0.97).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --side-knee <dB>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Shape how abruptly a side band flips between coded and zeroed at the threshold.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: stereo AAC core, MS bands. No effect in HE-AAC v2, and nothing to do under {\f1 --msmask 0}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 -24.0..50.0} dB, {\f1 0} = off (default, hard cliff as in stock)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: decimal, up to 3 places, {\f1 .} or {\f1 ,}\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --side-bias} moves where the coded/zeroed line sits. This switch changes the character of the line itself. Stock behaviour is a cliff: the moment a band's energy falls to the threshold, the entire band is thrown away, and the band next to it that cleared the threshold by a hair is coded normally. On decaying material that produces a brittle edge, because the side content of a reverb tail does not fade out, it switches off band by band.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A positive value makes the knee soft. Bands sitting up to N dB below the threshold are still kept, coded at the coarsest scalefactor rather than dropped, so the side fades instead of stopping. Air and reverb decay more smoothly and the stereo picture stays coherent while it gets quiet. It costs bits, spent on bands the encoder had already judged inaudible, so it is a quality setting rather than an efficiency one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A negative value makes the knee hard. Bands that only just clear the threshold, within N dB above it, are zeroed anyway, which cuts the side off earlier than stock. The result is leaner and more aggressive, and it frees bits for the bands that clearly matter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The negative direction saturates early: by -6 dB everything marginal has already been cut, and -6, -12 and -24 produce the same result. Treat -6 as the floor of the control and do not bother typing bigger numbers. The positive direction keeps changing across the range, so there is genuine room to explore upward.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild, soft knee: {\f1 --side-knee 2}. Near miss bands survive at a coarse scalefactor. A slightly gentler end to decays.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong, soft knee: {\f1 --side-knee 6}. Clearly smoother side fade out, with a real bit cost on sparse material. The usual working region is +3 to +6.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated, soft knee: {\f1 --side-knee 24}. A very wide band of inaudible side content dragged into the stream at the coarsest resolution. A caricature: you are spending bits on material that was below the masking threshold by a wide margin.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild, hard knee: {\f1 --side-knee -1}. The weakest surviving side bands are dropped. Slightly leaner, slightly tighter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong, hard knee: {\f1 --side-knee -3}. An early side cutoff, with the freed bits going to the loud content. Audibly narrower on ambience.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated, hard knee: {\f1 --side-knee -6}. The end of the control: every marginal side band is gone. A caricature, and the point past which the switch stops responding, since -12 and -24 behave identically.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: orthogonal to {\f1 --side-bias} and designed to be used with it; bias sets where the threshold is, knee sets how soft its edge is. Inert under {\f1 --msmask 0}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Measured: dense stereo LC. Negative values saturate at -6 dB (-6, -12 and -24 give an identical stream); positive values keep changing the stream across the range.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --mask-slope <dB>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Shift how far below the frame average a band must sit before the encoder stops caring about it.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: both channels, mid and side, in the AAC core. No effect in HE-AAC v2.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 -24.0..50.0} dB, {\f1 0} = off (default); distinct results only inside roughly -9.9..+9\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: decimal, up to 3 places, {\f1 .} or {\f1 ,}\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 FDK carries a non masking heuristic: when a scalefactor band's energy sits far below the average energy of the frame, more than about 10 dB below in stock tuning, the required SNR for that band is relaxed. In plain words, the encoder deliberately starves very quiet bands in order to spend the bits on the loud ones. Most of the time that is correct, because a band 20 dB under the rest of the frame is usually masked. Sometimes it is not, and what gets starved is the reverb tail, the room, or the decay of the previous note.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This switch shifts that "how far below average before I stop caring" line, for both channels at once. A positive value raises it, so fewer bands qualify as negligible and quiet detail survives. Quiet passages, decays and reverb tails keep more of their structure, and the bits come from somewhere else in the frame. A negative value lowers it, so more bands are starved and the result is leaner and hollower, with more bits for the loud material.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Set expectations honestly: this is a subtle control. It only touches the quietest bands in a frame, so on dense material the decoded error barely moves, and on our sparse probe only large values shifted it at all. It is most worth trying on sparse, reverberant or deliberately quiet material, and least worth trying on a loud dense mix.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The live range is narrower than the accepted range. Distinct results appear for roughly -9.9 to +9. At -10 and below the result stops changing, +10 to +40 collapse to one stream, and 45 to 50 to another. Keep your examples inside the live span, where the number actually corresponds to behaviour.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild, protect quiet bands: {\f1 --mask-slope 2}. Slightly fewer bands starved. Expect tenths of a dB in measurement and a marginally more open decay.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong, protect quiet bands: {\f1 --mask-slope 6}. A real share of quiet bands kept. The most useful setting on reverberant material.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated, protect quiet bands: {\f1 --mask-slope 9}. The top of the live range: almost nothing is treated as negligible, and bits are being spent on content that is very probably masked. A caricature, and the last value that still responds, since +10 and above all behave the same.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild, starve quiet bands: {\f1 --mask-slope -2}. A few more quiet bands given up. Slightly leaner.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong, starve quiet bands: {\f1 --mask-slope -6}. Quiet bands clearly starved, more bits for the loud content, a hollower sound on sparse material.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated, starve quiet bands: {\f1 --mask-slope -9.5}. The bottom of the live range, quiet detail stripped wherever the heuristic can reach. A caricature of a lean setting, and the floor of the control, since -10 and below stop changing anything.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: same family as {\f1 --side-bias}, but keyed on energy versus the frame average rather than on the side threshold, and applied to mid and side alike. Use one or the other while you learn what each does, then combine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Measured: mix48 at 40 kbps gives distinct streams only for about -9.9..+9; -10 and below collapse to one stream, +10..+40 to another, 45..50 to a third. On sparse48 at 40 kbps the decoded error barely moves, with only +12 and +24 shifting it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Intensity stereo (IS)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Intensity stereo is the lossy one. Across a run of upper bands the encoder stops sending two spectra and sends a single energy envelope plus a direction, and the decoder rebuilds both channels from that one spectrum. The saving is large, the cost is stereo separation in exactly those bands, and whether you can hear the cost depends entirely on what lives up there: a correlated cymbal wash survives it well, a hard panned effect or a wide reverb does not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Stock FDK is very cautious with IS. There is not one gate but several, and a band is coded as intensity only when all of them pass at once: a bitrate per band admission rule, a correlation test between the channels, a minimum run of contiguous bands, and a stable direction. This is why moving a single threshold so often appears to do nothing, another gate is still blocking. Loosening {\f1 --is-corr-thresh} on its own, for instance, does not open IS.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Start with {\f1 --is-aggression} from group A.} It is the consumer slider for exactly this problem: a single value from 0 to 100 that moves all of the gates together and coherently. 0 is stock, which in practice means IS barely activates at normal bitrates, and 100 is the real maximum: the bitrate gate dropped, the correlation requirement loosened, the minimum run reduced to a single band and the direction tolerance widened. Start around 30 to 50 and listen, then go up if the saving is worth more to you than the width. Only once you know what the slider does should you reach for the switches below, which override what it set.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --is <n>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Turn intensity stereo on or off globally instead of leaving it to the encoder.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: stereo AAC core (LC, LD, ELD, and the stereo core under HE-AAC). No effect in HE-AAC v2.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 -1} auto (default), {\f1 0} off, {\f1 1} on\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the master switch, and it is a permission, not a command: {\f1 1} allows intensity stereo, it does not place it. The gates described above still decide which bands actually go to IS, so {\f1 --is 1} alone on material the encoder considers unsuitable, or at a bitrate above the admission rule, can leave the stream unchanged. Pair it with {\f1 --is-aggression} when you want IS to actually happen.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --is 0} is the useful end of this switch on its own. It guarantees no intensity coding at all, which is what you want when stereo separation in the top octaves matters more than the bits, or when you are isolating the effect of some other switch and do not want IS moving underneath the measurement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Values:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 -1}: stock. The encoder decides, cautiously.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 0}: intensity stereo never used. Full L/R or MS coding everywhere.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 1}: intensity stereo allowed, subject to the usual gates.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 fdkaac -p 2 -b 64000 --is 1 --is-aggression 40 in.wav -o is.m4a\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: {\f1 --is-aggression} is what makes {\f1 --is 1} visible. {\f1 --is-force-lo}/ {\f1 --is-force-hi} bypass the gates entirely and work even without this switch. Completely independent stereo is {\f1 --msmask 0 --is 0}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --isbands <n>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Cap how many scalefactor bands may be coded as intensity stereo.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: stereo AAC core. No effect in HE-AAC v2.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 -1} no limit (default), or a count within the active SFB count\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A ceiling on the total extent of intensity coding, expressed as a number of bands rather than as a position in the spectrum. It is the brake to use when IS is helping the bitrate but you want to bound the damage: let the encoder pick where intensity goes, but refuse to let it swallow half the spectrum.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Read the ceiling against {\f1 --verbose} {\f1 active SFBs}: at LC 48 kHz stereo there are 49 bands, so a cap of 40 is barely a cap at all and a cap of 4 is severe. Bands beyond the cap are coded normally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild cap: {\f1 --isbands 24}. Half the spectrum may still go intensity. On most material this changes little, because IS rarely reaches that far.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong cap: {\f1 --isbands 8}. Intensity kept to a short run. Useful with a high {\f1 --is-aggression}, where it stops the slider from running away.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated cap: {\f1 --isbands 1}. A single band of intensity. A caricature: you keep the signalling and lose essentially all of the saving.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: {\f1 --is-lo}/{\f1 --is-hi} restrict where IS may go, this one restricts how much. They combine. Inert when IS never activates, so check with {\f1 --is-aggression} first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --is-min-sfbs <n>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Minimum run of contiguous bands before intensity stereo is allowed to start.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: stereo AAC core. No effect in HE-AAC v2.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 -1} default, which is 6, or a count of bands\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One of the gates. The encoder refuses to start intensity coding on an isolated band or two; it wants a continuous region of at least this many bands that all pass the other tests. The reasoning is that a short intensity patch surrounded by normally coded bands is an audible seam, a small region where the image suddenly collapses and then reopens.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Lower the number and IS catches short fragments as well, which means it triggers far more often and on more material, with those seams as the risk. Raise it and IS becomes rarer: only wide, consistently correlated regions qualify, which is the conservative setting.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is an advanced knob. {\f1 --is-aggression} already moves it as part of its coordinated sweep, so use this one only when you are tuning by hand with aggression at 0, and remember that an explicit value overrides what the slider set.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild, more IS: {\f1 --is-min-sfbs 4}. Slightly shorter regions qualify. IS a little more frequent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong, more IS: {\f1 --is-min-sfbs 2}. Short fragments accepted. Clearly more intensity coding, and the first point where seams become plausible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated, more IS: {\f1 --is-min-sfbs 1}. Any single band may become intensity. A caricature: intensity scattered through the spectrum one band at a time.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild, less IS: {\f1 --is-min-sfbs 8}. Slightly rarer than stock.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong, less IS: {\f1 --is-min-sfbs 16}. Only broad regions qualify; on most material IS almost never fires.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated, less IS: {\f1 --is-min-sfbs 40}. A run that long essentially never exists, so this is intensity stereo switched off by the back door. A caricature of a conservative setting, and {\f1 --is 0} says the same thing honestly.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: one gate of several, so lowering it alone may change nothing if the correlation or bitrate gate still blocks. Overridden in effect by {\f1 --is-force-lo}/{\f1 -hi}, which bypass it. Prefer {\f1 --is-aggression}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --is-corr-thresh <n>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b How similar the two channels must be in a band before intensity stereo is allowed.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: stereo AAC core. No effect in HE-AAC v2.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: Q8, where 256 = 1.0 correlation; {\f1 -1} default, which is about 243, meaning roughly 0.95\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The correlation gate, and the one people reach for first. The stock requirement is severe: the two channels must be about 95 percent alike in a band before the encoder will consider replacing them with one envelope and a direction. Very little real music is that correlated in the top bands, which is most of the reason IS seems inactive by default.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The direction is counter intuitive, so read it twice: LOWER means MORE intensity. You are lowering the bar that the material has to clear, so more bands clear it. 180 is about 0.70, which is a lot more permissive than stock. Too low and you start coding genuinely different channels as one steered source, which is heard as direction errors and as the top of the image gluing together.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is one gate among several, which has a practical consequence worth stating plainly: changing this on its own does not open intensity stereo. If the bitrate admission rule or the minimum run is still blocking, the stream will not move no matter what you set here.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild, more IS: {\f1 --is-corr-thresh 230}. About 0.90. Slightly more permissive than stock.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong, more IS: {\f1 --is-corr-thresh 180}. About 0.70. Substantially more bands qualify; this is the usual hand tuning target.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated, more IS: {\f1 --is-corr-thresh 60}. About 0.23, which accepts channels that are barely related. A caricature: expect audible direction errors wherever it fires.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild, less IS: {\f1 --is-corr-thresh 250}. Tighter than stock, IS slightly rarer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong, less IS: {\f1 --is-corr-thresh 254}. Only nearly identical channels qualify.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated, less IS: {\f1 --is-corr-thresh 256}. Perfect correlation demanded, which real material essentially never reaches. A caricature: intensity stereo disabled by an impossible test, where {\f1 --is 0} would be clearer.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: alone it does not open IS, since the other gates still apply. Moved automatically by {\f1 --is-aggression}, and an explicit value here overrides the slider. Bypassed by {\f1 --is-force-lo}/{\f1 -hi}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --is-lr-ratio <n>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b How even the left/right energy balance must be in a band before intensity stereo is allowed.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: stereo AAC core. No effect in HE-AAC v2.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: Q8, where 256 = 1.0; {\f1 -1} default, which is about 179, meaning roughly 0.7\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The panning gate. Correlation says the two channels have the same shape; this says they have comparable level. A band where one side carries almost everything is a badly chosen place for intensity coding, because the single shared spectrum plus a direction has to represent a strongly lopsided pair, and the direction parameter carries the whole burden.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Lower values accept more lopsided bands and therefore produce more intensity coding, in the same counter intuitive direction as the correlation threshold. Higher values demand a more even balance and make IS rarer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the third of the three hand tuning thresholds and the one to set last. Work in the order correlation, minimum run, then ratio: the first two decide whether IS fires at all, this one mostly decides where.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild, more IS: {\f1 --is-lr-ratio 160}. Slightly more tolerant of uneven bands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong, more IS: {\f1 --is-lr-ratio 110}. Clearly lopsided bands accepted, more intensity placement across the spectrum.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated, more IS: {\f1 --is-lr-ratio 20}. Almost any imbalance accepted. A caricature: hard panned content gets reduced to one spectrum plus a pan value, which is precisely where IS sounds worst.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild, less IS: {\f1 --is-lr-ratio 200}. A bit stricter than stock.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong, less IS: {\f1 --is-lr-ratio 240}. Only well balanced bands qualify; IS becomes rare on anything with a wide mix.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated, less IS: {\f1 --is-lr-ratio 256}. Exact level equality demanded. A caricature that switches IS off through a test nothing passes.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: one gate of several; alone it rarely changes the stream. Moved by {\f1 --is-aggression}, overridden by an explicit value. Bypassed by {\f1 --is-force-lo}/{\f1 -hi}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --is-lo <sfb>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Allow intensity stereo only from this scalefactor band upward.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: stereo AAC core. No effect in HE-AAC v2.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 -1} off (default), or {\f1 0..N} within the active SFB count\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A placement restriction, not a trigger. Bands below the given number stay L/R or MS no matter what the gates say; bands at or above it remain subject to the normal gates. This switch can only take intensity coding away, never add it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The obvious use is protecting the bottom of the spectrum. Intensity stereo high up is a reasonable trade, intensity stereo in the mids or the bass is not, because that is where the ear localises by level and timing and where a collapsed image is immediately heard. There is a trap worth knowing: at low bitrate the encoder tends to place IS on relatively LOW bands, lower than intuition suggests, so if you want to see this switch do anything, scan small values rather than starting at 30.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild, push IS upward: {\f1 --is-lo 10}. Bass and low mids protected. Often enough to remove the worst placements.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong, push IS upward: {\f1 --is-lo 25}. Intensity confined to the upper half of the spectrum. A sensible default for careful work at middling bitrate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated, push IS upward: {\f1 --is-lo 46}. Intensity permitted only in the top few bands, which at the default coded bandwidth may be above the coded range entirely. A caricature, and the same trap as the MS high band example: check it against the coded bandwidth or it silently means "no IS at all".\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: pairs with {\f1 --is-hi} as a window. Restricts only, so it cannot make IS happen; use {\f1 --is-aggression} for that. Has nothing to do with {\f1 --is-force-lo}, which is the forcing switch with a similar name.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --is-hi <sfb>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Allow intensity stereo only up to this scalefactor band, inclusive.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: stereo AAC core. No effect in HE-AAC v2.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 -1} off (default), or {\f1 0..N} within the active SFB count\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The upper edge of the permitted intensity window, inclusive, and the same kind of restriction as {\f1 --is-lo}: it narrows where the encoder may place IS and never forces it. Together the pair defines a from..to region, everything outside stays conventionally coded.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Capping from above is the less common direction, but it has a clear use. If the very top of your spectrum carries the stereo information you care about most, air, cymbal placement, room, you can let intensity take the busy upper mids and keep the top bands in real stereo.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild cap: {\f1 --is-hi 44}. Only the topmost bands excluded from intensity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong cap: {\f1 --is-hi 30}. Intensity confined below the top of the spectrum; the air band keeps genuine separation.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated cap: {\f1 --is-hi 3}. A window so low that the gates essentially never fire inside it. A caricature that amounts to disabling IS, where {\f1 --is 0} is clearer and cheaper to reason about.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: {\f1 --is-lo} is the other half of the window. Combines with {\f1 --isbands}, which limits the count rather than the position. Does not interact with the forced range.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --is-force-lo <sfb>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Force intensity stereo from this band upward, bypassing every gate.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: stereo AAC core. No effect in HE-AAC v2.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 -1} off (default), or {\f1 0..N} within the active SFB count; pair with {\f1 --is-force-hi}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the laboratory switch, and it behaves differently from everything above it. The correlation test, the minimum run, the level balance rule and the bitrate admission rule are all skipped: the bands in the forced range are coded as intensity because you said so. It works with no other IS switch set, so it does not need {\f1 --is 1} and it ignores {\f1 --is-aggression}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Understand what that means for the sound. Intensity coding keeps one spectrum and a direction, so within the forced range the channels can no longer differ in anything except level and sign. On uncorrelated content the result is not subtle; it is a deliberate, audible collapse of the image, and it can sound badly wrong. That is the point of the switch: it lets you hear and measure the mechanism at full strength, and it lets you build a specific effect. The stream stays standards compliant, so every decoder will play it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Pair it with {\f1 --is-force-hi}. Set it high in the spectrum first to hear what intensity does where it is meant to be used, then walk it downward to hear why the gates exist.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild force: {\f1 --is-force-lo 40 --is-force-hi 48}. Intensity on the top bands only, which is roughly where the encoder would put it anyway. Closest to a usable setting.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong force: {\f1 --is-force-lo 20 --is-force-hi 48}. The whole upper half forced to intensity. Clear narrowing of the image above the mids, and a real bitrate saving.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated force: {\f1 --is-force-lo 0 --is-force-hi 48}. The entire spectrum coded as one steered channel. A caricature and a useful demonstration: this is what the stereo image costs you, all at once.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: bypasses {\f1 --is-corr-thresh}, {\f1 --is-min-sfbs}, {\f1 --is-lr-ratio} and the bitrate gate, so none of them limit it. Distinct from {\f1 --is-lo}, which only restricts. Needs {\f1 --is-force-hi} to define the top of the range.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Measured: {\f1 --is-force-lo} and {\f1 --is-force-hi} change the stream with no other IS switch given.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --is-force-hi <sfb>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Upper band, inclusive, of the forced intensity range.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: stereo AAC core. No effect in HE-AAC v2.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 -1} off (default), or {\f1 0..N} within the active SFB count; pair with {\f1 --is-force-lo}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The other half of the forced range, inclusive of the band you name. Both halves together define the region that is coded as intensity regardless of what the material looks like.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Keep the range inside the coded bandwidth. Forcing intensity on bands above the cutoff forces it on silence, which changes nothing and will have you chasing a switch that is working perfectly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --is-force-lo 44 --is-force-hi 46}. Three bands of forced intensity near the top. A narrow probe for A/B listening.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --is-force-lo 30 --is-force-hi 48}. A wide forced region through the upper spectrum.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --is-force-lo 5 --is-force-hi 48}. Everything above the bass forced. A caricature: the bass keeps its stereo and everything else is steered mono, which sounds exactly as odd as it reads.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: meaningless without {\f1 --is-force-lo}. Independent of the gate switches and of {\f1 --is-lo}/{\f1 --is-hi}. Check the range against {\f1 --verbose} {\f1 active SFBs} and against the coded bandwidth.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Parametric stereo (PS)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Parametric stereo is what HE-AAC v2 ({\f1 -p 29}) uses, and it is a different proposition from everything above. The waveform coder sees one channel: the two inputs are downmixed to mono, the mono signal gets an AAC core plus SBR, and the stereo image travels separately as a small set of parameters per frame. The decoder takes the mono signal, decorrelates it, and rebuilds a left and a right from those parameters.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The parameters are few and specific. IID is the level difference between the channels per parameter band, which is what places a level panned source. ICC is the coherence, how alike the two channels are, which is what decides whether a band sounds like a point source or like a wide diffuse field. Optionally IPD carries the phase difference between the channels, and OPD says where that rotation sits relative to the downmix. That is the whole stereo picture: a few dozen numbers per frame rather than a second spectrum.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is why PS works at bitrates where nothing else does. At 24 or 32 kbps a real stereo core would be starving both channels; a mono core at the same rate is comfortable, and the parameters cost almost nothing. It is also why PS has its own failure modes, which have nothing to do with quantisation noise: the image can be too coarsely sampled in frequency (too few bands), too coarsely sampled in time (too few envelopes), or momentarily absent (frames sent with no parameters at all). The switches below address exactly those three.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If you take one setting from this section, take {\f1 --ps-env 4 --ps-env-reduce 0}. Asking for four envelopes alone often changes nothing, because an automatic reduction loop collapses them again; switching that loop off is what delivers the improvement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Everything here needs {\f1 -p 29}. Under LC, HE-AAC v1, LD or ELD there is no PS layer and these switches do nothing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --ps <n>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Force the IID stereo parameter to be sent, or forbid it, instead of leaving it to the encoder's heuristic.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: HE-AAC v2 ({\f1 -p 29}) only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 -1} auto (default), {\f1 0} off, {\f1 1} on\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 IID is the level difference parameter, the backbone of the PS image. The encoder decides whether to send it from a loudness difference heuristic: if the two channels sit at nearly the same level it may conclude there is nothing to describe and send nothing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --ps 0} suppresses IID entirely, which flattens the image toward the bare mono downmix plus whatever ICC provides. It is a blunt instrument and mostly useful as a reference: encode once with it to hear what the core alone sounds like, then compare.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --ps 1} forces IID into the stream unconditionally. That is the setting that matters on material the heuristic misjudges, near equal level content above all, and it is also what carries the phase band count when you use IPD.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Values:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 -1}: stock. The loudness difference heuristic decides.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 0}: IID never sent. Flattened, near mono image.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 1}: IID always sent.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 fdkaac -p 29 -b 32000 --ps 1 in.wav -o ps.m4a\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: {\f1 --ps-ipd 1} makes the stream signal IID anyway, because that is the only route by which the phase band count reaches the decoder. {\f1 --ps-iid-quant} chooses the grid IID is quantised on. {\f1 --ps-icc} controls the other main parameter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --ps-iid-quant <n>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Choose the coarse or the fine quantisation grid for the IID level differences.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: HE-AAC v2 ({\f1 -p 29}) only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 -1} default, {\f1 0} coarse, {\f1 1} fine\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 IID is quantised onto a fixed grid of level differences. The fine grid resolves smaller level differences and therefore places sources more precisely, especially near the centre where small differences do most of the work. The coarse grid spends fewer bits per parameter and, on material whose image is wandering slightly from frame to frame, can actually sound steadier, because small analysis fluctuations quantise to the same step instead of dithering between two.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At the bitrates where PS is used, the parameter bits are a small part of the budget, so the fine grid is usually the right choice for music with real placement. Try coarse on very low rate speech or on material where you can hear the image jitter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Values:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 -1}: stock grid for the configuration.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 0}: coarse grid. Fewer parameter bits, blunter placement, steadier under jitter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 1}: fine grid. Finer placement, slightly more parameter bits.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 fdkaac -p 29 -b 32000 --ps-iid-quant 1 in.wav -o fine.m4a\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: only meaningful when IID is actually sent, so pair with {\f1 --ps 1} if the heuristic is suppressing it. Independent of {\f1 --ps-bands}, which decides how many IID values exist rather than how precisely each is coded.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --ps-icc <n>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Force the coherence parameter on or off.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: HE-AAC v2 ({\f1 -p 29}) only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 -1} auto (default), {\f1 0} off, {\f1 1} on\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 ICC tells the decoder how much of a band should come from the decorrelated copy of the mono signal rather than from the mono signal itself. High coherence means a tight point source, low coherence means a wide diffuse one. On reverberant material it is ICC, not IID, that makes the difference between a room and a dry mono signal with a pan value.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --ps-icc 0} removes that control, so bands fall back to the coherent case and the result sounds drier and narrower, even when IID is still placing sources correctly. {\f1 --ps-icc 1} forces coherence data into the stream where the encoder might have judged it unnecessary, which is the setting to try on ambient or heavily reverberant material whose width sounds collapsed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Values:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 -1}: stock. The encoder decides.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 0}: no ICC. Drier, narrower, point source feel.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 1}: ICC always sent. Diffuse content keeps its width.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 fdkaac -p 29 -b 32000 --ps-icc 1 in.wav -o icc.m4a\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: {\f1 --ps-icc-mode} changes only how the same matrix is signalled and is not a quality alternative to this switch. Complementary to {\f1 --ps}, which controls the level parameter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --ps-ipd <0|1>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Transmit the inter-channel phase difference in the PS extension.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: HE-AAC v2 ({\f1 -p 29}) only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 0} off (default, stock, phase never sent), {\f1 1} on\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number, on/off\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Without phase, PS positions sound using level differences and coherence alone. That covers anything panned by level, which is most of a modern mix, but it cannot represent a source placed by TIMING. A real inter-channel delay, the way low frequency localisation actually works in nature, is invisible to IID and ICC: both channels carry the same signal at the same level and differ only in when it arrives. Turning on IPD sends that angle, quantised onto the 8 defined steps of pi/4, in a length prefixed PS extension.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Where it helps is narrow and worth knowing exactly. IPD is coded for the lower 5 parameter bands in the 10 band configuration and the lower 11 in the 20 band configuration, which in frequency means roughly the bottom 690 Hz: those 11 bands live inside the first three QMF bands. Listen or measure above 690 Hz and you will find nothing at all. The content that benefits is low and low mid material, wide soft sources, reverb, and genuine inter-channel micro delays.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is also a ceiling, and it is a property of the representation rather than a defect. A 45 degree grid can only track a delay while the phase it produces stays unambiguous across the coded range. By the top of the IPD range a delay of about 0.7 ms has already swung through nearly the full plus or minus 180 degrees, so the grid can no longer say where it is. This is the classic phase ambiguity limit of ITD, and the same reason hearing relies on timing cues mainly down low.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One practical coupling: the phase extension does not carry its own band count. The decoder derives that from the IID signalling, so enabling {\f1 --ps-ipd} also makes the stream signal IID. That is a cheap, always legal parameter and it is the only route by which the band count reaches the decoder.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Compatibility is safe by construction. The payload sits in a length prefixed extension, so a decoder without phase synthesis reads the byte count and skips it, which the standard explicitly permits. Note which decoders use it: the Fraunhofer decoder IGNORES the IPD extension, so decoding with it shows no change at all. ffmpeg does use it. Choose your decoder deliberately before concluding the switch did nothing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Values:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 0}: stock. No phase in the stream.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 1}: IPD transmitted for the lower 5 (10 band) or 11 (20 band) parameter bands, and IID signalled along with it.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 fdkaac -p 29 -b 32000 --ps-ipd 1 --ps-opd 1 in.wav -o ipd.m4a\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: {\f1 --ps-opd} is the other half of the phase pair and is only meaningful with this on. Forces IID signalling, so it overlaps {\f1 --ps 1}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Measured, with ffmpeg as the decoder: probes built as identical signal in both channels, equal level, offset only by an inter-channel delay, comparing the decoded phase profile against the source inside the IPD range of 60 to 690 Hz.\par}
{
