{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At 0.25 and 0.4 ms the phase error more than halves, with identical figures at 32 and 48 kbps. At 0.7 ms the benefit is gone, which is the ambiguity ceiling described above.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --ps-opd <0|1>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Send the phase of the left channel relative to the mono downmix, the companion to IPD.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: HE-AAC v2 ({\f1 -p 29}) only, and only together with {\f1 --ps-ipd 1}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 0} pins OPD to zero, {\f1 1} computes it (default behaviour once IPD is on)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number, on/off\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The distinction between the two phase parameters repays a careful read. IPD is the phase of the left channel relative to the right. OPD is the phase of the left channel relative to the mono downmix. The decoder needs both, because it rotates the paths fed from the downmix by OPD and the others by OPD minus IPD. Subtract those two and OPD cancels, so the inter-channel DIFFERENCE always comes out as IPD no matter what OPD says. What OPD decides is where that rotation sits relative to the downmix.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 That cancellation is exactly why the problem is easy to miss. With OPD pinned to zero the left channel is glued to the downmix phase and the entire rotation is dumped onto the right one. Any measurement of arg(L) minus arg(R) is blind to this, because the difference is still correct. Measure each channel's absolute phase against the source instead, inside the IPD range, and the asymmetry is plain, as is OPD removing it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Deriving OPD costs essentially nothing, which is the neat part. The downmix is L+R, so sum(L * conj(L+R)) equals sum(|L|^2) plus sum(L * conj(R)), whose real part is pwrL + pwrCr and whose imaginary part is pwrCi: the same accumulators already feeding IID, ICC and IPD. So OPD is atan2(pwrCi, pwrL + pwrCr), one more atan2 over numbers already in the loop, with no downmix signal needing to be passed in from elsewhere. For the equal level delay case this reduces analytically to IPD/2, since arg(1 + e^-i.phi) = -phi/2, which is a useful sanity check; the general form is also correct when the channels differ in level, where IPD/2 would not be.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Keep {\f1 0} for A/B comparison on identical material, and note that {\f1 --ps-opd 0} on its own, without {\f1 --ps-ipd 1}, gives the stock stream, because there is no phase layer for it to modify. Note also that OPD differences only appear on material with real inter-channel delay; on level panned content there is nothing for it to correct.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Values:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 0}: OPD pinned to zero. Phase error asymmetric between the channels.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 1}: OPD computed from the existing accumulators. Symmetric phase error.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 fdkaac -p 29 -b 32000 --ps-ipd 1 --ps-opd 0 in.wav -o opd0.m4a\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: meaningless without {\f1 --ps-ipd 1}. Does not change the inter-channel difference, only the absolute phase of each channel, so pick your measurement accordingly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Measured, with ffmpeg as the decoder: absolute phase error of each channel against the source, inside the IPD range.\par}
{
