{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At OPD = 0 the two channels never agree, 14.7 against 20.1 and later 48.1 against 56.2. With OPD computed the pair converges, by +6.4 degrees on average and +13 at 0.7 ms. Note that 0.7 ms is the case where the plain IPD measurement showed no benefit: the inter-channel difference was already past what a 45 degree grid can resolve, yet the absolute phase of each channel still improved substantially.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --ps-icc-mode <n>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Select which of the two defined ICC rotation modes is signalled.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: HE-AAC v2 ({\f1 -p 29}) only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 -1} default, {\f1 0} ROT_A, {\f1 1} ROT_B\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Set expectations before you spend time here: this is signalling only. Both modes describe the same stereo matrix, the decoder simply derives it differently. Treat it as a compatibility knob rather than a quality one, and if you are hunting for audible stereo differences in PS, {\f1 --ps-bands} and {\f1 --ps-env} are where they actually live.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is worth having because decoder implementations are not all equally exercised on both paths, so if a particular player renders a PS stream oddly, switching the rotation mode is a cheap thing to test.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Values:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 -1}: stock choice for the configuration.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 0}: ROT_A signalled.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 1}: ROT_B signalled.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 fdkaac -p 29 -b 32000 --ps-icc-mode 1 in.wav -o rotb.m4a\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: only relevant when ICC is being sent, so check {\f1 --ps-icc}. Audibly far less significant than {\f1 --ps-bands}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --ps-bands <10|20>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Frequency resolution of the stereo parameters: how many PS bands describe the image.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: HE-AAC v2 ({\f1 -p 29}) only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 10} or {\f1 20}; {\f1 -1} picks from the bitrate (default), and from 22 kbps upward the table always gives 20\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is one of the two axes that decide how many stereo parameters exist at all, and it is the frequency axis. Twenty bands describe the image in finer spectral detail, so a mix whose placement varies across the spectrum, bass centred, guitars wide, hats offset, keeps that structure. Ten bands average the same picture over twice the spectrum each.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The interesting case is the low end of the bitrate range. A coarse grid is not only cheaper, it can also sound steadier, because each parameter is averaged over more spectrum and therefore jitters less from frame to frame. A wandering image is more distracting than a slightly blurred one. Stock FDK derives the count from the bitrate alone and always picks 20 at 22 kbps and above, so without this switch you cannot audition the coarse grid at all on normal material.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Values:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 10}: coarse grid. Fewer parameter bits, blurrier placement across frequency, steadier under analysis jitter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 20}: fine grid. Finer placement, more parameter bits.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 -1}: stock, picked from the bitrate.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 fdkaac -p 29 -b 24000 --ps-bands 10 in.wav -o coarse.m4a\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: {\f1 --ps-env} is the time axis of the same idea. Both are audibly more significant than {\f1 --ps-icc-mode}. {\f1 --ps-iid-quant} changes the precision of each value rather than how many values there are.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --ps-env <1|2|4>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Time resolution of the stereo parameters: how many parameter envelopes per frame.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: HE-AAC v2 ({\f1 -p 29}) only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 1}, {\f1 2} or {\f1 4}; {\f1 -1} picks from the bitrate (default), and from 36 kbps upward the table gives 4\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The other resolution axis. One envelope means the whole frame gets a single stereo snapshot, four means the image is sampled four times within the frame. This is what decides whether a moving pan or a left/right transient keeps its position or smears across the frame into an average.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is a trap, and it is the most important thing in this section: asking for four envelopes does not mean you get four. An automatic reduction loop keeps halving the count, four to two to one, whenever neighbouring envelopes look similar by a built-in error threshold, and on ordinary music that threshold is met very often. The request is undone before it reaches the stream. {\f1 --ps-env-reduce 0} is what stops that, and the measurement under that card shows the difference plainly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Values:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 1}: one stereo snapshot per frame. Cheapest, smears any movement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 2}: two envelopes. A reasonable middle setting with {\f1 --ps-env-reduce 0}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 4}: four envelopes. Tracks moving panorama and transients closely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 -1}: stock, picked from the bitrate.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 fdkaac -p 29 -b 32000 --ps-env 4 --ps-env-reduce 0 in.wav -o env4.m4a\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: effectively requires {\f1 --ps-env-reduce 0} to be honoured on ordinary material. {\f1 --ps-bands} is the frequency axis of the same tradeoff.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Measured: at HE-AAC v2 32 kbps {\f1 --ps-env 4} does change the stream, because the bitrate table picks 2 there; at 48 kbps the table already gives 4, so the request matches stock and only {\f1 --ps-env-reduce 0} makes a difference.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --ps-env-reduce <0|1>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Switch off the automatic envelope halving loop so the requested envelope count is the one transmitted.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: HE-AAC v2 ({\f1 -p 29}) only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 0} disables the reduction loop, {\f1 1} leaves it on; {\f1 -1} off, meaning stock, which has the loop on (default)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number, on/off\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The encoder keeps a reduction loop that compares neighbouring parameter envelopes and collapses four into two, and two into one, whenever they look similar enough by a built-in error threshold. The intent is to save parameter bits on static material. The side effect is that the envelope count you asked for is frequently not what gets transmitted, and because the saving is small while the cost is temporal resolution of the image, the trade is often a bad one on material with any movement at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --ps-env-reduce 0} turns the loop off and makes {\f1 --ps-env} literal. This is the single highest value setting in the PS group. The gain is in temporal accuracy of the stereo picture, at essentially the same file size, because parameter bits are a small fraction of the budget at these rates.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Values:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 -1} or {\f1 1}: stock. The loop runs and may collapse the envelope count.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 0}: loop disabled, {\f1 --ps-env} taken literally.\sa180\par}
