{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Requesting 4 envelopes alone changes nothing at 48 kbps, the output is byte identical to stock, because the reduction loop undoes the request immediately. The gain appears only once the loop is stopped: a third less panorama error for roughly the same file size.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --ps-noenv-skip <0|1>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Forbid parameter-less PS frames, so every frame carries stereo information.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: HE-AAC v2 ({\f1 -p 29}) only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 0} never emit parameter-less frames, {\f1 1} allow them; {\f1 -1} off, meaning stock, which allows them (default)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number, on/off\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Beyond thinning out envelopes, the encoder can skip stereo parameters entirely for a stretch of frames. When successive IID and ICC sets look similar it may send up to ten consecutive frames carrying no stereo information at all, leaving the decoder to coast on the last values it received.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 On static material that is free. On material where the image drifts slowly it is audible as the stereo picture flattening and then snapping back when parameters resume, because the decoder held a stale snapshot through the drift and then jumped to the current one. Once you know the artefact you tend to hear it, and it is more distracting than the bits it saves are worth.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --ps-noenv-skip 0} forbids the skipping and forces parameters into every frame. Use it whenever the image seems to breathe or stutter. The cost is a modest number of parameter bits per frame.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Values:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 -1} or {\f1 1}: stock. Up to ten consecutive frames may carry no stereo parameters.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 0}: parameters in every frame.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 fdkaac -p 29 -b 32000 --ps-noenv-skip 0 --ps-env 4 --ps-env-reduce 0 in.wav -o steady.m4a\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: the same family of hidden thinning as {\f1 --ps-env-reduce}, and the two are usually set together when the goal is a stable image. Neither changes the core audio, only the stereo parameter stream.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 C. Bandwidth and SBR\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two different things decide how high your file sounds, and they are easy to confuse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The first is the {\b core bandwidth}: the highest frequency the AAC core itself actually codes, with real spectral lines and real bits. Above that line the core sends nothing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The second is the {\b SBR crossover}, also called the SBR start frequency. SBR (spectral band replication) does not code the high band. It copies lower, already coded spectrum upward and then describes, with a handful of parameters per frame, how that copy should be shaped. The crossover is where that copy begins. In a well formed HE-AAC stream the core bandwidth and the crossover sit at the same place: the core stops, SBR takes over, and the listener hears one continuous spectrum.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What SBR sends per frame is a short list:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b envelopes}: the level of each SBR band over time. More envelopes per frame means finer time resolution up top and more bits.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b noise floor}: how much synthetic noise to mix into each band. This is what turns a copied tonal region back into something that sounds like air, cymbals or breath.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b inverse filtering level} (invf): per band, how hard to whiten the copy. The copied material carries the tonal structure of the band it came from. If that structure does not belong up there, you hear it as a metallic, ringing, chorused top. Whitening flattens that structure out.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b missing harmonics}: flags that tell the decoder to insert a synthetic sine in a band where a real partial of the original got lost or landed in the wrong place.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The last two are decided by {\b estimators} inside the encoder, which compare the tonality of the original high band with the tonality of the patched copy, band by band and frame by frame. The estimators are where the character of the top end is set, and they trade off against each other in one direction: too little whitening and too little fill noise gives a metallic, ringing, phasey top; too much of both gives a hissy, washed out top where cymbals turn into shaped noise and sustained partials disappear. Most of the knobs in this group move you along exactly that axis. There is no universally correct point on it; it depends on the material and on the bitrate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 SBR is active in these profiles:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 -p 5} HE-AAC\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 -p 29} HE-AAC v2 (SBR plus parametric stereo)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 -p 39 -L 1} AAC-ELD with SBR\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In {\f1 -p 2} (AAC-LC), {\f1 -p 23} (AAC-LD) and {\f1 -p 39} without {\f1 -L 1} there is no SBR, and every {\f1 --sbr-*} switch in this group does nothing. {\f1 --core-cutoff} works everywhere.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two conventions used throughout this group:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b x100 knobs} scale an internal constant: {\f1 100} means 1.00 and is exactly stock, below 100 makes the constant smaller, above 100 makes it larger. Both directions work. A few knobs are documented as {\b raw x100}, which means the value replaces the constant outright rather than scaling it ({\f1 --sbr-tran-peak 90} {\i is} the stock 0.90).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 --verbose} prints the effective core bandwidth, crossover, final top edge and SBR header period. Under SBR it is the only reliable way to see what you actually got, and several switches in this group are validated by FDK only at initialisation time, so checking is not optional.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Core bandwidth: {\f1 --core-cutoff}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --core-cutoff <hz>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Forces the upper limit of the band the AAC core codes, in Hz, and it keeps working under SBR where {\f1 -w} does not.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: every profile. This is the one switch in group C that is useful without SBR.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: Hz, from well above 0 up to the core Nyquist (half the core sample rate); default {\f1 0} = leave FDK's own decision\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The standard {\f1 -w/--bandwidth} is overridden when SBR initialises: the SBR tuning table decides the core bandwidth, so that the core stops exactly where the crossover begins, and whatever you asked for with {\f1 -w} is discarded. {\f1 --core-cutoff} is applied after that and survives it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two situations make this worth reaching for. The first is SBR material where you want more real coded spectrum under the crossover than the table gives you, typically because the patched band sounds synthetic and you would rather spend bits on genuine mid treble. The second has nothing to do with SBR: AAC-LD and AAC-ELD pick their core bandwidth from a table that is very conservative, about 2 kHz at 48 kbps and about 5.8 kHz at 64 kbps. Those profiles sound muffled for that reason alone, and {\f1 --core-cutoff} is the fix.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The encoder cannot cut at an arbitrary Hz value. The spectrum is divided into scalefactor bands, so the cutoff snaps to the nearest SFB boundary, and {\f1 --verbose} reports the real, SFB anchored result. Asking for 17300 Hz and being told 17915 Hz is not an error; 17915 Hz is what is in the stream.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Mind the ceiling. The maximum is half the {\i core} sample rate, and with dual rate SBR the core runs at half the input rate: for 48 kHz input under HE-AAC the core runs at 24 kHz, so the hard ceiling is 12 kHz. There is also a separate 20 kHz cap that applies without SBR; {\f1 --uncap-bandwidth} (group A) lifts it so that {\f1 --core-cutoff} can reach up to half the sample rate on high rate material, and that flag does nothing on its own.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 You police the result yourself. Pushing the core cutoff far {\i below} the crossover leaves everything between the core top and the SBR start with nothing in it, which is audible as a gap in the mid treble rather than as a gentle rolloff. If you want a genuinely low core, move the crossover down with {\f1 --sbr-start} so that the two meet again.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, narrowing the core (HE-AAC {\f1 -p 5}, 64 kbps stereo, 48 kHz input, where the table gives about 10500 Hz of core):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --core-cutoff 9000}. A slightly cheaper core, a slightly wider SBR region. Bits freed up in the core go to the lower spectrum.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --core-cutoff 7500}. Clearly more of the spectrum is now replicated rather than coded. Expect a thinner, more synthetic 8..10 kHz region, with a cleaner low mid.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --core-cutoff 2000}. The core ends at the top of the vocal fundamentals. Everything from 2 kHz to the crossover is a hole. A caricature unless you also pull {\f1 --sbr-start} right down.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, widening the core (same conditions):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --core-cutoff 11500}. A little real spectrum above the usual crossover.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --core-cutoff 12000} at 48 kHz input under HE-AAC. The core now runs to its Nyquist. Real coded treble, but the core pays full price for a band SBR was already covering for free, so at 64 kbps the rest of the spectrum gets coarser.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --core-cutoff 20000} on AAC-LC at 320 kbps, with {\f1 -p 2} and no SBR at all. Legal and sometimes genuinely wanted, but at low bitrate the same request would drain the bit budget into inaudible treble.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: under SBR, {\f1 -w} is ignored, and {\f1 --verbose} still labels the automatic value "[from -w]" even though {\f1 --core-cutoff} is what set it. Checking the reported number rather than the label is the habit to build. Pair with {\f1 --sbr-start} when you move the cutoff far, so the core and the crossover still meet. {\f1 --uncap-bandwidth} is needed only to go past 20 kHz without SBR.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Measured: {\f1 --core-cutoff 7500} under HE-AAC v2 at 48 kbps gives an effective 7500 Hz core, where stock {\f1 -w 7500} leaves 8613 Hz because it is ignored.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 SBR frequency layout: {\f1 --sbr-start} {\f1 --sbr-stop} {\f1 --sbr-freqscale} {\f1 --sbr-alterscale} {\f1 --sbr-noise-bands} {\f1 --sbr-amp-res} {\f1 --sbr-data-extra}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These seven switches define the shape of the SBR band before anything is measured in it: where the replicated region starts, where it ends, how it is divided into bands, how finely the noise floor is described, and how precisely envelope levels are quantised.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The two important ones are {\f1 --sbr-start} and {\f1 --sbr-stop}. Both take a table index, not a frequency, and the frequency an index maps to depends on the sample rate, the profile and the bitrate. Measured values are given in the cards below; read them as a guide to the direction and the spacing, and confirm the actual numbers for your settings with {\f1 --verbose}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The rule that catches everyone: {\b legality depends on the pair, the bitrate and the sample rate.} SBR requires a valid number of master bands between start and stop, and FDK validates the combination when the encoder initialises. A bad pair does not degrade gracefully; it reports "encoder initialization failed" and nothing is written. The legal combinations form a diagonal band: as you raise the start you must usually raise the stop with it. At HE-AAC 64 kbps stereo, 48 kHz input, every start from 0 to 4 is illegal with every stop, and the documented looking pair {\f1 --sbr-start 5 --sbr-stop 9} fails there. Pairs that do work at that setting: {\f1 8/9}, {\f1 8/14}, {\f1 14/12}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So: change one index at a time, run the encode, and read {\f1 --verbose}. An encode that fails to initialise costs you a second; a stream with a crossover you did not intend costs you the listening session.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --sbr-start <n>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Sets where the core stops and the replicated high band begins.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: SBR profiles only ({\f1 -p 5}, {\f1 -p 29}, {\f1 -p 39 -L 1})\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: table index {\f1 0..15}; default {\f1 -1} = the value from the SBR tuning table\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the most consequential single number in an HE-AAC stream. Everything below the crossover is real coded spectrum; everything above it is a described copy. Lowering the start gives the core less to do, so what it does code it codes more cleanly, and a larger share of the spectrum becomes parametric. Raising the start does the opposite: more real treble, a smaller SBR region, and a core that has to pay for it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At very low bitrate, lowering the crossover is often the only way to make the core sound clean at all. At 64 kbps and above, raising it tends to win, because the core can afford genuine 10..12 kHz detail and a copied band always carries some of the character of the band it was copied from.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Measured core bandwidth per start index, as printed by {\f1 --verbose} at HE-AAC {\f1 -p 5}, 64 kbps, 48 kHz stereo input, with the default stop. "Illegal" means encoder initialisation failed for every stop tried:\par}
