{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same sweep at 44.1 kHz input, HE-AAC 32 kbps: start 5 gives 5038 Hz, 8 gives 5943 Hz, 11 gives 7579 Hz, 14 gives 9646 Hz, 15 gives 11025 Hz. Note that index 8 and 9 land on the same core bandwidth at 48 kHz: the mapping is a table, not a formula, and neighbouring indices can coincide.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, lowering the crossover (HE-AAC 64 kbps stereo, 48 kHz, stock 14):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --sbr-start 13}. Core to 9000 Hz. Marginally cleaner core, slightly wider patch.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --sbr-start 11}. Core to 7640 Hz. Noticeably more of the top is parametric. Worth trying when the core sounds grainy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --sbr-start 6 --sbr-stop 9}. Core to 5484 Hz. Almost everything above the presence region is a copy; cymbals and sibilants are entirely SBR's invention. This is the right shape at 24 kbps and a caricature at 64.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, raising the crossover:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --sbr-start 15}. Core to 12000 Hz, the top of the index range at this rate. Real coded spectrum up to 12 kHz, a narrower SBR region above it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: raising the crossover further is not available through this switch at this setting, because 15 is the highest index. The next step is to drop SBR and code the whole band with {\f1 -p 2} plus {\f1 -w}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: the same, taken to its conclusion: {\f1 -p 2 -b 64000 -w 16000}. No SBR, no copy, and at 64 kbps stereo a core that is clearly short of bits for a 16 kHz band. Instructive to compare against HE-AAC on the same material.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: must form a legal pair with {\f1 --sbr-stop}. {\f1 --core-cutoff} can move the core bandwidth independently of the crossover, which is how you create a gap if you are not careful. Lowering the start widens the band that {\f1 --sbr-noise-bands}, {\f1 --sbr-invf*} and the missing harmonics detector are responsible for, so their settings matter more the lower you go.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --sbr-stop <n>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Sets the top edge of the replicated band, which is the top edge of the file.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: SBR profiles only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: table index {\f1 0..13}; default {\f1 -1} = the value from the SBR tuning table\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Where {\f1 --sbr-start} splits the spectrum, this sets the ceiling. Raising it extends the patched region higher, which costs almost nothing in bits (SBR describes bands, not lines) but spreads the same parameter budget over a wider region, so each band is described more coarsely. Lowering it closes the file off earlier, which sounds like a conventional lowpass and sometimes reads as cleaner, because a mediocre 18..22 kHz patch contributes more hiss than content.\par}
