{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, lowering the top edge (from stock 12):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --sbr-start 14 --sbr-stop 11}. Top at about 20250 Hz. Hard to hear, slightly denser description of what remains.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --sbr-start 14 --sbr-stop 9}. Top at about 16875 Hz. A deliberate, audible ceiling, with the remaining bands described more finely. Often the better trade at 32 kbps and below.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --sbr-start 14 --sbr-stop 4}. Top at about 10875 Hz with a 10500 Hz core: SBR now covers a few hundred Hz and contributes nothing. You have effectively built an AAC-LC stream with SBR overhead attached.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, raising the top edge:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: none upward at this setting. Stop 12 (about 22125 Hz) is already the stock top and stop 13 is not legal with start 14. The top can be held while the crossover moves up: {\f1 --sbr-start 15 --sbr-stop 12} keeps about 22125 Hz with a 12000 Hz core.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --sbr-start 8 --sbr-stop 14}. A wide patched region from 6468 Hz up. Cheap in bits, coarse in description: watch for a "shimmering" quality on dense material.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --sbr-stop 13} at this setting, which fails to initialise. The index exists in the standard but not in every legal pair, and this is the fastest way to see what the diagonal legality band means in practice.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: the pair constraint with {\f1 --sbr-start} governs both. Index 13 is accepted by the parser but is not legal in every pair, which is why {\f1 --verbose} and a test encode come before any listening.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --sbr-freqscale <n>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Chooses how the replicated band is divided into SBR bands: linearly or on one of three logarithmic grids.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: SBR profiles only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 0} = linear, {\f1 1}, {\f1 2}, {\f1 3} = progressively finer logarithmic grouping; default {\f1 -1} = the value from the SBR tuning table\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 SBR describes its region band by band, and this picks the ruler. A linear grid gives every band the same width in Hz, so the top octave gets many bands and the bottom of the SBR region gets few. A logarithmic grid spaces bands by ratio, which matches hearing: more resolution where the ear resolves more, down near the crossover.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Values:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 0} linear. Even spacing in Hz. Fine resolution high up, coarse just above the crossover, which is the opposite of what the ear wants. Occasionally useful on material whose high band is genuinely uniform, such as broadband noise beds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 1} coarsest logarithmic grouping. Fewest bands, cheapest description, least control.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 2} middle logarithmic grouping.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 3} finest logarithmic grouping. Most bands, most parameters, best shaping of an uneven high band.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 -1} off: the tuning table decides, which is the right choice unless you have a reason.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Example in context: {\f1 fdkaac -p 5 -b 64000 --sbr-freqscale 3 -o out.m4a in.wav}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: the number of bands this produces interacts with {\f1 --sbr-noise-bands} (noise bands are derived per octave, not per SBR band) and with how much the invf decision can vary across the region. A finer grid means a finer invf map.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --sbr-alterscale <n>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Switches to the alternative band grouping resolution, a secondary adjustment to the grid chosen by {\f1 --sbr-freqscale}.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: SBR profiles only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 0} = off, {\f1 1} = on; default {\f1 -1} = the value from the SBR tuning table\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a standard SBR header flag that slightly changes how the master band table is derived, which in practice moves a few band edges. It is a refinement, not a character control: there is no listening result here worth promising in either position, and nothing in our measurements isolates it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Values:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 0} the alternative resolution off.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 1} the alternative resolution on.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 -1} off: follow the tuning table. Recommended.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Example in context: {\f1 fdkaac -p 5 -b 64000 --sbr-freqscale 2 --sbr-alterscale 1 -o out.m4a in.wav}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: only meaningful together with {\f1 --sbr-freqscale}, since it adjusts that grid. If you are hunting for a tonal change up top, {\f1 --sbr-invf*} and {\f1 --sbr-noise-bands} are the switches with real leverage; reach for this one last.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --sbr-noise-bands <n>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Sets how finely the SBR noise floor is described across frequency.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: SBR profiles only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 0}, {\f1 1}, {\f1 2}, {\f1 3}; default {\f1 -1} = the value from the SBR tuning table\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The noise floor is how SBR puts "air" back into a copied band. With one noise band for the whole region, the encoder sends a single noise level and the decoder spreads it evenly: cheap, and wrong whenever the high band is not uniform, for example a sustained string note under a hi-hat. With more noise bands, the level can follow the material, so a quiet tonal region does not get the same hiss as a busy one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The field in the SBR header is two bits wide, so there are exactly four settings and no more. The values are {\i not} a literal band count: they are a density, roughly bands per octave.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Values:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 0} one noise band for the entire SBR region. Cheapest, bluntest. At very low bitrate the saving is real; on anything with structure up top you hear noise in the wrong places.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 1} about one noise band per octave.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 2} about two per octave.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 3} about three per octave. Densest description, best separation of tonal and noisy regions, most side information.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 -1} off: the tuning table decides by bitrate.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Example in context: {\f1 fdkaac -p 5 -b 96000 --sbr-noise-bands 3 -o out.m4a in.wav}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: this decides the {\i resolution} of the noise floor; {\f1 --sbr-noise-max}, {\f1 --sbr-nf-weight} and {\f1 --sbr-noise-floor-offset} decide its {\i level}. Raising the density is wasted if the level is clipped at a low ceiling, so if you are chasing air, look at {\f1 --sbr-noise-max} first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --sbr-amp-res <n>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Chooses the quantisation step of the SBR envelope levels: 1.5 dB or 3.0 dB.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: SBR profiles only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 0} = 1.5 dB, {\f1 1} = 3.0 dB; default {\f1 -1} = the value from the SBR tuning table\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Envelope levels are sent as quantised numbers, and this is the step size. A 1.5 dB step tracks level changes in the high band more exactly and costs more bits per envelope. A 3.0 dB step is cheaper and coarser; on material with strong level movement up top, the coarse step is audible as the high band stepping rather than gliding.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Values:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 0} 1.5 dB steps. Finer, more expensive. Prefer at higher bitrate or on dynamic material.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 1} 3.0 dB steps. Coarser, cheaper. The sensible trade when bits are scarce, which is why the table often picks it low down.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 -1} off: the tuning table decides.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Example in context: {\f1 fdkaac -p 5 -b 80000 --sbr-amp-res 0 -o out.m4a in.wav}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: interacts directly with {\f1 --sbr-num-env} and the transient detector, because every extra envelope costs a full set of levels at whatever step size is in force. Four envelopes at 1.5 dB resolution is the expensive corner of this group.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --sbr-data-extra <n>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Writes the optional extra SBR header fields instead of omitting them.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: SBR profiles only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 0} = do not write, {\f1 1} = write; default {\f1 -1} = the value from the SBR tuning table\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The SBR header has an optional block carrying additional configuration. Writing it makes every header slightly larger and the configuration more explicit; omitting it leaves the decoder on default assumptions for those fields. This is a bitstream formatting choice, not a sound control, and it is only worth touching when you are chasing compatibility with a specific decoder or inspecting headers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Values:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 0} do not write the extra header data.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 1} write it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 -1} off: follow the tuning table.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Example in context: {\f1 fdkaac -p 5 -b 64000 --sbr-data-extra 1 -o out.m4a in.wav}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: combined with a short {\f1 --sbr-header-period} the extra bytes repeat often, so if you are optimising a low bitrate stream for fast SBR lock, keep this off.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 SBR time resolution and stereo: {\f1 --sbr-num-env} {\f1 --sbr-freqres-fixfix} {\f1 --sbr-stereo-mode} {\f1 --sbr-header-period} {\f1 --sbr-header-time}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Frequency layout decides where the SBR band is. This section decides how often it is described, how it is split between channels, and how often the configuration itself is repeated in the stream.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Time resolution is a direct bits versus smearing trade. One envelope per frame means one level per band for the whole frame, so a hi-hat inside that frame is averaged with the silence around it. Four envelopes track the shape but quadruple the envelope data. The transient detector normally makes this decision per frame, which is why forcing a fixed count is a bigger intervention than it looks.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The header period is a different kind of setting: it does not change how the file sounds once playback is running, it changes how long a decoder that joins mid stream waits before it has any high band at all. It matters for live streaming and for nothing else.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --sbr-num-env <1|2|4>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Forces a fixed number of SBR envelopes per frame and switches off the transient detector's framing decision.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: SBR profiles only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 1}, {\f1 2}, {\f1 4}; default {\f1 -1} = off, the transient detector decides per frame\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Normally SBR uses a variable time grid: frames with a detected attack get more, shorter envelopes so the attack is not smeared, and steady frames get one. This switch replaces that with a static grid, every frame the same, regardless of content.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 That makes it a good tool for stable material and a bad one for percussive material. On a sustained pad, organ or string bed, forcing 2 or 4 envelopes buys you time resolution the detector would not have asked for, and the cost is predictable. On drums, the static grid means attacks no longer get their own envelope, and you get exactly the backwards smearing the detector exists to prevent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The value 8 would exceed the standard's grid and is rejected.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Values:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 1} one envelope per frame. Cheapest, bluntest; the high band holds one level per band for the whole frame. Expect audible smearing on anything transient.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 2} two envelopes per frame. A middle ground, and a reasonable forced setting on steady material at low bitrate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 4} four envelopes per frame. Finest static resolution, most envelope data. Good on sustained, ringing sounds; on sharp attacks it still loses to the dynamic grid, because the splits are in fixed places rather than at the attack.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 -1} off. The transient detector picks per frame. This is the better default, and the knobs to shape it are in the transient detector section below.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Example in context: {\f1 fdkaac -p 5 -b 64000 --sbr-num-env 4 -o out.m4a organ.wav}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: this overrides the framing part of what {\f1 --sbr-tran-thr} and {\f1 --sbr-tran-split} influence, so combining them is contradictory: if you want better behaviour on attacks, leave {\f1 --sbr-num-env} off and lower {\f1 --sbr-tran-thr} instead. The bit cost per envelope is set by {\f1 --sbr-amp-res}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --sbr-freqres-fixfix <0|1>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Sets the frequency resolution of the FIXFIX envelope, the envelope type used in frames with no detected transient.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: SBR profiles only\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 0} = low, {\f1 1} = high; default {\f1 -1} = off\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An envelope can describe the high band with few bands or with many. FIXFIX is the envelope shape used for ordinary, transient free frames, which on most material is the large majority of them, so this quietly affects most of the file.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Low resolution groups bands together: fewer levels per envelope, cheaper, and less able to follow an uneven high band. High resolution sends more levels, tracks spectral shape better, and costs bits that at low bitrate come out of the core.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Values:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 0} low frequency resolution. Cheaper; the high band is described more coarsely across frequency. Sensible at low bitrate and on uniform high bands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 1} high frequency resolution. More levels per envelope, better shaping of structured high bands. Sensible when bits are available.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 -1} off: the encoder's own choice, which follows the tuning table and the frame type.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Example in context: {\f1 fdkaac -p 5 -b 96000 --sbr-freqres-fixfix 1 -o out.m4a in.wav}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: this is the frequency axis; {\f1 --sbr-num-env} is the time axis. Both multiply into the same envelope data budget, and {\f1 --sbr-amp-res} sets the price per level. It applies only to transient free frames, so on heavily percussive material its share of the file is smaller.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --sbr-stereo-mode <0..3>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Chooses how the SBR high band is shared between the two channels, independently of the core's stereo coding.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: SBR profiles only, stereo input\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 0} mono, {\f1 1} LR, {\f1 2} coupling, {\f1 3} switch-LRC; default {\f1 -1} = off\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The core and SBR make separate stereo decisions. The core may be coding mid/side while SBR is coupling, or the reverse. This switch addresses only the high band.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Full left/right separation sends an independent set of SBR parameters per channel: the widest, most faithful top end, at roughly double the SBR side information. Coupling sends one shared envelope plus level information, which is much cheaper and collapses the stereo image up top towards the centre. On most material that collapse is less audible than you would expect, which is why it is the normal choice at low bitrate; on wide, hard panned cymbals and on recordings with real room difference between channels, it is clearly audible.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Values:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 0} mono. One high band for both channels. Only sensible on genuinely mono content, or as a deliberate caricature to hear what the other modes buy you.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 1} LR, full separation. Independent high band per channel. The audiophile setting at higher bitrate; the expensive one at 32 kbps.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 2} coupling. Shared envelope plus level and balance. Cheap, slightly narrower top.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 3} switch-LRC. The encoder decides per frame, which is the sensible general case and what the default behaviour amounts to.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 -1} off: the encoder decides, as in {\f1 3}.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Example in context: {\f1 fdkaac -p 5 -b 96000 --sbr-stereo-mode 1 -o out.m4a in.wav}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: in {\f1 -p 29} (HE-AAC v2) parametric stereo is also describing the image, so forcing LR here fights the premise of that profile; at HE-AAC v2 bitrates the bits are better spent elsewhere. Core stereo switches in group B are a separate decision and do not override this one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --sbr-header-period <n>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Sets how many frames apart SBR headers are written, which decides how long a decoder joining a live stream hears core only audio before the high band appears.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: SBR profiles only. In HE-AAC and HE-AAC v2 it controls the in-band {\f1 sbr_header}; in AAC-ELD with SBR there is no in-band SBR header, so the period applies to the LATM/LOAS StreamMuxConfig and therefore needs {\f1 -f 6}, {\f1 -f 7} or {\f1 -f 10}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: frames, {\f1 1} and up; default {\f1 -1} = off, FDK's own period of about 10 frames (about 0.46 s at 44.1 kHz)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The entire SBR configuration lives in a periodic header, not in every frame. A listener who tunes into an Icecast, Shoutcast or HLS stream mid stream decodes the core immediately and hears a muffled, lowpassed version of the programme until the next SBR header arrives. Then the top end appears. On a file this is invisible, since playback starts at the beginning; on a live stream it is the first thing a new listener hears.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Writing the header in every frame removes that moment almost entirely, at the cost of the header bytes in every frame. At low bitrate those bytes are not nothing. Stretching the period saves them and lengthens the core only moment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The value is taken literally, with no one second ceiling applied.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, shortening the period:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --sbr-header-period 5}. Roughly halves the wait against the default.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --sbr-header-period 2}. A header every other frame; the core only moment is down to tens of milliseconds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --sbr-header-period 1}. A header in every single frame, near instant SBR lock, maximum overhead. On a 24 kbps stream that overhead is a real share of the budget, which is why it is a caricature there and a reasonable choice at 96 kbps.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, lengthening the period:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --sbr-header-period 25}. About a second between headers at 44.1 kHz. Barely noticeable on a stream people stay tuned to.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --sbr-header-period 100}. Several seconds of core only audio for a new listener. Saves header bytes, sounds broken to anyone channel hopping.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --sbr-header-period 5000}. Minutes between headers. For a file this is harmless; for a stream it means most listeners never hear the high band at all.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: {\f1 --sbr-header-time} expresses the same thing in seconds and wins when both are given. {\f1 --sbr-data-extra 1} makes each header bigger, so the two together multiply the overhead. {\f1 --verbose} prints the effective period in frames and milliseconds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --sbr-header-time <s>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b The same setting as {\f1 --sbr-header-period}, expressed in seconds instead of frames.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: SBR profiles only; in AAC-ELD with SBR it sets the LATM/LOAS StreamMuxConfig period, so use {\f1 -f 6}, {\f1 -f 7} or {\f1 -f 10}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 0.01..600} seconds; default {\f1 -1} = off\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: decimal, up to 3 places, {\f1 .} or {\f1 ,}\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Frames are an awkward unit when a stream operator specifies a requirement, because the frame duration depends on the sample rate and the profile. This switch takes the seconds directly and converts them for you, so the same command line behaves the same way at 44.1 and 48 kHz.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It wins over {\f1 --sbr-header-period} when both are given.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 With AAC-ELD in ADTS or M4A there is nothing to repeat: the configuration sits only in the container, and {\f1 --verbose} reports the SBR header period as the LATM/LOAS config period rather than as an in-band SBR header.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, shortening the time:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --sbr-header-time 0.25}. A quarter second worst case wait.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --sbr-header-time 0.05}. Effectively immediate lock for a listener joining at random.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --sbr-header-time 0.01}, the minimum. At most sample rates this is shorter than a frame, so it amounts to a header in every frame with the maximum overhead that implies.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, lengthening the time:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --sbr-header-time 1}. One second, close to a common operator requirement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --sbr-header-time 3.5}. Three and a half seconds of core only audio for a new listener. Measured on the decoder side with this setting, the longest observed wait for the high band after joining mid stream was 3.41 s for HE-AAC, 3.46 s for HE-AAC v2 and 3.52 s for AAC-ELD.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --sbr-header-time 600}, the maximum, ten minutes. Legal, and for a live stream indistinguishable from shipping a core only stream.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: overrides {\f1 --sbr-header-period}. Check the result in {\f1 --verbose}, which prints the effective period both ways. Both {\f1 .} and {\f1 ,} are accepted as the decimal separator.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 SBR transient detector: {\f1 --sbr-tran-peak} {\f1 --sbr-tran-quiet} {\f1 --sbr-tran-dom} {\f1 --sbr-tran-thr} {\f1 --sbr-tran-split}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 SBR divides each frame into time slots and decides, from the energy in those slots, whether something in the high band attacked. If it says yes, the attack gets its own short envelope and the level jump is coded where it happens. If it says no, one envelope covers the whole stretch and the energy of the hit is averaged across it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Averaging across an attack is audible, and it is audible {\i before} the attack, not after: you hear a short burst of high frequency hiss just ahead of the hi-hat or the clap. That is pre-echo, and in the replicated band it is one of the more recognisable coding artefacts, because the material up there is already synthetic enough that a smear reads as noise rather than as a soft attack.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Five knobs reach into this detector. One of them, {\f1 --sbr-tran-thr}, is far more effective than the rest and is the one to try first. Two of them, {\f1 --sbr-tran-quiet} and {\f1 --sbr-tran-dom}, live in a second, faster detector that only runs for AAC-ELD with SBR, so they do nothing in HE-AAC and HE-AAC v2.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 All five are x100 knobs and all of them work in both directions: {\f1 100} means the stock constant exactly, below 100 makes the constant smaller, above 100 makes it larger. {\f1 --sbr-tran-peak} is the exception in meaning, not in format: it replaces the constant rather than scaling it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --sbr-tran-thr <n>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Scales the master threshold that decides how readily the SBR transient detector fires at all, and it is the one knob in this section with a large, measured effect.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: SBR profiles ({\f1 -p 5}, {\f1 -p 29}, {\f1 -p 39 -L 1})\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 1..10000}, x100 where {\f1 100} = stock x1.00; default {\f1 -1} = off\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The detector compares slot energies against a threshold derived from the signal. Lower the threshold and marginal attacks that previously went unnoticed now get their own envelope; raise it and only the most obvious hits do. Because missing an attack is what produces pre-echo, lowering this is the direct cure, and the cost is envelope data: more detected transients means more envelopes means more bits, taken from the core.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the knob that justifies the group. On percussive material it removes high band pre-echo almost entirely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Measured, pre-echo in the SBR band. Test signal: 24 percussive attacks of hi-hat and clap character plus four decaying tones between 8 and 13 kHz. Metric: energy above 9 kHz appearing in the two 5.8 ms windows before each attack that is absent from the source, so lower is better. Encoded as HE-AAC, decoded, lag aligned by cross correlation first:\par}
