{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The effect is monotonic, reproducible at both bitrates and saturated below 60. Values of 150 and 400 do change the stream, in the other direction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, lowering the threshold (detector fires more readily):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --sbr-tran-thr 80}. Pre-echo roughly cut in half on the measured signal, modest extra envelope cost.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --sbr-tran-thr 40}. Pre-echo essentially gone on percussive material. This is the recommended starting point when you hear smearing up top.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --sbr-tran-thr 1}. The detector fires on almost everything. Below 60 there is nothing further to gain in pre-echo, so all you buy is envelope data spent on non events, which at low bitrate takes bits off the core and makes the whole file worse.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, raising the threshold (detector fires less readily):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --sbr-tran-thr 150}. Marginal attacks are ignored, slightly fewer envelopes, slightly more smearing. Occasionally worth it on steady material at a very tight budget.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --sbr-tran-thr 400}. Only unmistakable attacks get their own envelope. Expect clearly more pre-echo on drums.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --sbr-tran-thr 10000}. The detector effectively never fires; every frame is described with the static grid. A caricature, and a useful one if you want to hear the artefact this detector exists to prevent.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: {\f1 --sbr-num-env} forces a static grid and so removes the framing benefit this knob buys, which makes combining them self defeating. {\f1 --sbr-tran-split} handles the separate question of splitting frames that have no detected transient at all. For pre-echo in the {\i core} (LC, LD, ELD), the tools are in group E.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --sbr-tran-split <n>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Scales the threshold for splitting a frame that has no detected transient into two envelopes anyway.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: SBR profiles\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 1..10000}, x100 where {\f1 100} = stock x1.00; default {\f1 -1} = off\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Not every level change in the high band is an attack. A crescendo, a swell, a chord change or a cymbal decaying unevenly all move the high band without triggering the transient detector, and one envelope for the whole frame then averages the movement away. This threshold decides how readily such a frame is still split in two, giving the second half its own levels.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Lowering it splits more often: finer time resolution on material that moves but does not attack, at the cost of a second set of envelope levels in many frames. Raising it splits less often: cheaper, and the high band follows slow movement less closely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a smaller effect than {\f1 --sbr-tran-thr}, and it addresses a different symptom. If the problem is hiss before a hit, use the threshold; if the problem is a high band that sounds static while the music swells, use this.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, lowering (split more often):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --sbr-tran-split 70}. Slightly more frames get two envelopes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --sbr-tran-split 30}. Most frames with any movement get split. Noticeably more envelope data, better tracking of swells.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --sbr-tran-split 1}. Nearly every frame split regardless of content; the bit cost is real and the benefit on steady material is zero.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, raising (split less often):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --sbr-tran-split 150}. Marginally fewer splits, marginally cheaper.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --sbr-tran-split 300}. Splits become rare. Measured: this does change the HE-AAC stream.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --sbr-tran-split 10000}. No frame is ever split on movement alone. One envelope per non transient frame, maximum smearing of slow high band changes, minimum envelope data.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: ignored in effect when {\f1 --sbr-num-env} forces a static grid. Complements {\f1 --sbr-tran-thr} rather than duplicating it: that one is about attacks, this one about frames with no attack. Every extra envelope is priced by {\f1 --sbr-amp-res}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --sbr-tran-peak <n>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Sets how peaky a candidate must be before it counts as an attack: the signal must fall below this fraction of the previous slot.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: SBR profiles\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 1..200}, raw x100 where {\f1 90} = the stock 0.90; default {\f1 -1} = off\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A real attack is not just loud, it is brief: the energy rises and then drops again. The detector checks that drop, requiring the following slot to fall below a fraction of the previous one. That fraction is this constant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Note the unit carefully. This is a {\b raw} x100 value, not a scale factor: it replaces the constant outright, so {\f1 90} is the stock 0.90 and is a no-op, while {\f1 100} is 1.0, not "stock". And 1.0 has a special consequence: a fraction of 1.0 or more means the condition can never be satisfied by a genuine drop in the way the detector tests it, so a slot never counts as dropping. Every value from 100 to 200 therefore produces one identical stream. The live range is 1..99.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Lowering the value demands a sharper drop, so fewer candidates qualify as attacks. Raising it toward 99 accepts gentler decays as attacks, so more do.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, lowering (stricter, fewer transients):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --sbr-tran-peak 80}. Slightly stricter than stock; soft attacks stop qualifying.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --sbr-tran-peak 50}. Only sharply decaying hits count. Expect more smearing on anything but dry percussion.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --sbr-tran-peak 1}. The drop requirement is absurd; almost nothing qualifies as an attack and the peak test alone suppresses the detector.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, raising (looser, more transients):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --sbr-tran-peak 95}. Gentler decays now count as attacks.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --sbr-tran-peak 99}. The loosest setting that still does anything. Attacks with long tails get their own envelopes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --sbr-tran-peak 200}. Beyond the useful range: 100 and above all mean 1.0, a slot never counts as dropping, and 100 through 200 are one and the same stream. Useful only as a demonstration that the knob has a hard edge.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: this is one condition of several; {\f1 --sbr-tran-thr} scales the master threshold and has far more leverage. If you want more or fewer transients overall, start there and use this only to change the {\i character} of what qualifies.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --sbr-tran-quiet <n>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Sets the quiet slot energy floor of the fast SBR transient detector, which decides how readily quiet passages produce transients.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: AAC-ELD with SBR only ({\f1 -p 39 -L 1}). No effect in HE-AAC or HE-AAC v2, which use the other detector\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 1..10000}, x100 where {\f1 100} = stock; default {\f1 -1} = off\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 AAC-ELD with SBR runs a second, lower latency transient detector, and that detector has an energy floor below which a slot is treated as quiet. The floor exists so that noise in silence does not register as attacks. Lower it and the detector looks into quieter passages, so soft hits in a sparse arrangement can get their own envelope. Raise it and quiet material is ignored.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Behaviour is strongly material dependent, which is worth knowing before you spend time on it: on a purely tonal test signal the detector never reached a decision this knob could change.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Measured at AAC-ELD with SBR, 24 kbps, across five test signals: values from 30 to 99 change the stream, {\f1 100} and above gave no change at all, and {\f1 1} and {\f1 10} give the same stream as each other. So the live range here is roughly 11..99 downward, with a floor effect below that and a ceiling at 100.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, lowering (quiet passages produce transients more readily):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --sbr-tran-quiet 90}. A small extension into quieter slots.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --sbr-tran-quiet 50}. Clearly more sensitive in sparse material; measured to change the stream.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --sbr-tran-quiet 1}. Floored: {\f1 1} and {\f1 10} give the same stream, so the detector has hit the bottom of what the floor can express.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, raising (quiet passages ignored):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild through exaggerated: {\f1 --sbr-tran-quiet 120}, {\f1 300}, {\f1 10000} all gave no stream change in our measurements at ELD plus SBR 24 kbps. The stock floor is already high enough that raising it further changes no decision on this material. Honest status: the direction is wired, the effect was not observed upward.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: nothing in HE-AAC or HE-AAC v2, because the fast detector is not used there. Within ELD plus SBR it sits alongside {\f1 --sbr-tran-dom}; {\f1 --sbr-tran-thr} and {\f1 --sbr-tran-split} apply to the main detector.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --sbr-tran-dom <n>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Sets how much louder a slot must be than the two before it when those are already marked as transients.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: AAC-ELD with SBR only ({\f1 -p 39 -L 1}). No effect in HE-AAC or HE-AAC v2\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 100..500}, x100 where {\f1 140} = the stock 1.4; default {\f1 -1} = off\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A roll, a flam or a double hit produces several candidate slots in a row. This rule stops the detector from marking all of them: once the two preceding slots are already transients, the next one has to be clearly louder, by this factor, to be marked as well. It is a dominance test, which is where the name comes from.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Lowering the factor toward 100 lets consecutive slots all count, so dense percussion produces more transients. Raising it means only a decisive escalation counts, so a roll registers once.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Honest status: this knob is wired to the constant it documents, and in our measurements it produced no stream change at all. Values from 100 to 500 gave identical output on five test signals at ELD plus SBR. The reason is most likely that the surrounding conditions are rarely all satisfied on this material, so the dominance test is not what decides. Treat it as available for experiments, not as a tool with a predictable result, and reach for {\f1 --sbr-tran-thr} instead.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, lowering (consecutive slots count more readily):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --sbr-tran-dom 130}. Slightly below stock.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --sbr-tran-dom 110}. Nearly no dominance requirement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --sbr-tran-dom 100}. The factor is 1.0, so a slot merely needs to match the two before it. No stream change observed.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, raising (only decisive escalation counts):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --sbr-tran-dom 180}. Above stock.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --sbr-tran-dom 300}. A slot must be three times the level of its predecessors.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --sbr-tran-dom 500}, the maximum. No stream change observed at any of these.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: same ELD plus SBR fast detector as {\f1 --sbr-tran-quiet}, and the same limitation: nothing in HE-AAC. If you want the transient behaviour in that detector to change audibly, {\f1 --sbr-tran-quiet} in the 30..99 range is the one with measured effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Missing harmonics detector: {\f1 --sbr-mh-tone} {\f1 --sbr-mh-diff} {\f1 --sbr-mh-decay-orig} {\f1 --sbr-mh-decay-diff} {\f1 --sbr-mh-sfm-sbr} {\f1 --sbr-mh-sfm-orig} {\f1 --sbr-mh-maxcomp} {\f1 --sbr-mh-deltatime}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The transient detector deals with time. This detector deals with a problem created by the copying itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 SBR builds the high band by patching lower spectrum upward. A tone that existed in the original high band is not guaranteed to survive that: the patch brings up whatever happened to be in the source region, so a real partial can land at the wrong frequency, arrive accompanied by partials that were never there, or simply vanish. On a glockenspiel, a triangle, a bell or a bright string harmonic this is exactly the content you notice. The missing harmonics detector compares the tonality of the original band with the tonality of the patched band, decides that a tone is missing, and signals the decoder to insert a synthetic sine in that band.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Both failure directions are audible. Too eager and you get whistling: steady, pitched tones up top that were never in the recording, often slightly off from the real harmonic series, which is the classic "SBR warble". Too conservative and bells and cymbals go dull as their partials quietly disappear.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Eight knobs reach into this detector. Be aware of their real status before spending time here: on our test material only two of them moved the stream at all. {\f1 --sbr-mh-tone} changed the output on a tonal signal at HE-AAC 24 kbps at values 1, 5 and 10, and {\f1 --sbr-mh-diff} at value 10. The other six produced no stream change at HE-AAC 24 to 96 kbps on tonal and mixed material, in either direction. That is a property of this detector on this material, not of the scaling: the x100 knobs here work in both directions and {\f1 100} is exactly stock. The detector simply needs several conditions to coincide before any of them decides the outcome, and on ordinary music it rarely gets there. Each card below says plainly where it stands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --sbr-mh-tone <n>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Scales how tonal a band must be before SBR fabricates a synthetic harmonic in it, and it is the main control in this section.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: SBR profiles\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 1..10000}, x100 where {\f1 100} = stock x1.00; default {\f1 -1} = off\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The detector measures tonality in the original band and compares it against a threshold. Scaling that threshold down makes more bands qualify as tonal, so more synthetic harmonics get added. Scaling it up makes fewer qualify.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What you hear in each direction is specific. More added tones means more of a bell's or cymbal's pitched content is restored, and more risk of whistling artefacts that were never in the source. Fewer added tones means a safer, duller top, with bright percussion losing its ring.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Measured: this is one of the two knobs in this section that moved the stream, on a tonal test signal at HE-AAC 24 kbps, at values 1, 5 and 10. Those are all far below stock, which tells you how low the threshold has to go before the detector changes its mind on that material.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, lowering the threshold (more synthetic harmonics):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --sbr-mh-tone 70}. A modest widening of what counts as tonal. On much material this will not change the stream at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --sbr-mh-tone 10}. Measured to change the stream on tonal material. Expect more added tones and a brighter, more pitched high band.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --sbr-mh-tone 1}. Almost any band counts as tonal. Measured to change the stream, and the direction to use if you want to hear what whistling artefacts sound like.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, raising the threshold (fewer synthetic harmonics):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --sbr-mh-tone 150}. Slightly stricter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --sbr-mh-tone 400}. Only strongly tonal bands get a harmonic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --sbr-mh-tone 10000}. The detector effectively never adds a harmonic. No stream change was observed above 100 on our material, so if you want this result, verify it on yours before relying on it.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: {\f1 --sbr-mh-diff} is the other trigger path into the same decision, and {\f1 --sbr-mh-sfm-orig} / {\f1 --sbr-mh-sfm-sbr} move the tone versus noise line that feeds the tonality measurement. {\f1 --sbr-mh-maxcomp} controls what happens around a harmonic once it is added, not whether it is added.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --sbr-mh-diff <n>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Scales the original versus patched tonality difference that triggers harmonic compensation.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: SBR profiles\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 1..10000}, x100 where {\f1 100} = stock x1.00; default {\f1 -1} = off\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --sbr-mh-tone} asks "is the original band tonal enough". This asks a different question: "did the patching change the tonality". It is the path that catches the case where the original had one clean tone and the patched band has several, or the reverse, which is the specific damage copying does.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Lowering the threshold makes a smaller difference count as damage, so compensation triggers more often. Raising it means only a large mismatch counts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Measured: this is the second of the two knobs in this section that moved the stream, at value 10 on our material. Values nearer stock, and values above stock, produced no change.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, lowering (compensate on smaller mismatches):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --sbr-mh-diff 70}. Modest; may well change nothing on your material.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --sbr-mh-diff 20}. Close to the value where we saw a change.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --sbr-mh-diff 10}. Measured to change the stream. Almost any tonality mismatch between original and patch triggers compensation.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, raising (compensate only on large mismatches):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --sbr-mh-diff 150}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --sbr-mh-diff 400}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --sbr-mh-diff 10000}. The difference path effectively never fires, leaving {\f1 --sbr-mh-tone} as the only trigger. No stream change observed upward on our material.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: the two trigger paths, this one and {\f1 --sbr-mh-tone}, feed the same decision, so raising one while lowering the other partly cancels. The flatness knobs {\f1 --sbr-mh-sfm-sbr} and {\f1 --sbr-mh-sfm-orig} decide what counts as tonal in the first place on each side of the comparison.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --sbr-mh-decay-orig <n>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Scales how long an already detected tone keeps being tracked in the original band as it decays.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: SBR profiles\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 1..10000}, x100 where {\f1 100} = stock x1.00; default {\f1 -1} = off\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A detector that only looked at the current frame would drop a tone as soon as it faded below threshold, which on a bell or cymbal tail is exactly when you still want it. So the detector keeps tracking a tone it has already found, with a decay. This scales the length of that memory.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Longer tracking means synthetic harmonics persist into tails, so decays stay pitched instead of turning into noise. Shorter tracking means tones are dropped sooner, so tails lose their ring earlier.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Honest status: wired, no effect observed. On tonal and mixed material at HE-AAC 24 to 96 kbps this produced no stream change in either direction. The decay only matters once the detector has found a tone to track, and the trigger conditions rarely coincided on our material.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, shortening the tracking:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --sbr-mh-decay-orig 70}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --sbr-mh-decay-orig 30}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --sbr-mh-decay-orig 1}. Effectively no memory: a tone is forgotten as soon as it drops. No stream change observed.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, lengthening the tracking:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --sbr-mh-decay-orig 150}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --sbr-mh-decay-orig 400}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --sbr-mh-decay-orig 10000}. A tone, once found, is tracked almost indefinitely. No stream change observed.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: only relevant where the detector fires, so lowering {\f1 --sbr-mh-tone} into its measured active range is the prerequisite for this knob mattering at all. {\f1 --sbr-mh-decay-diff} is the same mechanism on the difference guide.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --sbr-mh-decay-diff <n>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b The same decay as {\f1 --sbr-mh-decay-orig}, applied to the difference guide instead of the original band.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: SBR profiles\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 1..10000}, x100 where {\f1 100} = stock x1.00; default {\f1 -1} = off\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The detector keeps two tracking guides: one following tones in the original band, one following the original versus patch tonality difference. This scales the memory of the second, so it governs how long a detected {\i mismatch} keeps being treated as present after it stops being obvious.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Longer means compensation decisions persist across frames and are more stable. Shorter means they are re-evaluated nearly from scratch each frame, which reacts faster and can flicker.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Honest status: wired, no effect observed. No stream change on tonal or mixed material at HE-AAC 24 to 96 kbps, in either direction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, shortening:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --sbr-mh-decay-diff 70}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --sbr-mh-decay-diff 30}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --sbr-mh-decay-diff 1}. No memory on the difference guide. No stream change observed.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, lengthening:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --sbr-mh-decay-diff 150}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --sbr-mh-decay-diff 400}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --sbr-mh-decay-diff 10000}. The guide never forgets. No stream change observed.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: pairs with {\f1 --sbr-mh-decay-orig}, and like it depends on {\f1 --sbr-mh-diff} actually triggering before the memory length can matter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --sbr-mh-sfm-sbr <n>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Scales the spectral flatness threshold above which the patched band is judged noise like rather than tonal.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: SBR profiles\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 1..10000}, x100 where {\f1 100} = stock x1.00; default {\f1 -1} = off\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Spectral flatness measures how evenly energy is spread across a band: near 0 for a single clean tone, near 1 for noise. The detector uses it to classify each band, and this knob moves the line for the {\i patched} side of the comparison.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Lowering the threshold means the patched band is called noise like sooner, so the detector more often concludes that the copy lost the tone and compensation is needed. Raising it means the patch is given the benefit of the doubt as tonal, so fewer harmonics are added.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Honest status: wired, no effect observed. No stream change on tonal or mixed material at HE-AAC 24 to 96 kbps in either direction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One practical note if you measure this yourself: flatness has to be measured in the real SBR region, from the core bandwidth up to the final top edge as printed by {\f1 --verbose}. A window that runs to Nyquist includes the empty part above the top edge and returns a flatness of 0, which looks like perfect tonality and is an artefact of the window.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, lowering (patch called noise like sooner, more compensation):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --sbr-mh-sfm-sbr 70}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --sbr-mh-sfm-sbr 30}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --sbr-mh-sfm-sbr 1}. Nearly any patched band counts as noise like. No stream change observed.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, raising (patch called tonal for longer, less compensation):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --sbr-mh-sfm-sbr 150}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --sbr-mh-sfm-sbr 400}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --sbr-mh-sfm-sbr 10000}. The patched band is effectively always tonal. No stream change observed.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: works as a pair with {\f1 --sbr-mh-sfm-orig}; between them they catch "one tone in the original became several after patching". Moving only one side shifts the comparison asymmetrically, which is sometimes what you want and is harder to reason about.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --sbr-mh-sfm-orig <n>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b The same flatness threshold as {\f1 --sbr-mh-sfm-sbr}, for the original band.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: SBR profiles\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 1..10000}, x100 where {\f1 100} = stock x1.00; default {\f1 -1} = off\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This moves the tone versus noise line on the source side. Lowering it means the original band is called noise like sooner, so the detector concludes less often that there was a tone worth restoring. Raising it means the original is treated as tonal more readily, so more bands become candidates for a synthetic harmonic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Note that the two sfm knobs push the final decision in opposite senses despite being the same kind of threshold, because one describes what you had and the other what you got. If you want "add more harmonics", lower {\f1 --sbr-mh-sfm-sbr} and raise this one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Honest status: wired, no effect observed. No stream change on tonal or mixed material at HE-AAC 24 to 96 kbps in either direction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, lowering (original called noise like sooner, fewer harmonics):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --sbr-mh-sfm-orig 70}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --sbr-mh-sfm-orig 30}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --sbr-mh-sfm-orig 1}. The source is almost never tonal, so almost nothing is judged missing. No stream change observed.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, raising (original called tonal more readily, more harmonics):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --sbr-mh-sfm-orig 150}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --sbr-mh-sfm-orig 400}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --sbr-mh-sfm-orig 10000}. Nearly every original band counts as tonal. No stream change observed.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: the mirror of {\f1 --sbr-mh-sfm-sbr}, and both feed the tonality comparison that {\f1 --sbr-mh-diff} thresholds. Changing all three at once makes the result very hard to attribute; move one at a time.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --sbr-mh-maxcomp <n>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Caps how much envelope compensation is applied around a synthetic harmonic.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: SBR profiles\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 0..200}, a plain integer where {\f1 50} = stock; default {\f1 -1} = off\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Inserting a sine into a band changes the energy of that band, so the envelope around it has to be adjusted or the band ends up too loud. This caps that adjustment. It is not an x100 scale: the number is the limit itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A higher cap allows larger corrections, so neighbouring bands are pulled further and less noise is left sitting next to the added tone: the harmonic stands out more cleanly. A lower cap limits the correction, so the added tone sits in more of its original surrounding noise and blends in, or becomes audibly too loud if the correction needed was larger than the cap.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Honest status: wired, no effect observed. No stream change on tonal or mixed material at HE-AAC 24 to 96 kbps in either direction, which follows from the detector rarely adding a harmonic on this material in the first place.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, lowering the cap:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --sbr-mh-maxcomp 35}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --sbr-mh-maxcomp 10}. Very little compensation allowed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --sbr-mh-maxcomp 0}. No compensation at all around an added harmonic. No stream change observed.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, raising the cap:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --sbr-mh-maxcomp 70}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --sbr-mh-maxcomp 120}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --sbr-mh-maxcomp 200}, the maximum. Correction is effectively unlimited. No stream change observed.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: only applies where a harmonic is actually added, so {\f1 --sbr-mh-tone} has to be in its active range first. The noise that sits next to the added tone is also governed by {\f1 --sbr-noise-max} and the noise floor knobs, which act on every band rather than just the harmonic's neighbours.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --sbr-mh-deltatime <n>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Sets the maximum transient distance for a frame to count as a transient frame inside the missing harmonics detector.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: SBR profiles\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 0..64}, a plain integer; default {\f1 -1} = off, which leaves 9 for AAC and 16 for AAC-LD\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The missing harmonics detector behaves differently in frames it considers transient, because adding a steady synthetic sine across an attack is a bad idea. This sets how far, in detector time units, a transient may be from the frame for the frame still to count as transient. It is a plain integer, not an x100 scale, and the stock value differs by profile.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A larger distance means more frames count as transient, so the detector takes its cautious path more often and adds fewer harmonics in the neighbourhood of hits. A smaller distance means fewer frames count as transient, so the detector adds harmonics closer to attacks, which is where a synthetic sine is most likely to sound wrong.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Honest status: wired, no effect observed. No stream change on tonal or mixed material at HE-AAC 24 to 96 kbps in either direction.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, lowering the distance:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --sbr-mh-deltatime 6}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --sbr-mh-deltatime 2}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --sbr-mh-deltatime 0}. No frame counts as transient on distance, so the cautious path is never taken. No stream change observed.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, raising the distance:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --sbr-mh-deltatime 16}, which is the AAC-LD stock value applied to AAC.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --sbr-mh-deltatime 32}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --sbr-mh-deltatime 64}, the maximum. Almost every frame near any hit counts as transient. No stream change observed.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: this reads the transient decision, so it is downstream of {\f1 --sbr-tran-thr} and {\f1 --sbr-tran-peak}: changing what counts as a transient changes what this knob is measuring distance to. It has no influence on framing itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Inverse filtering and noise floor: {\f1 --sbr-invf} {\f1 --sbr-softness} {\f1 --sbr-invf-min} {\f1 --sbr-invf-max} {\f1 --sbr-invf-bias} {\f1 --sbr-invf-table} {\f1 --sbr-invf-hyst} {\f1 --sbr-invf-sbr-thr} {\f1 --sbr-invf-orig-thr} {\f1 --sbr-invf-smooth} {\f1 --sbr-nf-weight} {\f1 --sbr-nf-weight-from} {\f1 --sbr-nf-smooth} {\f1 --sbr-noise-max} {\f1 --sbr-noise-floor-offset}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is where the character of the high band is actually decided, and it is the part of SBR worth understanding properly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The copied high band inherits the fine structure of the band it was copied from. That structure is tonal: peaks and valleys at the wrong frequencies for where it has landed. The result sounds metallic, ringing, sometimes chorused, and once you have heard it you cannot unhear it. Inverse filtering is the cure: the decoder applies a whitening filter to the patch, flattening that inherited structure. There are four strengths, 0 off, 1 low, 2 mid, 3 high, chosen per band and per frame. The noise floor is the other half of the same cure: synthetic noise mixed into each band to replace the structure that whitening removed. Too little of both and the top is metallic; too much and it is hissy and washed out, with cymbals reduced to shaped noise.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The level is normally chosen by a {\b tonality estimator} that compares the original band with the patched band, band by band and frame by frame. That dynamic decision is the thing to preserve: it is right much more often than any fixed value you could pick.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So this section has two kinds of switch, and the priority between them matters:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 1.\tx360\tab {\f1 --sbr-invf} {\b forces} one fixed level for the whole file, in every band and every moment. It wins over everything else here. Use it to diagnose, or when you know the material is uniform.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 2.\tx360\tab Everything else leaves the decision dynamic and changes the estimator's rules: bounds, bias, decision table, hysteresis, smoothing, border positions, noise weight. The decision is still made per band and per frame, just along different rules.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Within group 2, {\f1 --sbr-softness} is a macro that sets several of those rules at once, and {\b every explicit knob wins over the macro}. So {\f1 --sbr-softness 6 --sbr-invf-max 2} gives you the macro's softness with your own ceiling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two measurement facts shape how you should use this section.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 First, the softness macro deliberately does {\i not} move the tonality borders. Shifting them was measured to make the per band decision flicker, 0.2 to 0.29 changes per band per frame against 0.004 to 0.04 for stock, which is audible as instability. {\f1 --sbr-invf-sbr-thr} and {\f1 --sbr-invf-orig-thr} expose that mechanism anyway, because sometimes you want it, but that is why the macro avoids them.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Second, the noise floor weight is clipped by the noise ceiling. With the tuning table's ceiling (0.125 at 24 to 48 kbps) about 95 percent of bands already sit at the ceiling, so {\f1 --sbr-nf-weight} on its own frequently changes nothing at all. It needs a raised ceiling {\i and} high invf bands to do anything: measured, it moved the stream only with {\f1 --sbr-noise-max 6} plus either {\f1 --sbr-invf 3} or {\f1 --sbr-nf-weight-from 1}. Alone, or with only one of those, nothing changed. Levels 5 to 7 of {\f1 --sbr-softness} raise the ceiling for you.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At their neutral values ({\f1 --sbr-invf-hyst 1.0}, {\f1 --sbr-invf-bias 0}, {\f1 --sbr-invf-smooth 2}, {\f1 --sbr-nf-weight 0.25}, {\f1 --sbr-nf-weight-from 3}, {\f1 --sbr-invf-table 0}, {\f1 --sbr-invf-min 0}, {\f1 --sbr-invf-max 3}) these knobs give a stream byte identical to stock, as do {\f1 --sbr-softness 2} and {\f1 --speech --sbr-softness 4}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --sbr-invf <0..3>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Forces one fixed inverse filtering level for the whole file, overriding the tonality estimator completely.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: SBR profiles ({\f1 -p 5}, {\f1 -p 29}, {\f1 -p 39 -L 1})\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 0} off, {\f1 1} low, {\f1 2} mid, {\f1 3} high; default {\f1 -1} = auto, the tonality estimator decides per band and per frame\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Higher means more whitening of the patched band: less metallic ringing, less detail, a flatter and more noise like top. Lower means the inherited tonal structure is left in place: more apparent detail, more metallic character.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The drawback of forcing is exactly what it says: one level applies everywhere, including in the bands and moments where the estimator would have chosen differently. A file with a quiet tonal intro and a dense chorus wants different levels in those places, and a forced value gives them the same one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 That said, this is the most useful knob here for two jobs. The first is diagnosis: encode at {\f1 0} and at {\f1 3} and listen to the extremes, and you will immediately know which end of the axis your material needs. The second is uniform material, where there is nothing for the estimator to adapt to anyway.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Values:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 0} inverse filtering off. No whitening at all. The patch keeps its inherited structure: maximum apparent detail, maximum metallic ringing. On tonal material this is clearly an artefact; on noise like material it can be the cleanest option.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 1} low. A light whitening.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 2} mid. Moderate whitening, which is roughly where the estimator sits on average with the music table.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 3} high. Strong whitening. The top becomes flat and noise like; metallic ringing disappears and so does a good deal of perceived detail.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 -1} auto. The estimator decides. The recommended setting unless you are diagnosing.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Example in context: {\f1 fdkaac -p 5 -b 64000 --sbr-invf 3 -o out.m4a in.wav}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: this {\b wins over every other switch in this section}, including {\f1 --sbr-softness} and the min/max/bias knobs, because there is no dynamic decision left to bound. If you want to shape the decision rather than replace it, leave this off. Pairing {\f1 --sbr-invf 3} with {\f1 --sbr-noise-max 6} is one of the two combinations that makes {\f1 --sbr-nf-weight} do anything.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --sbr-softness <0..7>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b One macro from a hard, metallic top to a very soft one, setting several estimator rules at once while keeping the decision dynamic.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: SBR profiles\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 0..7}; default {\f1 -1} = off (stock, which is equivalent to level {\f1 2} for music)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the knob to try first. It moves you along the metallic versus hissy axis without freezing the decision, by setting the decision table, the bias, the noise weight, the min/max bounds and the noise ceiling in combinations that were measured to be monotonic and stable. It deliberately does not touch the tonality borders, because doing that was measured to make the per band decision flicker.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Values:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 0} one step less whitening than the music tuning: harder, more metallic than stock.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 1} never HIGH whitening. Note that on music the estimator rarely picks HIGH anyway, so on music material this bound often never binds: measured at HE-AAC 32 kbps, levels {\f1 1} and {\f1 2} gave a stream identical to stock on tonal, voice and mixed signals.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 2} stock music estimators. Byte identical to not using the switch at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 3} at least LOW whitening: the estimator may no longer choose off. Measured equal to {\f1 --sbr-invf 1} on a tonal signal, because there the estimator wanted off everywhere and the floor lifted it all to LOW.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 4} stock speech estimators: the speech decision table and noise weight, without the rest of {\f1 --speech}, so the crossover is untouched.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 5} as {\f1 4} plus a raised noise ceiling (0.5) and the weight extended to MID bands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 6} as {\f1 5} plus one more step of whitening.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 7} very soft: ceiling 1.0, maximum whitening and fill noise. The top becomes unambiguously noise like.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 -1} off.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Measured at HE-AAC 32 kbps, invf levels read back from the bitstream, flatness of the decoded SBR band 7 to 14.5 kHz where 0 is tonal and 1 is noise like:\par}
