{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The scale is monotonic on every sample, and per band mode changes stay at the stock rate (0.004 to 0.04 per band per frame). Also measured: level {\f1 0} equals {\f1 --sbr-invf 0} on tonal and mixed material, and levels {\f1 4} through {\f1 7} are distinct from each other.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Example in context: {\f1 fdkaac -p 5 -b 32000 --sbr-softness 6 -o out.m4a in.wav}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: {\b every explicit knob below wins over this macro}, which is how you combine them: {\f1 --sbr-softness 6 --sbr-invf-max 2} keeps the macro's noise handling with your own ceiling. {\f1 --sbr-invf} wins over this entirely. Neighbouring levels can give the same stream when your material never reaches the bound they differ in; that is expected, not a failure.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --sbr-invf-min <0..3>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Sets a floor under the estimator: the lowest inverse filtering level it is allowed to pick.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: SBR profiles\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 0..3}; default {\f1 -1} = off (no floor, equivalent to {\f1 0})\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The estimator keeps deciding per band and per frame; this just removes the bottom of its range. It is the tool for "the top is metallic in the quiet passages", where the estimator correctly sees a tonal band and chooses little or no whitening, and you disagree.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Raising the floor guarantees a minimum amount of whitening everywhere, which costs detail in the bands that genuinely did not need it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Values:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 0} floor at off. No constraint; identical to not using the switch.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 1} the estimator may never choose off. The gentlest useful floor, and the one worth trying when metallic ringing appears only occasionally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 2} at least MID whitening everywhere. A strong intervention: bands that wanted none now get moderate whitening.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 3} at least HIGH everywhere, which means the level is pinned to HIGH since 3 is the maximum. That makes it equivalent in effect to {\f1 --sbr-invf 3}, and if that is what you want, say so directly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 -1} off.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Example in context: {\f1 fdkaac -p 5 -b 48000 --sbr-invf-min 1 -o out.m4a in.wav}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: applied after {\f1 --sbr-invf-bias}, together with {\f1 --sbr-invf-max} as the other end of the clamp. Setting {\f1 --sbr-invf-min 3} or {\f1 --sbr-invf-min} equal to {\f1 --sbr-invf-max} collapses the dynamic range to a single value, which is {\f1 --sbr-invf} the long way round. Wins over {\f1 --sbr-softness}; loses to {\f1 --sbr-invf}. {\f1 --sbr-softness 3} is the packaged version of {\f1 --sbr-invf-min 1}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --sbr-invf-max <0..3>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Sets a ceiling over the estimator: the highest inverse filtering level it is allowed to pick.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: SBR profiles\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 0..3}; default {\f1 -1} = off (no ceiling, equivalent to {\f1 3})\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mirror of {\f1 --sbr-invf-min}, and the more frequently useful of the two. It is the tool for "the top goes hissy and loses detail on the loud passages", where the estimator picks strong whitening and takes the sparkle with it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Lowering the ceiling preserves detail at the price of letting some metallic character through in the bands that genuinely needed whitening.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Values:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 3} ceiling at HIGH. No constraint; identical to not using the switch.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 2} never HIGH. The usual choice: it removes only the most aggressive whitening and keeps the rest of the estimator's judgement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 1} never above LOW. A strong detail preserving setting; expect audible metallic character on tonal material.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 0} the ceiling is off, so no whitening is permitted anywhere. Equivalent in effect to {\f1 --sbr-invf 0}, and better written that way.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 -1} off.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Example in context: {\f1 fdkaac -p 5 -b 64000 --sbr-invf-max 2 --sbr-invf-hyst 1.5 -o out.m4a in.wav}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: applied after {\f1 --sbr-invf-bias}, paired with {\f1 --sbr-invf-min}. {\f1 --sbr-softness 1} is the packaged version of {\f1 --sbr-invf-max 2}, and note the measured caveat there: on music the estimator rarely reaches HIGH, so this ceiling can be a no-op on music material and still be exactly right on speech. Wins over {\f1 --sbr-softness}; loses to {\f1 --sbr-invf}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --sbr-invf-bias <-3..3>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Shifts every dynamic inverse filtering decision up or down by a fixed number of steps.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: SBR profiles\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 -3..3} steps; default {\f1 0} = off\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Where min and max clip the range, this slides it. The estimator's per band, per frame pattern of decisions is preserved exactly, and the whole pattern moves. That makes it the cleanest way to say "the estimator has the right idea, it is just consistently too soft" or "consistently too hard". Positive means more whitening.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Because the result is clamped to 0..3 afterwards, large biases flatten the pattern at whichever end they run into: at {\f1 +3} almost everything lands on HIGH and the per band variation you were trying to keep is gone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, more whitening:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --sbr-invf-bias 1}. Every band one step more whitened. The pattern is intact, the character is softer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --sbr-invf-bias 2}. Two steps. Bands that wanted off now get MID; metallic character is largely gone and so is some detail.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --sbr-invf-bias 3}. Everything the estimator did not already want at HIGH gets pushed there by the clamp. This is {\f1 --sbr-invf 3} with extra steps.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, less whitening:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --sbr-invf-bias -1}. One step less everywhere. More detail, a hint more metallic.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --sbr-invf-bias -2}. Two steps less. Only the bands the estimator wanted at HIGH retain any whitening.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --sbr-invf-bias -3}. Nearly everything clamps to off, which is {\f1 --sbr-invf 0} the long way round, with maximum metallic ringing on tonal material.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: applied {\b before} {\f1 --sbr-invf-min} and {\f1 --sbr-invf-max}, so you can bias and then clamp: {\f1 --sbr-invf-bias 2 --sbr-invf-max 2} shifts everything up but refuses HIGH. Wins over {\f1 --sbr-softness}; loses to {\f1 --sbr-invf}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --sbr-invf-table <0|1>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Chooses which decision table the tonality estimator uses: the music table or the speech table.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: SBR profiles\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 0} music, {\f1 1} speech; default {\f1 -1} = follows {\f1 --speech}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The estimator's thresholds come from a table, and FDK ships two: one tuned for music, one for speech. The speech table whitens considerably more readily, because speech above the crossover is mostly fricatives and breath, where inherited tonal structure is pure artefact and there is no detail worth protecting.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This switch picks the table and {\b only} the table. {\f1 --speech} also changes the crossover and other tuning; if you want the speech estimator behaviour on music material without rearranging the frequency layout, this is the switch for it. That combination is genuinely useful: spoken word programme, audiobooks, podcast material at low bitrate, where you want the whitening behaviour but your own crossover.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Values:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 0} music table. Conservative whitening, protects tonal detail.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 1} speech table. Whitens much more readily. Measured mean invf level with the speech table is near 2.0 against about 1.0 for the music table at HE-AAC 32 kbps.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 -1} off: follow {\f1 --speech}, so music table unless {\f1 --speech} is given.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Example in context: {\f1 fdkaac -p 5 -b 32000 --sbr-invf-table 1 -o out.m4a speech.wav}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: {\f1 --sbr-softness 4} is the packaged version of this plus the speech noise weight. {\f1 --speech} sets this implicitly and also moves the crossover, which is the difference worth remembering. Wins over {\f1 --sbr-softness}; loses to {\f1 --sbr-invf}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --sbr-invf-hyst <x>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Sets the hysteresis of the decision borders: how much the tonality has to change before the estimator switches a band to a different level.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: SBR profiles\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 0..2}, decimal where {\f1 1.0} = stock; default {\f1 -1} = off\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: decimal, up to 3 places, {\f1 .} or {\f1 ,}\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A band whose tonality sits right on a decision border would otherwise flip level every frame, and flipping whitening levels frame to frame is audible as a kind of fluttering or unsteadiness up top. Hysteresis widens the borders into a dead zone, so a band keeps its current level until the tonality has moved meaningfully past the line.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Lowering it reacts faster to genuine changes and switches more often. Raising it is more stable and slower to follow real changes, so a passage that turns tonal part way through keeps the previous treatment for longer. Above 2 the widened borders would overlap each other, which has no meaning, so that is rejected.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For reference, the stock per band switching rate is 0.004 to 0.04 changes per band per frame, and a configuration measured at 0.2 to 0.29 was clearly unstable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, less hysteresis (faster, more switching):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --sbr-invf-hyst 0.8}. Slightly quicker to follow changes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --sbr-invf-hyst 0.3}. Clearly more switching; use when the estimator feels sluggish on material that changes character quickly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --sbr-invf-hyst 0}. No dead zone at all. Bands sitting on a border flip every frame, which is the instability hysteresis exists to prevent.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, more hysteresis (slower, more stable):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --sbr-invf-hyst 1.2}. A little stickier than stock.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --sbr-invf-hyst 1.5}. Levels hold through moderate tonality changes. A good pairing with {\f1 --sbr-invf-max 2} when you want a calm, predictable top.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --sbr-invf-hyst 2}, the maximum. The dead zones are as wide as they can be; bands essentially keep whatever level they first landed on, which on material that changes character is simply the wrong level most of the time.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: {\f1 --sbr-invf-smooth} attacks the same instability from the other side, by averaging over frames instead of widening borders; using both at their extremes is redundant. {\f1 --sbr-invf-sbr-thr} and {\f1 --sbr-invf-orig-thr} move the borders this knob is widening. Wins over {\f1 --sbr-softness}; loses to {\f1 --sbr-invf}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --sbr-invf-sbr-thr <x>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Shifts the tonality decision borders for the patched band, roughly in dB.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: SBR profiles\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 -20..20}, about dB, decimal; default {\f1 0} = off\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: decimal, up to 3 places, {\f1 .} or {\f1 ,}\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The estimator measures tonality on both sides of the comparison and compares each against borders. This moves the borders on the patched side. Positive means the patched band counts as tonal sooner, and a tonal patch is what whitening is for, so positive means more invf.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a sharper instrument than bias or min/max, and a riskier one. Moving borders changes {\i which} bands cross them and in which frames, so it can make the decision flicker: this mechanism measured 0.2 to 0.29 changes per band per frame against 0.004 to 0.04 for stock, which is why {\f1 --sbr-softness} does not use it. If you reach for this, consider raising {\f1 --sbr-invf-hyst} at the same time to buy the stability back.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, positive (patched band tonal sooner, more invf):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --sbr-invf-sbr-thr 2}. A small shift; a few more bands cross into whitening.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --sbr-invf-sbr-thr 8}. Many more bands count as tonal. Expect noticeably more whitening and some flicker.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --sbr-invf-sbr-thr 20}, the maximum. Essentially every patched band counts as tonal, so the measurement no longer discriminates and the estimator's judgement is replaced by a constant. {\f1 --sbr-invf 3} does the same thing without the flicker.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, negative (patched band tonal later, less invf):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --sbr-invf-sbr-thr -2}. Slightly fewer bands whitened.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --sbr-invf-sbr-thr -8}. Only strongly tonal patches get whitened; more detail, more metallic character.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --sbr-invf-sbr-thr -20}, the minimum. Nothing counts as tonal, so nothing is whitened. The caricature of "preserve all detail", and it sounds like it.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: the mirror of {\f1 --sbr-invf-orig-thr}, and the two combine into the original versus patch comparison. {\f1 --sbr-invf-hyst} is the stability counterweight. Wins over {\f1 --sbr-softness}; loses to {\f1 --sbr-invf}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --sbr-invf-orig-thr <x>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Shifts the tonality decision borders for the original band, roughly in dB.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: SBR profiles\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 -20..20}, about dB, decimal; default {\f1 0} = off\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: decimal, up to 3 places, {\f1 .} or {\f1 ,}\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The same mechanism on the source side, and the sign reads the other way round, which is the thing to get right. The estimator whitens when the patch looks more tonal than the original deserves. So making the {\i original} count as tonal {\b later} means the comparison finds a bigger mismatch more often, and positive values therefore also mean more whitening. Both threshold knobs point the same way in outcome despite describing opposite sides.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The flicker caveat applies here identically: this is a border shift, and border shifts destabilise the per band decision more than bias or clamping do.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, positive (original counts as tonal later, more invf):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --sbr-invf-orig-thr 2}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --sbr-invf-orig-thr 8}. The original is credited with tonality rarely, so the patch looks wrong more often and gets whitened.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --sbr-invf-orig-thr 20}, the maximum. The original never counts as tonal, so every patch is treated as damage. Maximum whitening by a route that also maximises flicker.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, negative (original counts as tonal sooner, less invf):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --sbr-invf-orig-thr -2}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --sbr-invf-orig-thr -8}. The original is generously credited as tonal, so the patch is more often judged acceptable and left alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --sbr-invf-orig-thr -20}, the minimum. Everything in the original counts as tonal, the comparison finds no mismatch, and whitening effectively stops.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: pairs with {\f1 --sbr-invf-sbr-thr}; moving both in the same direction partly cancels, since one raises the mismatch and the other lowers it. The noise floor reads the same original versus patch gap through {\f1 --sbr-nf-weight}, so shifting these borders changes the fill noise as well, not just the whitening. Wins over {\f1 --sbr-softness}; loses to {\f1 --sbr-invf}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --sbr-invf-smooth <0..2>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Sets how many frames the tonality estimator averages before deciding.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: SBR profiles\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 0}, {\f1 1}, {\f1 2}; default {\f1 -1} = off (stock averages 2)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Averaging over frames is the other defence against an unstable decision. With averaging, one anomalous frame cannot flip a band's whitening level; without it, each frame is judged on its own. The trade is the usual one: stability against reaction speed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Values:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 0} this frame only. Fastest reaction to a genuine change of character, most frame to frame switching. Use on material that changes quickly, and expect some unsteadiness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 1} a shorter average than stock. The middle option.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 2} stock. The balance FDK chose, and a good one on most material.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 -1} off, which is the same as {\f1 2}.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Example in context: {\f1 fdkaac -p 5 -b 64000 --sbr-invf-smooth 0 --sbr-invf-hyst 1.5 -o out.m4a in.wav}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: this and {\f1 --sbr-invf-hyst} both buy stability, by different means, so pairing low smoothing with high hysteresis is a reasonable way to get fast reaction without flutter. Wins over {\f1 --sbr-softness}; loses to {\f1 --sbr-invf}, which has no decision left to smooth.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --sbr-nf-weight <x>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Sets how strongly the original versus patch tonality gap raises the noise floor.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: SBR profiles, and only where the noise ceiling leaves room (see below)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 0.0..1.0}, decimal; default {\f1 -1} = follows {\f1 --speech} (music 0.25, speech 1.0)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: decimal, up to 3 places, {\f1 .} or {\f1 ,}\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Whitening removes structure; fill noise replaces it. This weight decides how much of the measured mismatch between the original and the patched band is converted into noise floor. Higher means more fill noise wherever the patch is too tonal, which is why the speech tuning uses 1.0: in speech the high band should be noise, so filling it is always right. Lower means the gap is mostly handled by whitening alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Read this before using it.} The weighted noise is clipped by the noise ceiling, and with the tuning table's ceiling (0.125 at 24 to 48 kbps) about 95 percent of bands are already at the ceiling, so raising the weight changes nothing because there is no headroom to use. Measured, this knob moved the stream only with {\f1 --sbr-noise-max 6} {\b plus} either {\f1 --sbr-invf 3} or {\f1 --sbr-nf-weight-from 1}. Alone, or with only one of those two, nothing changed at all. It needs a raised ceiling and bands at a high invf level, since by default only HIGH bands receive the weight.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So the working recipe is {\f1 --sbr-noise-max 6 --sbr-nf-weight-from 1 --sbr-nf-weight <x>}, or let {\f1 --sbr-softness 5}, {\f1 6} or {\f1 7} raise the ceiling for you.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, more fill noise (with the ceiling raised):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --sbr-noise-max 6 --sbr-nf-weight-from 1 --sbr-nf-weight 0.4}. A little more air where the patch is too tonal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --sbr-noise-max 6 --sbr-nf-weight-from 1 --sbr-nf-weight 0.7}. Clearly more fill noise; the top reads as airier and less metallic, with less apparent detail.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --sbr-noise-max 6 --sbr-nf-weight-from 1 --sbr-nf-weight 1}, the maximum, which is the speech value applied to every band from LOW upward with the ceiling wide open. On music this is hiss where cymbals used to be.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, less fill noise:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --sbr-nf-weight 0.15}. Below the music default; slightly drier top.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --sbr-nf-weight 0.05}. The tonality gap contributes almost no noise; whitening has to do the work alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --sbr-nf-weight 0}, the minimum. The gap is ignored entirely. With the default ceiling this may well be bit identical to stock, since most bands are clipped anyway, which is itself the lesson about this knob.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: needs {\f1 --sbr-noise-max 3} or {\f1 6} to have headroom, and {\f1 --sbr-nf-weight-from} to reach bands below HIGH. {\f1 --sbr-noise-floor-offset} raises the floor unconditionally and is the blunter alternative when this one refuses to move. Wins over {\f1 --sbr-softness}; {\f1 --sbr-invf 3} does not block it, it is one of the two things that enables it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --sbr-nf-weight-from <1..3>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Sets the lowest inverse filtering level whose bands receive the noise floor weight.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: SBR profiles\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 1} LOW and above, {\f1 2} MID and above, {\f1 3} HIGH only; default {\f1 -1} = off (stock is {\f1 3})\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The tonality gap weight is not applied everywhere. By default only bands that got HIGH whitening receive it, on the reasoning that those are the bands where structure was really destroyed and needs replacing. This switch widens that set downward.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 That makes it the enabling switch for {\f1 --sbr-nf-weight}: on music the estimator rarely picks HIGH, so with the stock setting the weight has almost no bands to act on, and the knob looks dead. Set this to {\f1 1} and every whitened band is in scope.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Values:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 3} HIGH only. Stock. The most conservative, and the reason {\f1 --sbr-nf-weight} so often appears to do nothing on music.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 2} MID and HIGH. A moderate widening, and what {\f1 --sbr-softness 5} and above do.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 1} LOW, MID and HIGH. Every band with any whitening gets the weight. The widest scope, and the one measured to let {\f1 --sbr-nf-weight} change the stream when paired with {\f1 --sbr-noise-max 6}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 -1} off, same as {\f1 3}.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Example in context: {\f1 fdkaac -p 5 -b 32000 --sbr-noise-max 6 --sbr-nf-weight-from 1 --sbr-nf-weight 0.7 -o out.m4a in.wav}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: pointless without {\f1 --sbr-nf-weight} doing something, and {\f1 --sbr-nf-weight} is pointless without headroom from {\f1 --sbr-noise-max}. All three move together. Wins over {\f1 --sbr-softness}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --sbr-nf-smooth <0>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Turns off the time smoothing of the noise floor.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: SBR profiles\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 0} = no smoothing; default {\f1 -1} = off, which leaves the stock smoothing over 4 frames\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The noise floor is normally averaged over four frames, so the amount of fill noise in a band changes gradually. That is right on steady material, where a jumping noise level would be audible as a pumping or breathing quality in the top end. It is wrong where the material genuinely changes fast: the smoothed floor arrives late, so a sudden quiet passage still carries the noise of the loud one for a few frames, and a sudden bright passage is under filled at its start.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This switch is one directional. There is no value that increases the smoothing; {\f1 0} removes it and anything else means leave it alone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Values:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 0} no time smoothing. The noise floor follows the frame by frame estimate exactly. Faster, and on steady material audibly less stable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 -1} off: stock smoothing over 4 frames. The right default.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Example in context: {\f1 fdkaac -p 5 -b 64000 --sbr-nf-smooth 0 -o out.m4a transients.wav}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: the noise analogue of {\f1 --sbr-invf-smooth 0}, and the same reasoning applies: use it on material with fast changes of character, and expect less stability on sustained material. Its effect is bounded by {\f1 --sbr-noise-max}: if the ceiling is clipping most bands, there is little variation left for smoothing to smooth. Wins over {\f1 --sbr-softness}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --sbr-noise-max <6|3|-3>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Sets the ceiling on how loud the noise SBR injects may get: the air versus hiss limit up top.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: SBR profiles\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 6} = 1.0, {\f1 3} = 0.5, {\f1 -3} = 0.125; default {\f1 -1} = the value from the bitrate tuning table\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the most consequential noise knob, not because it sets the level but because it sets the limit everything else runs into. The three values are the three the FDK noise estimator understands, and they correspond to amplitude ceilings of 1.0, 0.5 and 0.125. The tuning table picks 0.125 at 24 to 48 kbps, and at that ceiling about 95 percent of bands are already clipped, which is why fill noise feels fixed until you raise it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Raising the ceiling does not by itself add noise; it allows the estimator and the weight to add more where they already wanted to. Lowering it caps the top end into something drier and more tonal, which on sparse material is cleaner and on cymbals and breath sounds thin.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Which of the three values is a no-op depends on the tuning table row you are on, so measure rather than assume. Measured: on tonal and mixed signals at HE-AAC 32 kbps, {\f1 -3} equals stock and {\f1 6} equals {\f1 3}; on a voice signal at the same setting, {\f1 6}, {\f1 3} and stock are all the same.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Values:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 -3} ceiling 0.125. The driest, and the table's choice at low bitrate. More tonal, more metallic, less hiss.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 3} ceiling 0.5. Four times the headroom. Fill noise can actually vary band to band.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 6} ceiling 1.0. Maximum headroom. Paired with {\f1 --sbr-nf-weight} and {\f1 --sbr-nf-weight-from 1} this is the configuration where the noise floor knobs finally have room to work; on its own it mostly lifts the bands the estimator was already pushing against the ceiling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 -1} off: the bitrate tuning table decides.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Example in context: {\f1 fdkaac -p 5 -b 32000 --sbr-noise-max 6 --sbr-nf-weight-from 1 --sbr-nf-weight 0.7 -o out.m4a in.wav}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: this is the gate in front of {\f1 --sbr-nf-weight}, {\f1 --sbr-nf-weight-from} and {\f1 --sbr-nf-smooth}, and {\f1 --sbr-softness} levels 5 to 7 raise it for you (5 and 6 to 0.5, 7 to 1.0). {\f1 --sbr-noise-bands} sets the frequency resolution of the floor this ceiling caps, so a dense floor at a low ceiling is wasted side information.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel3 \b \fs24 {\f1 --sbr-noise-floor-offset <n>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Offsets the whole SBR noise floor, adding or removing fill noise everywhere at once.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: SBR profiles\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 0..11}; default {\f1 -128} = off\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Where {\f1 --sbr-nf-weight} adds noise conditionally, based on a measured mismatch, this simply shifts the floor. Bigger means more fill noise in the reconstruction across the board, which reads as more air and more hiss, and smothers whatever inherited tonal structure survived whitening. Smaller means a drier, more tonal top.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 That bluntness is sometimes exactly what you want. When {\f1 --sbr-nf-weight} refuses to move because every band is clipped at the ceiling, this is the knob that still works, because it acts on the floor itself rather than on the gap measurement.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The accepted range is {\f1 0..11} and nothing else. Outside it, FDK's own noise handling hits an assertion and aborts, so the encoder rejects out of range values with a clean error rather than letting that happen.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, more fill noise:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --sbr-noise-floor-offset 3}. A touch more air up top.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --sbr-noise-floor-offset 7}. Clearly noisier top; metallic character is buried and so is fine detail.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --sbr-noise-floor-offset 11}, the maximum. The high band is dominated by fill noise. Cymbals become shaped hiss, and the result is unambiguously a caricature, which makes it a useful reference point for how much of your top end is noise.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, less fill noise:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --sbr-noise-floor-offset 2}. Slightly drier.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --sbr-noise-floor-offset 1}. Minimal fill; the patched band's own structure and whatever whitening leaves behind carry the top end.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --sbr-noise-floor-offset 0}, the minimum. No offset contribution at all. On tonal material expect the metallic ringing that fill noise exists to mask, which pairs badly with low {\f1 --sbr-invf} settings and is worth hearing once for exactly that reason.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: still subject to {\f1 --sbr-noise-max}, so a high offset against the 0.125 ceiling clips. Works in the same direction as {\f1 --sbr-nf-weight} but unconditionally, which makes it the right choice when you want a global change and the wrong one when you want the encoder to decide where noise belongs. Against low {\f1 --sbr-invf} levels it is fighting a different artefact with the wrong tool: metallic ringing from un whitened structure is better fixed with whitening than masked with noise.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 D. Masking, noise shaping and detail\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the group that decides how much noise the encoder is willing to leave in each band, and where it fabricates signal instead of coding it. Four ideas carry the whole group, so they are explained once here and the cards refer back.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Masking threshold.} For every scalefactor band, in every frame, the psychoacoustic model computes a level below which added noise should be inaudible, because louder content in and around that band covers it. That level is the masking threshold. The quantiser then spends only as many bits as it takes to push the quantisation noise down to the threshold, and not one more. Raise the thresholds globally and the encoder decides more noise is acceptable, so it spends fewer bits and the file gets coarser at the same bitrate; lower them and it demands more precision everywhere and runs out of bits sooner, which under CBR means something else has to give. {\f1 --ath-scale} is the global scale on this threshold. {\f1 --spread-mask} changes how far masking leaks between neighbouring bands, which is a different question: not how loud the mask is, but how wide.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b SNR demand.} Separately from the threshold, FDK keeps a required signal to noise ratio per band, the minimum quality the coder must deliver in that band whatever the threshold says. This is the closest FDK gets to the tone masking noise and noise masking tone constants that MusePack and LAME expose. It matters more than it sounds, because the hole avoidance logic clamps thresholds back to this demand: if the rate loop wants to starve a band, the SNR demand is the line it is not allowed to cross. {\f1 --minsnr-scale} scales the demand, and the two clamp switches move the ceiling and the floor the demand itself is held between.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b TNS, temporal noise shaping.} Inside one block, quantisation noise is spread evenly across the whole block in time. That is wrong for a transient: a block containing a drum hit has noise sitting in the quiet part before the hit, where nothing masks it, which you hear as pre-echo. TNS applies a prediction filter across frequency, which has the effect of shaping the noise envelope in time so it follows the signal envelope and hides under the attack. {\f1 --tns-mask} chooses which of the filters are allowed to run and {\f1 --tns-order} how long they may be. For LD and ELD this is the main defence against pre-echo, since those profiles have no short blocks at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b PNS, perceptual noise substitution.} When a band is just noise, coding its exact spectral lines is a waste: nobody can tell one noise realisation from another at that level. PNS throws the lines away and stores a single number, the band's energy, with a flag saying "noise here". The decoder regenerates random noise scaled to that energy. The saving on noisy material is large. The risk is that a band which is not really noise gets substituted, and then a genuine tone turns into hiss. Eight switches in this group control PNS: whether it runs at all, from which frequency, whether the low bitrate gate applies, how loud the fabricated noise is, and three detection thresholds plus a minimum band width that together decide which bands qualify.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One convention before the cards: all the {\f1 --pns-*} scaling knobs take a plain decimal multiplier on FDK's factory value. {\f1 1.0} means leave the factory value alone, {\f1 1.5} means one and a half times it, {\f1 0.5} means half. {\f1 --pns-min-width} is the exception, it is a raw count.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 {\f1 --ath-scale <n>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b The global masking threshold scale: one knob for "be cleaner" or "be more economical" across the whole spectrum.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: every profile with an AAC core, so all of them. Under SBR it affects the core only, since SBR has its own noise model.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: Q8, {\f1 1..4096}, where {\f1 256} is x1.0; default: {\f1 256}, no change\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The masking threshold is the noise level the model believes you cannot hear in a given band. {\f1 --ath-scale} multiplies all of them at once. Above 256 the thresholds rise, the encoder concludes more noise is inaudible, and it spends fewer bits per band: more aggressive coding, which at a fixed bitrate means those bits go somewhere else or the frame simply finishes early. Below 256 the thresholds fall, more content is deemed worth coding accurately, and the demand for bits rises.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two things keep this knob from being as strong as its range suggests. It operates on the log domain threshold copy, and part of its effect is undone downstream by the minimum SNR logic and the 29 dB threshold reduction ceiling in the CBR quantiser. And under CBR a global threshold change mostly reshuffles bits rather than changing their number, because the rate loop still has to hit the target. If you want a stronger and more direct lever on detail, {\f1 --minsnr-scale} is the one, because it moves the line that the hole avoidance clamps back to.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Where it earns its keep is at high bitrate, where you have surplus bits and want the encoder to find more things worth coding. Values a little below 256 convert that surplus into preserved detail. At low bitrate the useful direction is usually upward, as a way of accepting coarseness deliberately rather than having the rate loop decide where to cut.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild, cleaner: {\f1 --ath-scale 230}. Thresholds about 10 percent lower. Slightly more bits to quiet detail; subtle at any bitrate, most visible above 192 kbps.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong, cleaner: {\f1 --ath-scale 180}. A clear shift of effort towards low level content. Reverb tails and room noise survive better; at a tight bitrate this starves something else.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated, cleaner: {\f1 --ath-scale 64}. Thresholds a quarter of stock. The model is effectively told almost nothing is masked. Under CBR the rate loop fights back all frame long and the result is not better, just differently compromised; worth hearing once to understand where the limit sits.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild, coarser: {\f1 --ath-scale 290}. A little more noise accepted per band, a small bit saving.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong, coarser: {\f1 --ath-scale 512}. Thresholds doubled. Audibly grainier on broadband material, noticeably cheaper per band.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated, coarser: {\f1 --ath-scale 2048}. Thresholds eight times stock. The encoder believes almost everything is masked, and the result is a caricature of aggressive coding: hollow, hissy, with detail gone wherever anything louder sits nearby.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: {\f1 --minsnr-scale} is the stronger tool for the same goal and partly overrides the effect of this one, since the SNR demand is a floor on quality that a raised threshold cannot push through. {\f1 --reduce-clamp 0} lets thresholds be pushed deeper than the 29 dB ceiling, which gives low values here more room to act. {\f1 --spread-mask} is the companion knob for the width rather than the height of masking.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 {\f1 --spread-mask <n>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Scales how far masking leaks from one band into its neighbours, which decides how many quiet bands next to loud ones survive.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: every profile with an AAC core. Largest effect where bits are tight but not desperate, roughly 96..192 kbps.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: Q8, {\f1 0} and up, where {\f1 256} is x1.0; default: {\f1 -1}, off\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A loud tone does not only mask noise in its own band, it masks a skirt of bands around it, more above than below. The model simulates that with a spreading function. The spread is the reason a quiet band sitting next to a loud one gets almost no bits: the model says its content is covered by the neighbour.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This switch scales the spreading. Below 256 means less masking spread, so the skirt shrinks, fewer bands count as covered, and more of them get coded. That is the same idea as relaxing the tone masks noise assumption in other encoders. Above 256 widens the skirt and more bands are written off as covered by their neighbours, which is cheaper and flatter.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason the effect concentrates in the 96..192 kbps region is simple arithmetic. Far below that, the bands next to loud ones are not the problem, the whole frame is starved and the rate loop is cutting everywhere. Far above it there are enough bits that the spread rarely decides anything. In between, the spread is what the encoder is actually using to choose what to skip.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild, less masking: {\f1 --spread-mask 230}. A slightly narrower skirt. A few more bands survive beside loud content; more detail around strong partials.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong, less masking: {\f1 --spread-mask 180}. Clearly more bands coded next to loud ones. On dense material this is where detail around transient energy is regained, at real bit cost.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated, less masking: {\f1 --spread-mask 32}. Spreading almost removed. The model loses most of its ability to decide what is covered, so bits scatter across bands that genuinely are inaudible; a caricature that shows what the spreading function buys you.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild, more masking: {\f1 --spread-mask 300}. A touch wider skirt, a small saving.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong, more masking: {\f1 --spread-mask 512}. Spread doubled. Noticeably flatter spectrum beside loud content, cheap, with quiet detail written off.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated, more masking: {\f1 --spread-mask 1536}. Almost everything near anything loud counts as masked. The result is hollow and smeared, and useful mainly as the opposite end of the demonstration.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: combine with {\f1 --ath-scale} below 256 when you want both a lower and a narrower mask; the two are independent and the effects add. {\f1 --minsnr-scale} still bounds the result from below, so a very low spread cannot push any band below its SNR demand.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 {\f1 --minsnr-scale <n>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Scales the signal to noise ratio the coder is required to deliver in each band; the most direct knob for detail in this group.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: every profile with an AAC core.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: Q8, {\f1 1} and up with no documented ceiling, where {\f1 256} is x1.0; default: {\f1 -1}, off\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Besides the masking threshold, each band carries a required coding SNR. It is the guaranteed minimum: whatever the threshold computation says, the coder must reach this quality in that band. FDK derives it from its tuning tables, and it is the nearest equivalent to the tone masking noise and noise masking tone constants that MusePack exposes directly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Below 256 demands a higher SNR than stock, so more detail and more bits per band. Above 256 demands less and the result is coarser. Note the inversion against your intuition: you are scaling the permitted noise, not the demanded quality, so smaller is cleaner.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This switch is more effective than {\f1 --ath-scale} for the same purpose, and the reason is structural. The hole avoidance logic clamps thresholds back to the minimum SNR line, which means the SNR demand is the quality floor the rate loop is not allowed to breach. Move the threshold and the clamp can undo you. Move the demand and the clamp follows.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild, more detail: {\f1 --minsnr-scale 220}. A modest lift in required quality per band. Visible at medium bitrate as fewer bands dropping out.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong, more detail: {\f1 --minsnr-scale 150}. Clearly higher demand. Combined with a generous bitrate, this is the knob that turns surplus bits into preserved detail; at a tight bitrate it forces the rate loop to make the cut somewhere else.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated, more detail: {\f1 --minsnr-scale 16}. A sixteen fold demand. The frame can never satisfy it and the rate loop spends the whole frame in rescue mode, so the result is worse than stock; instructive once, not a setting.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild, coarser: {\f1 --minsnr-scale 300}. A little less demanded per band, a small saving.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong, coarser: {\f1 --minsnr-scale 512}. Half the demanded quality. Audibly grainier, with a real bit saving that can be redirected with other switches.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated, coarser: {\f1 --minsnr-scale 4096}. The demand is effectively removed, the floor under quality is gone, and bands collapse freely. A caricature of what the SNR demand was protecting.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: {\f1 --minsnr-clamp-hi} and {\f1 --minsnr-clamp-lo} bound the result of this scaling, so pushing far below 256 eventually hits the stock ceiling of about -1 dB unless you raise it with {\f1 --minsnr-clamp-hi}. {\f1 --reduce-clamp 0} is the usual companion for extreme detail, since it removes the 29 dB reduction ceiling that otherwise limits how deep thresholds can be pushed. It is stronger than {\f1 --ath-scale}, so set this one first and use {\f1 --ath-scale} as a trim.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 {\f1 --minsnr-clamp-hi <n>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Raises or lowers the ceiling on how much SNR a band is allowed to demand.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: every profile with an AAC core. Only acts when some band actually wants more than the stock ceiling, which in practice means you are also using {\f1 --minsnr-scale} below 256.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: Q8, {\f1 1} and up, where {\f1 256} is x1.0; default: {\f1 -1}, off\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The per band SNR demand is held between a ceiling and a floor. The ceiling, FDK's MAX_SNR, sits at roughly -1 dB and exists so no band can demand an absurd amount of precision and starve the rest of the frame. If you scale the demand up with {\f1 --minsnr-scale}, bands pile up against that ceiling and the scaling stops producing any further effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Above 256 here lifts the ceiling, so bands may demand more than stock allows. That is the only way to make very low {\f1 --minsnr-scale} values keep working. Below 256 lowers the ceiling, capping the demand sooner, which is a blunt way to limit how much any one band can take from the frame.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild, higher ceiling: {\f1 --minsnr-clamp-hi 320}. A small amount of headroom above the stock cap. Lets a modest {\f1 --minsnr-scale} keep acting in the most demanding bands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong, higher ceiling: {\f1 --minsnr-clamp-hi 512}. Twice the stock cap. This is the pairing for serious detail work at high bitrate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated, higher ceiling: {\f1 --minsnr-clamp-hi 4096}. The ceiling is effectively gone and one band can demand everything the frame has. Expect the rate loop to spend its entire budget on a few bands and collapse the rest.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild, lower ceiling: {\f1 --minsnr-clamp-hi 220}. Demands capped a little sooner; a slight evening out of bit distribution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong, lower ceiling: {\f1 --minsnr-clamp-hi 128}. Half the stock cap. No band may ask for much, so the spectrum is coded more uniformly and more coarsely at the peaks.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated, lower ceiling: {\f1 --minsnr-clamp-hi 8}. The ceiling is so low that the demand is essentially removed as a mechanism, which is the same end state as removing it from below.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: only meaningful together with {\f1 --minsnr-scale}; alone it changes nothing unless stock demands were already at the cap. It is the counterpart of {\f1 --minsnr-clamp-lo}, which moves the other end of the same bracket.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Measured: at AAC-LC 64 kbps, {\f1 --minsnr-clamp-hi 512} changes the stream.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 {\f1 --minsnr-clamp-lo <n>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Raises or lowers the floor under the per band SNR demand, the level below which no band may be allowed to fall.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: every profile with an AAC core, but only where bands are actually being pushed down to the floor, which means very low bitrate. At comfortable bitrates the floor is never reached and the switch does nothing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: Q8, {\f1 1} and up, where {\f1 256} is x1.0; default: {\f1 -1}, off\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The other end of the bracket from {\f1 --minsnr-clamp-hi}. FDK's MIN_SNR floor sits at roughly -25 dB and is the worst quality any band may be assigned. Without it, a band under bit pressure could be allocated essentially nothing while still nominally being coded.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Below 256 lowers the floor and lets bands be driven further down, which frees bits for the rest of the frame at the cost of the worst bands getting worse. Above 256 raises the floor, so nothing may get as bad as stock permits, which protects the weakest bands and takes those bits from elsewhere.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Honest status: at ordinary bitrates this switch has no observable effect, because the floor is simply not reached. It is a tool for the 16..32 kbps region and for experiments where you have deliberately starved the core, for instance with a wide {\f1 --core-cutoff} at a low bitrate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild, higher floor: {\f1 --minsnr-clamp-lo 300}. The worst bands are held slightly better. Only visible at very low bitrate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong, higher floor: {\f1 --minsnr-clamp-lo 512}. Twice the stock floor. At low bitrate this reduces the gap between the best and worst coded bands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated, higher floor: {\f1 --minsnr-clamp-lo 2048}. The floor sits so high it meets the ceiling and the bracket collapses; every band demands the same, which is not a psychoacoustic model any more.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild, lower floor: {\f1 --minsnr-clamp-lo 220}. The worst bands may go a little further down, a small amount of bits freed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong, lower floor: {\f1 --minsnr-clamp-lo 128}. Half the stock floor. Audible at low bitrate as the quietest bands giving up earlier in exchange for a cleaner midrange.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated, lower floor: {\f1 --minsnr-clamp-lo 4}. No meaningful protection remains; bands under pressure are allocated almost nothing, which is close to letting them become holes without the hole logic being involved.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: pairs with {\f1 --minsnr-clamp-hi} as the two ends of one bracket and with {\f1 --minsnr-scale}, which is what moves the demand inside it. Group E's hole switches address the same symptom from the other side, by controlling which bands may be zeroed outright.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Measured: {\f1 --minsnr-clamp-lo 512} produced no change at AAC-LC 64 kbps, because the floor is never reached there, while {\f1 --minsnr-clamp-hi 512} did change the stream at the same settings. Expect this one to need a much lower bitrate before it acts.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 {\f1 --reduce-clamp <0|1>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Removes the 29 dB ceiling on how far the CBR quantiser may push thresholds down, letting the other detail switches act without hitting a wall.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: CBR, that is the default {\f1 -m 0} path. VBR uses a different code path and is unaffected.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 0} or {\f1 1}; default: {\f1 1}, the ceiling applies, as in stock\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number, on/off\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When the CBR rate loop needs more quality in a band, it reduces that band's threshold. There is a ceiling on the total reduction, about 29 dB, which stops the loop from chasing one demanding band indefinitely. For normal material the ceiling never binds. If you are deliberately demanding much more precision than stock, with {\f1 --minsnr-scale} well below 256 or {\f1 --ath-scale} well below 256, the ceiling is exactly what stops your setting from producing a further effect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Setting {\f1 0} drops the ceiling, so thresholds may be pushed deeper and more bits flow into the demanding bands. On its own this changes little, because stock rarely reaches 29 dB of reduction. It is a companion switch, not a standalone one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Values:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 1}: the 29 dB reduction ceiling applies (stock, default).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 0}: ceiling removed, thresholds may be pushed deeper.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Example in context:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 fdkaac -p 2 -b 256000 --minsnr-scale 150 --reduce-clamp 0 -o out.m4a in.wav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: pair it with {\f1 --minsnr-scale} below 256 for extreme detail; it is near pointless alone. No effect under VBR ({\f1 -m 1..5}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 {\f1 --tns-mask <n>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Chooses which of the temporal noise shaping filters the encoder may use.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: every profile with an AAC core. It matters most in LD and ELD, where TNS is the only defence against pre-echo, and on transient heavy material in LC.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 0..15}, a four bit mask; default: {\f1 -1}, stock {\f1 0xF}, all filters allowed\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 TNS shapes quantisation noise in time so it hides under an attack instead of sitting in the quiet before it. FDK runs more than one TNS filter, differing by block type and by the frequency region they cover, and the mask says which of them are enabled. Stock is {\f1 0xF}, which is 15, meaning all four bits set.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a diagnostic and laboratory switch rather than a quality knob. The useful settings are {\f1 0}, which disables TNS entirely and lets you hear what it was doing, and {\f1 15}, which is stock. Intermediate masks let you isolate one filter, which is interesting when you are trying to attribute an artifact, and rarely interesting otherwise.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Disabling TNS has a second consequence worth knowing: FDK forces PNS off when TNS is off. So {\f1 --tns-mask 0} silently takes perceptual noise substitution with it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Values:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 -1}: stock, all filters enabled ({\f1 0xF}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 0}: TNS fully off. Pre-echo on sharp attacks gets noticeably worse, especially in LD and ELD; PNS is forced off as well.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 1} to {\f1 14}: individual filters enabled by bit. For isolating one filter's contribution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 15}: all filters enabled, the same as stock.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Example in context:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 fdkaac -p 2 -b 128000 --tns-mask 0 --verbose -o no_tns.m4a attacks.wav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: {\f1 --tns-order} limits how long the enabled filters may be, so the two are read together. {\f1 --tns-thresh} in group E decides how often TNS switches on at all, which is the knob you usually want instead of the mask. Setting {\f1 0} here forces PNS off regardless of {\f1 --pns}. {\f1 --verbose} prints the effective TNS mask as a hex bitmask.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 {\f1 --tns-order <n>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Caps the length of the temporal noise shaping filter, trading how precisely the noise envelope can follow the signal against the side information it costs.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: every profile with an AAC core. Short blocks are additionally capped at order 5 by the standard, so values above that only affect long blocks.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 1..12}; default: {\f1 -1}, FDK's own order\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The TNS filter is a linear predictor running across frequency. Its order is how many coefficients it has, and the order determines how detailed a time envelope it can impose on the noise. A low order can only produce a crude shape, roughly "more noise later, less earlier". A high order can follow a complicated envelope, at the cost of sending more filter coefficients per frame as side information.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Lower values make TNS cheaper and blunter. At low bitrate that trade can be worth it, because the coefficients compete with the spectrum for the same bits. Higher values let the shaping track attacks more precisely, which is the direction to go when pre-echo is the problem and you have bits, particularly in LD and ELD where no short blocks exist to help.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild, longer filter: {\f1 --tns-order 8}. Slightly finer time shaping in long blocks, a small side information cost.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong, longer filter: {\f1 --tns-order 12}. The maximum. Best time resolution TNS can give; worth trying in LD and ELD on percussive material.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated, longer filter: {\f1 --tns-order 12} is already the ceiling, so the caricature here is combining it with {\f1 --tns-thresh 0.5} from group E so the long filter also runs on nearly every frame. Expect side information to eat a visible share of a low bitrate budget and holes to appear in exchange.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild, shorter filter: {\f1 --tns-order 5}. The short block limit applied everywhere. Cheaper, with slightly coarser shaping in long blocks.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong, shorter filter: {\f1 --tns-order 3}. Crude envelope shaping only. Cheap; most of the pre-echo benefit is gone but the gross shaping remains.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated, shorter filter: {\f1 --tns-order 1}. A single coefficient, which can express almost nothing about the envelope. TNS is nominally on and effectively useless, which is a good way to confirm how much of the benefit comes from the filter length rather than from TNS being enabled.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: {\f1 --tns-mask} decides which filters exist, this decides how long they are; {\f1 0} on the mask makes this irrelevant. {\f1 --tns-thresh} in group E decides how often they run. In LC, short blocks already address most transients, so the order matters less there than in LD and ELD.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 {\f1 --pns <n>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Turns perceptual noise substitution on or off, overriding the encoder's own decision.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: AAC-LC, AAC-LD and AAC-ELD cores under CBR. FDK forces PNS off when the bitrate mode is VBR and when TNS is off, and those rules are above this switch.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 0} off, {\f1 1} on; default: {\f1 -1}, the encoder decides\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number, on/off\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 PNS replaces a band judged to be noise with a single energy value and a flag, and the decoder regenerates noise at that energy. On noisy material the saving is large, and on tonal material the risk is that a real tone becomes hiss.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The encoder's own decision comes from its tuning table and depends mostly on bitrate. At 64 kbps PNS is normally already on, so {\f1 1} there is the same as stock and only {\f1 0} changes anything. Below about 28 kbps the table disables PNS completely, and at that point neither {\f1 1} here nor {\f1 --pns-start} does anything, because PNS is switched off higher in the chain; {\f1 --force-pns} is the switch that bypasses that gate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The honest use for this card is diagnosis and deliberate choice: {\f1 0} to hear what PNS was contributing, {\f1 1} to assert it where you believe the table is being conservative. {\f1 --verbose} reports the resulting PNS state, which is the quickest way to find out whether your request survived the gates.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Values:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 -1}: leave FDK's decision (stock, default).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 0}: PNS off. On noisy material expect the noise bands to be coded properly and the bits to come from somewhere else.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 1}: PNS on, where the gates permit. At 64 kbps and up this usually matches stock; at low bitrate it needs {\f1 --force-pns} to have any effect.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Example in context:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 fdkaac -p 2 -b 64000 --pns 0 --verbose -o no_pns.m4a in.wav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: {\f1 --force-pns} removes the low bitrate gate. {\f1 --tns-mask 0} forces PNS off whatever you set here, because FDK requires TNS for PNS. VBR mode ({\f1 -m 1..5}) does the same. {\f1 --pns-start}, {\f1 --pns-tonality}, {\f1 --pns-refpower}, {\f1 --pns-gapfill} and {\f1 --pns-min-width} all shape PNS once it is running and do nothing when it is not.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Measured: at AAC-LC 64 kbps, {\f1 --pns 1} gives the stock stream, since stock already has PNS on there, while {\f1 --pns 0} changes it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 {\f1 --pns-start <hz>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Sets the lowest frequency at which noise substitution is allowed to act.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: the same places as {\f1 --pns}, and only when PNS is actually running.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: frequency in Hz; default: {\f1 -1}, FDK's own start frequency\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 PNS is safe high up and dangerous low down. Low bands carry the fundamental tones that define pitch and timbre, and substituting noise there is immediately audible. FDK therefore has a start frequency below which PNS is never used, and it depends on bitrate and sample rate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Lower the start and more of the spectrum becomes eligible, so more bands get noise coded and more bits are freed, with a growing risk of substituting something that was not noise. Raise it and PNS retreats upward, which is safer and cheaper in savings.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 One practical trap: the value is snapped to a scalefactor band boundary, so a change of a few hundred Hz may land in the same band and produce an identical stream. Move in steps of a kilohertz or more when you are probing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild, lower start: {\f1 --pns-start 3000}. PNS reaches a little deeper. Slightly more bands noise coded on broadband material.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong, lower start: {\f1 --pns-start 2000}. Clearly more of the spectrum eligible. A real saving on noisy material; on anything with upper midrange tonality, listen for the tone turning grainy.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated, lower start: {\f1 --pns-start 500}. PNS allowed almost everywhere. Sustained notes lose their identity where the detector misfires, which is exactly the artifact the start frequency exists to prevent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild, higher start: {\f1 --pns-start 6000}. PNS pushed up a bit, a small loss of saving and a small gain in safety.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong, higher start: {\f1 --pns-start 12000}. Only the top of the spectrum may be substituted. Safe, with most of the benefit gone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated, higher start: {\f1 --pns-start 20000}. Above the coded band in most configurations, so PNS has nowhere left to act and this is effectively {\f1 --pns 0} by another route.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: does nothing when PNS is off, which at low bitrate means you need {\f1 --force-pns} first. {\f1 --pns-min-width} restricts PNS by band width rather than by frequency, and since bands get wider as frequency rises, the two restrictions overlap. {\f1 --verbose} shows the resulting PNS state.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Measured: at AAC-LC 64 kbps and 48 kHz the stock start is 4100 Hz. {\f1 4000} maps to the same scalefactor band and gives an identical stream; {\f1 2000} and {\f1 12000} both change it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 {\f1 --force-pns}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Bypasses the low bitrate gate that otherwise disables noise substitution entirely below about 28 kbps.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: AAC-LC, AAC-LD and AAC-ELD under CBR, at bitrates below the gate. Above the gate it changes nothing, because PNS is already permitted.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: flag; default: off\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: flag\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 FDK's tuning table has a bitrate row below roughly 28 kbps in which every PNS entry is zero, for every sample rate. The consequence surprises people: at 24 kbps, exactly where cheap noise coding would help most, PNS is completely off, and both {\f1 --pns 1} and {\f1 --pns-start} are ignored. The audible result is the MDCT style artifact on noisy content that you would otherwise associate with MP3.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This flag makes the encoder use the first active row of the table instead of the zero row, so PNS also works at 24 kbps and below. The rest of FDK's requirements still apply: PNS needs TNS enabled and a non VBR mode, and nothing in this build changes that.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Values:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab omitted: the gate applies, PNS off below about 28 kbps (stock).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 --force-pns}: gate bypassed, PNS available at low bitrate.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Example in context:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 fdkaac -p 2 -b 24000 --pns 1 --force-pns --pns-start 4000 -o out.m4a in.wav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: usually given together with {\f1 --pns 1} and often with {\f1 --pns-start}, since the point of forcing PNS is to then control it. Pointless with {\f1 --tns-mask 0} or under VBR, both of which force PNS off anyway. At bitrates above the gate it is a no-op.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 {\f1 --pns-gain <x>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Sets how loud the fabricated noise is relative to the energy of the band it replaced.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: wherever PNS is actually running.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 0.0} and up, where {\f1 1.0} leaves the energy unchanged; default: {\f1 -1}, off\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: decimal, up to 3 places, {\f1 .} or {\f1 ,}\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When PNS substitutes a band, the one number it stores is that band's noise energy, and the decoder regenerates random noise scaled to it. This switch scales the stored energy directly, so it is the "how loud is the substituted noise" dial and the PNS knob you will reach for most often.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 At {\f1 1.0} the regenerated noise carries the original band's energy, which is what the mechanism intends. Above {\f1 1.0} the fill is louder than the original, which helps when the substituted noise sounds too timid and the mix turns dull: real noise floors often feel quieter after substitution because the regenerated noise lacks the original's structure. Below {\f1 1.0} it is quieter, which is the move when the fill hisses more than the source did.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is pure taste territory with no correct value, which is why both directions are worth trying on your own material before settling.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild, louder: {\f1 --pns-gain 1.2}. Slightly fuller noise fill; useful when cymbal wash loses presence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong, louder: {\f1 --pns-gain 2.0}. Twice the energy. Clearly more hiss and air; on broadband noise this can sound closer to the source than the nominally correct value.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated, louder: {\f1 --pns-gain 8.0}. Eight times the energy. The substituted bands dominate the top of the mix with obvious artificial hiss, which is a quick way to hear exactly which bands PNS has taken.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild, quieter: {\f1 --pns-gain 0.8}. A slightly more discreet fill.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong, quieter: {\f1 --pns-gain 0.4}. Noise fill clearly below the original energy. The top sounds cleaner and also duller, and a noise floor can disappear.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated, quieter: {\f1 --pns-gain 0.0}. Substituted bands carry no energy at all, which means they are silent, so PNS becomes a hole generator. A caricature, and a useful demonstration of how much of the signal was being carried by noise bands.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: does nothing when PNS is off. {\f1 --pns-tonality}, {\f1 --pns-refpower}, {\f1 --pns-gapfill} and {\f1 --pns-min-width} decide which bands get substituted; this one only changes how loud the result is. Using it at {\f1 0.0} is a clumsier way to get what {\f1 --pns 0} gives properly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 {\f1 --pns-tonality <x>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Scales the tonality threshold that decides whether a band counts as noise and may be substituted.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: wherever PNS is actually running.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 0.0} and up, where {\f1 1.0} is the factory threshold; default: {\f1 -1}, off\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: decimal, up to 3 places, {\f1 .} or {\f1 ,}\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 PNS only fires on bands the encoder judges noise like, which it measures as low tonality. The threshold is the line between "tonal, code it properly" and "noise, substitute it". This switch scales that line.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Higher than {\f1 1.0} lets more bands qualify, including somewhat tonal ones, so the noise substitution gets wider: more of the spectrum replaced by a cheap description, more saving, and more risk that a genuine tone gets smeared into hiss. Lower than {\f1 1.0} makes the detector pickier, so only the clearly noisy bands are substituted, which is safer and saves less.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the knob that controls the breadth of PNS, as opposed to {\f1 --pns-start}, which controls where it is geographically allowed, and {\f1 --pns-min-width}, which controls it by band size.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild, wider substitution: {\f1 --pns-tonality 1.3}. A few more borderline bands become PNS. Small saving, little risk.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong, wider substitution: {\f1 --pns-tonality 2.0}. Twice the threshold. Clearly more bands substituted; on noisy material a real saving, on tonal material audible smearing of partials.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated, wider substitution: {\f1 --pns-tonality 20.0}. Almost everything above the start frequency counts as noise. Sustained tones turn to hiss and the top of the mix becomes a noise bed: a caricature that makes the mechanism unmistakable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild, narrower substitution: {\f1 --pns-tonality 0.8}. Slightly pickier detection.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong, narrower substitution: {\f1 --pns-tonality 0.4}. Only unambiguous noise is substituted. Safe, with much of the saving gone.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated, narrower substitution: {\f1 --pns-tonality 0.01}. Effectively nothing qualifies, so this reaches the same place as {\f1 --pns 0} while leaving PNS nominally enabled.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: {\f1 --pns-refpower} is the other half of the detection decision and both must agree before a band becomes PNS, so moving only this one can be blocked by the power gate. {\f1 --pns-gain} changes the loudness of the result, not the selection. {\f1 --pns-min-width} can exclude a band that passed this test.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 {\f1 --pns-refpower <x>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Scales the reference power threshold a band's level must satisfy before it is considered a noise candidate.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: wherever PNS is actually running.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 0.0} and up, where {\f1 1.0} is the factory threshold; default: {\f1 -1}, off\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: decimal, up to 3 places, {\f1 .} or {\f1 ,}\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Tonality is not the only test. Each band's power is also compared against a reference before the band can be a PNS candidate, which keeps the mechanism away from bands whose level makes substitution a bad trade. This switch moves that power gate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The two detection tests are conjunctive: tonality and reference power must both agree before a band becomes PNS. That is why moving {\f1 --pns-tonality} alone sometimes does nothing, and why this card and that one are usually tried together.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Higher than {\f1 1.0} moves the power gate in the direction that admits bands it otherwise rejects on level grounds; lower than {\f1 1.0} makes the gate stricter. Because the gate interacts with material level, the practical approach is to change it and read the result, not to predict it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild, looser gate: {\f1 --pns-refpower 1.3}. A few additional bands pass the power test.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong, looser gate: {\f1 --pns-refpower 2.0}. Twice the threshold. Combined with {\f1 --pns-tonality 2.0} this is where wide noise substitution really takes hold.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated, looser gate: {\f1 --pns-refpower 20.0}. The power test stops discriminating at all and selection is left entirely to tonality, which is a good way to find out how much work this gate was doing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild, stricter gate: {\f1 --pns-refpower 0.8}. Slightly fewer candidates.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong, stricter gate: {\f1 --pns-refpower 0.4}. Clearly fewer bands pass. PNS retreats to the bands where it is least risky.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated, stricter gate: {\f1 --pns-refpower 0.01}. Almost nothing passes, so PNS is on paper enabled and in practice idle.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: conjunctive with {\f1 --pns-tonality}, so test them as a pair. {\f1 --pns-gapfill} can still add a band between two PNS bands that failed this test. Nothing here matters when PNS is off.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 {\f1 --pns-gapfill <x>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Scales the threshold of the heuristic that closes a one band gap between two substituted bands.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: wherever PNS is running, and only when two PNS bands already sit on either side of a coded band. Honest status: wired, no effect observed on our test material.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 0.0} and up, where {\f1 1.0} is the factory threshold; default: {\f1 -1}, off\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: decimal, up to 3 places, {\f1 .} or {\f1 ,}\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When PNS has marked bands on both sides of a narrow gap, a coded island stranded between two noise bands is usually worse than extending the noise across it: the island is the only place with real spectral structure and it stands out. The gap fill heuristic can mark that band as PNS too, and this switch scales the threshold it uses.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Higher makes gap filling more willing, lower makes it reluctant. Both directions are subtle at best, because the precondition is strict: for AAC-LC the rule needs both neighbours to be PNS already, and on our material that configuration did not arise, so no value of this switch changed the output. The switch is present and wired, and it may act on material whose spectrum produces the required pattern, but do not expect it to be a tool you reach for.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 If you want a genuine lever on how much of the spectrum becomes noise, use {\f1 --pns-tonality} or {\f1 --pns-start} instead.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild, more filling: {\f1 --pns-gapfill 1.3}. Slightly more willing to close a gap.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong, more filling: {\f1 --pns-gapfill 3.0}. Clearly more willing, where the precondition occurs at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated, more filling: {\f1 --pns-gapfill 50.0}. Any gap between two PNS bands is closed. On material that triggers it, this produces contiguous noise regions where the encoder had kept an island.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild, less filling: {\f1 --pns-gapfill 0.7}. Slightly more reluctant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong, less filling: {\f1 --pns-gapfill 0.3}. Gaps are left as coded islands.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated, less filling: {\f1 --pns-gapfill 0.0}. Gap filling never happens, which is the closest thing to switching the heuristic off.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: depends entirely on {\f1 --pns-tonality} and {\f1 --pns-refpower} producing two adjacent PNS bands with a gap, so widening those first is the only way to give this switch something to act on.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Measured: no stream change across 4 test signals, 5 bitrates and 6 values of this switch in AAC-LC. The precondition for the AAC-LC gap fill rule, both neighbours already PNS, did not occur on that material.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 {\f1 --pns-min-width <n>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Sets the minimum scalefactor band width, in spectral lines, that a band must have before noise substitution may act on it.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: wherever PNS is running. Only values above the built-in default have any effect, which for AAC-LC means above 16.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: raw line count, {\f1 1} and up; default: {\f1 -1}, off, which means FDK's own minimum (16 for AAC-LC)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Scalefactor bands get wider as frequency rises. A narrow band at the bottom of the spectrum holds few lines and usually carries tonal content, and substituting noise there is conspicuous. A wide band high up holds many lines and is where PNS belongs. This switch expresses that preference directly, as a minimum width.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Note the asymmetry, because the obvious reading of the name is wrong. Raising the minimum restricts PNS to wider bands, so to the upper spectrum, and that works. Lowering it below FDK's own minimum does not open PNS up on narrower bands: FDK's default is already the effective floor, so values below it give the stock stream. There is one useful direction here, upward.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Unlike the {\f1 --pns-*} switches above, this is a raw count of lines, not a multiplier.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild, wider bands only: {\f1 --pns-min-width 24}. PNS restricted a little further up the spectrum. Small reduction in substitution, small loss of saving.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong, wider bands only: {\f1 --pns-min-width 32}. Only clearly wide bands may be substituted. Safer on tonal material, with a visible drop in the PNS saving.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated, wider bands only: {\f1 --pns-min-width 64}. At AAC-LC 64 kbps this leaves no qualifying band at all, so the result is identical to {\f1 --pns 0}: PNS is enabled and has nothing it may touch.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Downward there is no useful example: {\f1 2} and {\f1 4} both give the stock stream, because the built-in minimum of 16 already binds.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: overlaps with {\f1 --pns-start}, which restricts PNS by frequency rather than by width; since width grows with frequency the two restrictions reinforce each other and the tighter one wins. Does nothing when PNS is off.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Measured: at AAC-LC, values {\f1 2} and {\f1 4} give the stock stream, confirming that the built-in default of 16 is the floor. {\f1 64} at AAC-LC 64 kbps produces exactly the stream that {\f1 --pns 0} produces, so PNS is effectively off at that width.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 E. Core transients, pre-echo and spectral holes\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These thirteen switches reach the AAC core itself, under SBR. They matter most where the core is short of bits: AAC-LC stereo at 24..50 kbps with the bandwidth forced well above what FDK would pick ({\f1 -w 16000..20000}), and LD or ELD with a widened {\f1 --core-cutoff}. At comfortable bitrates most of them change little, because the problems they address hardly occur there.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two different failures show up under bit pressure, and it helps to keep them apart.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Pre-echo.} A long AAC block covers 2048 samples, about 43 ms at 48 kHz. The quantisation noise of a block is spread over the whole block in time. When a sharp attack sits late in the block, that noise also lands before the attack, where the ear has almost nothing to mask it: backward masking lasts only a few milliseconds. You hear it as a soft smear or a "pre-shadow" in front of castanets, hand claps, plucked strings or a piano hit. The encoder has four tools against it, and this group exposes all four:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 1.\tx360\tab the attack detector, which switches the frame to eight short blocks of 256 samples ({\f1 --attack-decay}, {\f1 --attack-floor}, together with {\f1 --block-bias} in group F);\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 2.\tx360\tab the grouping of those eight short windows into scalefactor groups ({\f1 --short-groups});\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 3.\tx360\tab the window shape, which decides how much noise leaks away from where it belongs ({\f1 --window-shape});\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 4.\tx360\tab for the cases that stay in long blocks, TNS (temporal noise shaping, which shapes the noise in time inside a block: {\f1 --tns-thresh}, {\f1 --tns-start}) and the pre-echo control, which limits how fast a masking threshold may grow from one frame to the next ({\f1 --preecho-rise}, {\f1 --preecho-depth}).\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 AAC-LD and AAC-ELD have no short blocks and a fixed window, so for them only TNS and the pre-echo control are left. That is why those switches matter more there than in LC.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Spectral holes.} When a frame cannot afford all the bands it is asked to code, FDK first relaxes the SNR it demands from bands that are peaks or valleys of the spectrum, and if that is still not enough, it cuts whole scalefactor bands to zero. A zeroed band is silence in that frequency range for that frame. Over time this sounds like a bubbling, flickering or "underwater" high end: bands come and go from frame to frame. FDK calls the whole mechanism "avoid holes", which is somewhat ironic: it decides which holes to make. The order is fixed: bands are cut in up to eight energy steps, quietest first, and only from a starting band upwards, so that the bottom of the spectrum is protected. The switches {\f1 --avoid-holes}, {\f1 --hole-start}, {\f1 --hole-start-short}, {\f1 --hole-spread} and {\f1 --hole-ladder} change that mechanism.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 There is no free lunch in either case. Under CBR the frame budget is fixed. A band that is not cut costs bits that some other band no longer gets, and a short block costs more side information than a long one. Every switch here moves bits around; none adds any.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 All measurements quoted in this group were taken after decoding, on two synthetic signals built for the purpose. {\f1 attacks48.wav} holds castanet-like clicks over a very quiet bed; pre-echo is the error energy in the 1024 samples before each attack, relative to the energy of the attack itself. {\f1 dense48.wav} holds dense wideband chords, bright noise and a hi-hat; a hole is a 1/3-octave band between 2 and 16 kHz whose original energy is above -70 dBFS and which comes out of the decoder at least 15 dB too low. The tools that produce these numbers are in {\f1 tools/core/} in the repository, so the measurements can be repeated on any material.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A starting point for AAC-LC stereo at 24..50 kbps with a wide band:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 fdkaac-franken-x64.exe -p 2 -b 32000 -w 17000 --hole-ladder 2 --avoid-holes 1 --window-shape 1 -o out.m4a in.wav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 {\f1 --hole-ladder <1..8>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Limits how far the encoder may go when it cuts bands to zero, so that a starved frame gets coarser instead of emptier.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: the AAC core in every profile (LC, the core of HE-AAC and HE-AAC v2, LD, ELD). Matters only when bits run out.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: 1..8; default: off, which is stock behaviour (8 steps)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When a frame is over budget, FDK sorts the candidate bands by energy into eight steps and cuts them step by step, quietest first, rechecking the budget after each step. With eight steps, a hard frame can end up cutting quite loud bands. {\f1 --hole-ladder N} stops after N steps. Only the quietest bands may then be cut. If the frame is still over budget, the rate loop solves the rest the other way, by raising the quantisation step in all bands: the result is more noise spread evenly instead of more bands missing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the strongest switch against the bubbling top at low bitrate. The trade is real but usually favourable: a slightly grainier fill in exchange for bands that stay present from frame to frame. The ear is far more tolerant of steady noise than of bands that blink.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples (only one direction exists: fewer steps than stock):\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --hole-ladder 6}. Only the loudest cut steps disappear. Hard frames lose a few fewer bands; easy frames are untouched.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --hole-ladder 2}. Only the two quietest steps may be cut. On dense material at 32..50 kbps this removes most holes (see Measured).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --hole-ladder 1}. Only the very quietest step may be cut, so nearly every band survives and the whole budget problem turns into coarser quantisation. On very dense material at 24 kbps this can sound uniformly gritty: a caricature of the trade.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: combines well with {\f1 --avoid-holes 1} and {\f1 --hole-start}. The rate loop can only raise the step so far; at extreme budgets bands still vanish because they quantise to zero on their own, which no switch here prevents.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Measured (dense48, LC stereo, holes after decoding): 32 kbps / 17 kHz: 37% to 8% with {\f1 2}; 40 kbps / 18 kHz: 37% to 1.7%; 50 kbps / 19 kHz: 35% to 0.2%. The error-to-signal ratio in the bands that remain gets 0.3..0.5 dB worse.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Under the hood: the limit on {\f1 enIdx} in the "allow more holes" loop of {\f1 adj_thr.cpp}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 {\f1 --avoid-holes <0|1>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Forces FDK's peak and valley protection on or off.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: the AAC core in every profile.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 0} off, {\f1 1} on; default: off, which is stock behaviour (on from 20 kbps per channel, off below)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number, 0 or 1\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Before cutting bands, FDK can relax the SNR it demands in a smarter way: it lowers the demand in valleys between spectral peaks and keeps it in the peaks, so that narrow loud bands survive and the savings come from where the ear notices them least. Stock enables this only from 20 kbps per channel upwards, which for stereo means from 40 kbps. Below that, right inside the 24..50 kbps range this group is aimed at, the protection is off and cutting starts earlier.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Values:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 1}: protection on at every bitrate. Below 40 kbps stereo this is the meaningful setting; at or above it nothing changes, because stock already has it on.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 0}: protection off at every bitrate. A diagnostic setting: it shows what the protection was buying you.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Example in context: {\f1 -p 2 -b 32000 -w 17000 --avoid-holes 1 --hole-ladder 2}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: on its own the effect is small (measured), because the protection only reorders where the savings come from; it helps most together with {\f1 --hole-ladder}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Measured: at LC 40 and 128 kbps stereo {\f1 --avoid-holes 1} gives a stream identical to stock (already on there), {\f1 --avoid-holes 0} changes it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 {\f1 --hole-start <sfb>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Protects the lower part of the spectrum in long blocks from being cut to zero.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: the AAC core, long blocks (LC, the HE-AAC core, LD, ELD).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: scalefactor band 0..51; default: off, which is stock behaviour (band 15 when the protection is on, band 0 when it is off)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Bands below this number are never chosen as holes. Stock starts at band 15, which at 48 kHz is roughly 2 to 3 kHz: the bass and the low mids are safe, everything above may be cut. Raising the number moves the protected region up; lowering it allows holes deeper down. Remember that a protected band still costs bits: the savings then have to come from the bands above, or from coarser quantisation everywhere.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 How many bands exist depends on profile and sample rate; {\f1 --verbose} prints the count as {\f1 active SFBs (long)}, 49 for LC stereo at 48 kHz.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, protecting more:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --hole-start 20}. A few more bands in the low mids are safe.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --hole-start 25}. Protection up to roughly the presence region; measured to cut holes from 37% to 26% at 32 kbps.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --hole-start 51}. No band in a long block may be cut by this mechanism at all. The whole budget problem lands on the quantiser; at low bitrate the top gets very coarse, and bands can still vanish by quantising to zero.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, allowing deeper holes:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --hole-start 12}. A little more freedom just below the stock start.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --hole-start 6}. Holes allowed down into the low mids.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --hole-start 0}. Even the bass bands may be cut. Useful only to hear what the stock protection prevents.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: the mirror switch for short blocks is {\f1 --hole-start-short}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 {\f1 --hole-start-short <sfb>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b The same protection for short blocks.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: LC and the HE-AAC core, frames coded as short blocks. LD and ELD have none.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: short-block scalefactor band 0..15; default: off, which is stock behaviour (band 3)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A short block has about 14 bands at 44.1 or 48 kHz, so each band is wide and the numbers are small. Stock protects bands 0..2.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, protecting more:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --hole-start-short 5}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --hole-start-short 8}. Most of the band in a transient frame is protected.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --hole-start-short 15}. No short band may be cut; transient frames get coarse instead.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, allowing deeper holes:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --hole-start-short 2}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --hole-start-short 1}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --hole-start-short 0}. The attack frame may lose its bass.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 {\f1 --hole-spread <dB>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Decides how readily a band counts as covered by its neighbours, and therefore as a safe candidate for a hole.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: the AAC core in every profile.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: 0.0..24.0 dB; default: off, which is stock behaviour (3 dB for long blocks, 2 dB for short blocks)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: decimal, up to 3 places, {\f1 .} or {\f1 ,}\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A band may be cut more safely when its neighbours mask it anyway. To judge that, FDK compares the band's energy with the energy spread into it from neighbouring bands, after lowering that spread energy by a margin. A small margin makes neighbours look strong, so many bands look covered and become candidates. A large margin makes neighbours look weak, so fewer bands look covered and more of them are protected.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is a fine knob. On our material it moved the hole count by one or two percent of bands, far less than {\f1 --hole-ladder}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, protecting more:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --hole-spread 4.5}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --hole-spread 9}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --hole-spread 24}. Neighbours practically never count as covering, so almost no band qualifies on that ground.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, allowing more holes:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --hole-spread 2}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --hole-spread 1}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --hole-spread 0}. The spread energy is taken at face value; any band sitting next to louder ones is a candidate.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Note: setting this switch applies the same margin to long and short blocks, while stock uses 3 dB and 2 dB.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 {\f1 --preecho-rise <dB>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Limits how fast the masking threshold of a band may grow from one frame to the next, so that quantisation noise cannot run ahead of an attack in a long block.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: long blocks in every profile. Most relevant in LD and ELD, and in LC with long blocks forced ({\f1 --block-bias 0}).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: 0.0..24.0 dB; default: off, which is stock behaviour (3.01 dB, a factor of 2 in power)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: decimal, up to 3 places, {\f1 .} or {\f1 ,}\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The psychoacoustic model computes a masking threshold per band and frame. In a frame that contains an attack, the threshold jumps up, because the attack masks a lot. But the noise of that frame also sits before the attack, where nothing masks it. Pre-echo control therefore caps the rise: this frame's threshold may be at most N dB above the previous frame's. Below 3.01 the cap is tighter (less pre-echo, more bits on onsets); above it the cap is looser (fewer bits, more pre-echo).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In LC the effect is usually small, because real attacks are already moved into short blocks before this cap matters. LD and ELD have no short blocks, so there this switch and TNS are the main defence.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, tighter:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --preecho-rise 2}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --preecho-rise 1}. Onsets in long blocks get noticeably more bits.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --preecho-rise 0}. A threshold may never rise at all from frame to frame. Every crescendo is coded as if it were still quiet, which wastes bits on loud passages and starves everything else under CBR.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, looser:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --preecho-rise 4.5}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --preecho-rise 9}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --preecho-rise 24}. The cap is practically gone: pre-echo control is off.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: {\f1 --preecho-depth} limits the opposite side of the same rule.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Measured: tenths of a dB of pre-echo energy in LC on attacks48 (the attacks already go to short blocks). The switch changes LD and ELD streams.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 {\f1 --preecho-depth <dB>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Limits how far pre-echo control may lower a threshold at most.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: the same as {\f1 --preecho-rise}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: 0.0..60.0 dB (the effect stops growing near 45 dB); default: off, which is stock behaviour (20 dB)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: decimal, up to 3 places, {\f1 .} or {\f1 ,}\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 When the rise cap pulls a threshold down, this switch sets the floor: the threshold may not end up more than N dB below what the psychoacoustic model computed for the frame. It keeps the cap from demanding absurd precision after a long silence, when the previous frame's threshold was tiny.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, allowing deeper lowering:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --preecho-depth 25}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --preecho-depth 40}. After silence, the first attack is coded very cleanly and very expensively.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --preecho-depth 60}. Practically no floor; under CBR a single onset after a pause can eat the reservoir. The internal factor bottoms out near 45 dB, so values between about 45 and 60 behave the same.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, shallower:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --preecho-depth 15}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --preecho-depth 6}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --preecho-depth 0}. A threshold may not be lowered at all, which switches pre-echo control off regardless of {\f1 --preecho-rise}.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 {\f1 --attack-decay <x>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Sets the memory of the attack detector that chooses between long and short blocks.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: LC and the HE-AAC core (the profiles with short blocks). In our test it also had no effect on LD or ELD.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: 0.0..0.99; default: off, which is stock behaviour (0.70)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: decimal, up to 3 places, {\f1 .} or {\f1 ,}\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The detector splits each frame into sub-windows, filters out the low frequencies, and compares the energy of each sub-window with a running average of what came before. A large enough ratio is an attack. The running average keeps a share of its value per sub-window: that share is this switch. Higher values mean a long memory, so an attack is compared with a long average; lower values mean the comparison is with the last moment.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A long memory is calmer and more selective on dense material, but after a loud passage the average stays high and an attack that follows can be missed. A short memory catches attacks right after loud passages, but on busy material it can fire too often and put tonal frames into short blocks, which costs bits and resolution.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, longer memory:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --attack-decay 0.8}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --attack-decay 0.9}. Measured: worst case pre-echo 2.9 dB lower on attacks48.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --attack-decay 0.99}. The average barely moves; attacks after loud passages are often missed.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, shorter memory:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --attack-decay 0.6}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --attack-decay 0.4}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --attack-decay 0}. Each sub-window is compared only with the one before; the detector becomes jumpy on noisy material.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 {\f1 --attack-floor <dB>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Shifts the minimum energy an attack must have before it counts.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: LC and the HE-AAC core. In our test it also changed an LD stream, not ELD.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: -60.0..30.0 dB; default: off, which is stock behaviour (0 dB shift)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: decimal, up to 3 places, {\f1 .} or {\f1 ,}\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Besides the ratio, the detector requires an absolute minimum energy, so that ticks in near-silence do not trigger short blocks. Negative values lower that floor: quiet attacks such as brushes, soft hi-hats or a quiet guitar pick also switch to short blocks. Positive values raise it: only loud attacks count.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, catching quieter attacks:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --attack-floor -6}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --attack-floor -20}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --attack-floor -60}. Practically any rise in a quiet passage counts. Expect short blocks on room noise and on soft tonal passages, with the bit cost that brings.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, only loud attacks:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --attack-floor 6}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --attack-floor 15}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --attack-floor 30}. Soft percussion stays in long blocks and pre-echo appears on it.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 {\f1 --window-shape <0|1>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Chooses the window shape of every block: sine everywhere or KBD everywhere.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: AAC-LC and the LC core of HE-AAC and HE-AAC v2. LD and ELD use a fixed window and are not affected.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 0} sine, {\f1 1} KBD; default: off, which is stock behaviour (KBD for long blocks, sine for short blocks)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number, 0 or 1\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The window decides how the energy of a block leaks into neighbouring frequencies. A sine window has a narrow main lobe (good selectivity next to a peak) and slowly falling side lobes (energy and noise leak far). A KBD window has a wider main lobe and side lobes that fall much faster. In time the same property means that KBD keeps quantisation noise closer to where it belongs. Stock uses sine for short blocks, which are exactly the blocks around attacks.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The choice costs one bit per frame and is legal everywhere in LC; the stream carries the shape actually used, also in short blocks, and every decoder applies it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Values:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 1}: KBD in every block. Measured: pre-echo lower by 3.4..6.1 dB on average and by 1.2..6.6 dB in the worst case at 24, 40 and 128 kbps. The best single switch against pre-echo in LC.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 0}: sine in every block. Better selectivity next to strong tonal peaks in long blocks, more leakage; mainly a comparison setting.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Example in context: {\f1 -p 2 -b 40000 -w 18000 --window-shape 1}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 {\f1 --short-groups <0..3>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Chooses how the eight short windows of a transient frame are grouped.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: LC and the HE-AAC core, frames coded as short blocks.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 0}..{\f1 3}; default: off, which is stock behaviour (the table FDK picks by attack position)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A transient frame consists of eight short windows. Windows in the same group share one set of scalefactors, so fewer groups means less side information and more bits for the spectrum, but coarser control of the noise in time. More groups means the noise can be shaped window by window, at a price.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Values:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 0}: the stock table (the same as leaving the switch off).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 1}: one group for all eight windows. The cheapest; the noise is spread over the whole frame, which partly undoes what the short blocks were for.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 2}: four pairs. Even time resolution across the frame. Measured: the fewest holes in short frames on our material.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 3}: tight around the attack: the attack window alone, one window on each side of it, the rest lumped. The most bits where the attack is.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Example in context: {\f1 -p 2 -b 32000 -w 17000 --short-groups 2}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 {\f1 --tns-thresh <x>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Changes how readily TNS switches on.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: every profile with a core: LC, the HE-AAC core, LD, ELD.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: 0.5..4.0, a multiplier; default: off, which is stock behaviour (1.0)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: decimal, up to 3 places, {\f1 .} or {\f1 ,}\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 TNS (temporal noise shaping) runs a prediction filter across frequency. Where the signal has a strong time envelope, such as an attack inside a block, the filter makes the quantisation noise follow that envelope in time, so the noise sits under the loud part instead of in front of it. FDK enables TNS in a frame only when the prediction gain is high enough to be worth the side information. This switch multiplies that required gain. Below 1 TNS is used in more frames; above 1 in fewer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 In LC, short blocks already handle the sharpest attacks, so TNS covers the softer ones. In LD and ELD, which have no short blocks, TNS is the main tool against pre-echo, and a lower threshold there is often worth trying first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, more TNS:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --tns-thresh 0.85}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --tns-thresh 0.7}. Measured: in LC at low bitrate the extra side information leaves more holes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --tns-thresh 0.5}. TNS on almost everywhere, also where there is nothing to shape; bits go to filter coefficients.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, less TNS:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --tns-thresh 1.3}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --tns-thresh 2}. Fewer bits on side information, slightly fewer holes, more pre-echo on soft attacks.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --tns-thresh 4}. TNS practically off.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: {\f1 --tns-mask} and {\f1 --tns-order} (group D) decide whether and how large the filter may be; this switch decides how often it is worth it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 {\f1 --tns-start <Hz>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Sets the lowest frequency the lower TNS filter covers in long blocks.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: AAC-LC and the LC core of HE-AAC, long blocks. Measured no effect in LD and ELD.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: 0..24000 Hz; default: off, which is stock behaviour (8 scalefactor bands, about 1.4 kHz at 48 kHz)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Stock keeps the lowest bands out of TNS. The value is rounded to the nearest scalefactor band edge, and it cannot go above the top of the TNS range.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, reaching lower:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --tns-start 1000}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --tns-start 400}. TNS also shapes the noise of low mids, useful on piano and plucked bass notes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --tns-start 0}. TNS covers the spectrum from the bottom, including the bass bands, where its side benefit is small and its errors can show as low frequency "breathing".\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples, starting higher:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild: {\f1 --tns-start 2000}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong: {\f1 --tns-start 5000}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated: {\f1 --tns-start 15000}. Only the very top is shaped; for most material TNS is then effectively off in long blocks.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 F. Block switching and bitrate control\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Six switches: one that biases the long/short block decision, and five that control how freely the bitrate may move from frame to frame. The bitrate five only make sense once you know how the engine underneath works, so that comes first.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b The CBR engine.} You select a target with {\f1 -b} and the encoder aims at it as an average. Frames are not individually forced to that size. At 48 kHz an AAC-LC frame covers 1024 samples, about 21.3 ms, so a 128 kbps stereo target works out to roughly 2730 bits per frame on average. That is the number the rate loop steers towards.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b The bit reservoir.} Even in CBR, a buffer sits between the frames and the target. When a frame is easy, the encoder codes it sparingly and puts the unspent bits into the reservoir. When a hard frame arrives, it borrows from the reservoir and spends more than its share. The average is preserved while the instantaneous bitrate moves up and down, which is why CBR AAC is never perfectly flat. The reservoir size decides how far the encoder can stray and for how long.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b The hard ceiling.} An AAC frame holds at most 6144 bits per channel, so 12288 bits for stereo. Nothing in this encoder lifts that; it is a limit of the format. Every reservoir and peak setting is clamped against it, which is also why the swings available here are moderate compared with a true variable bitrate encoder.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Quasi-VBR.} The problem these switches address is specific and you have probably heard it: a difficult passage arrives, the encoder releases a few frames well above the average, and then it chokes the following frames to pay the debt back immediately. The result is an audible dimming right after a loud moment. Widening the reservoir and allowing a short term peak lets the encoder hold the elevated quality longer and come back down gently instead. It is still the CBR engine with a bigger buffer, not content based allocation like LAME's, so expect a lighter touch than the name VBR suggests.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A note on the two hard per frame limits: do not set {\f1 --max-bits-frame} below the average or {\f1 --min-bits-frame} above it. Both of those fight the target the rate loop is trying to hit, and the result is worse quality rather than a different tradeoff. {\f1 --verbose} prints the reservoir budget block, including the average bits per frame and the ceilings, which is where you get the numbers to set these switches from.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 {\f1 --block-bias <n>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Biases the decision between long and short blocks without ever forcing a block type, so the stream stays standards compliant.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: every profile that has block switching, so AAC-LC. LD and ELD have no short blocks, so there is nothing to bias there.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 0..255}, where {\f1 128} is the encoder's own threshold; default: {\f1 -1}, off\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A long block gives good frequency resolution and bad time resolution; a short block the reverse. The encoder uses an attack detector to decide, frame by frame, which it needs. Get it wrong in one direction and a transient smears its quantisation noise backwards in time as pre-echo; wrong in the other direction and a steady tonal passage is coded with eight short windows that cannot resolve its partials, which costs bits and sounds rough.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This switch shifts the threshold the detector compares against. Above 128 makes short blocks more likely, so the encoder treats the material as more transient than it is. Below 128 favours long blocks, and {\f1 0} means practically long blocks only.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Importantly, it shifts a decision, it does not override it. Forcing a window type without recomputing the band layout and grouping produces an illegal stream that decoders reject, and this switch never does that; whatever you set, the output is a valid AAC stream.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The practical finding is that the middle of the range does little on most material. The decision on a given frame is usually not close, so only values near the ends flip it. Treat this as a three position switch in practice, with {\f1 0} and {\f1 255} as the interesting settings, and the middle useful only on material that genuinely sits near the decision boundary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild, more short blocks: {\f1 --block-bias 160}. Marginal frames go short. Visible only on material near the boundary.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong, more short blocks: {\f1 --block-bias 220}. Clearly more short frames. Better time resolution on percussive content, worse frequency resolution and more bits spent on tonal passages.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated, more short blocks: {\f1 --block-bias 255}. Nearly everything short. Tonal material is coded with eight windows that cannot resolve its partials and sounds rough and expensive, which is a good demonstration of what long blocks are for.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild, more long blocks: {\f1 --block-bias 100}. Marginal frames stay long.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong, more long blocks: {\f1 --block-bias 40}. Clearly fewer short frames. Cheaper on tonal material, with pre-echo appearing on real attacks.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated, more long blocks: {\f1 --block-bias 0}. Practically long blocks only. Every transient must be carried by a long block, so pre-echo becomes the dominant artifact on percussive material. This is also the condition in which the group E pre-echo switches finally matter in AAC-LC, since the usual escape route to short blocks is closed.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: with {\f1 --block-bias 0}, {\f1 --preecho-rise}, {\f1 --preecho-depth} and {\f1 --tns-thresh} from group E become the tools that are left against pre-echo, which is the same situation LD and ELD are permanently in. {\f1 --short-groups} and {\f1 --window-shape} only matter where short blocks still occur, so a very low bias makes them irrelevant. {\f1 --attack-decay} and {\f1 --attack-floor} change the detector itself rather than the threshold it compares against.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Measured: at AAC-LC 128 kbps on attacks48, only {\f1 0} and {\f1 255} changed the stream. {\f1 64}, {\f1 128} and {\f1 192} were identical to stock, which is the basis for treating the middle of the range as inert on ordinary material.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 {\f1 --vbr-reservoir <bits>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Sets the size of the bit reservoir, which decides how far and how long a frame may stray from the average.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: every profile, in CBR mode. This is the central quasi-VBR knob.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: bits, from {\f1 1} up to {\f1 6144 * channels} minus the average bits per frame, and clamped there automatically; default: {\f1 -1}, off\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reservoir is the buffer the encoder borrows from and pays into. Its size is the memory of the system: a small reservoir means every overspend has to be repaid almost immediately, which produces the dimming right after a climax; a large one means the encoder can stay above the average for several frames and then return gradually.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Bigger is therefore the direction for material that should keep living after a difficult passage. Smaller holds the average more tightly from moment to moment, which is what you want when a transport link has a narrow instantaneous budget. A good starting point is two to three times whatever {\f1 --verbose} reports as the default.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two behaviours to know. {\f1 0} is treated as unset and does nothing, so if you want tight tracking of the average use a small positive value or {\f1 --bitres-mode 2}. And any value at or above the ceiling, that is {\f1 6144 * channels} minus the average bits per frame, collapses to the ceiling, so there is no way to overdo it and no benefit in trying; asking for 100000 bits on a stereo file gets you the same stream as asking for the ceiling exactly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Read the numbers from {\f1 --verbose}, which prints the average bits per frame and the ceilings in its reservoir budget block. Setting this switch from a guess is how people end up at the ceiling by accident.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild, more breathing: {\f1 --vbr-reservoir 4000}. Slightly wider swings at 128 kbps stereo; the encoder recovers from a peak over a few frames rather than one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong, more breathing: {\f1 --vbr-reservoir 12000}. The practical quasi-VBR setting at 128 kbps stereo. Climaxes hold their quality and the return to average is gradual.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated, more breathing: {\f1 --vbr-reservoir 100000}. Far above the ceiling, so it collapses to the ceiling. The encoder uses the maximum breathing the format allows, and the caricature is instructive precisely because it shows how modest that maximum is: the 6144 bits per channel limit, not the reservoir, is what bounds AAC's flight.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild, tighter: {\f1 --vbr-reservoir 2000}. Below the usual default. Swings narrow, the average is tracked more closely.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong, tighter: {\f1 --vbr-reservoir 500}. Very little buffer. Hard frames cannot borrow much, so difficult passages are coded coarsely rather than expensively.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated, tighter: {\f1 --vbr-reservoir 1}. Effectively no reservoir. Every frame must fit its own share, so transients and dense passages have nowhere to go, which sounds like a worse version of what {\f1 --bitres-mode 2} does on purpose.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: pairs with {\f1 --peak-bitrate}, which raises the short term ceiling the reservoir can push against; the reservoir without a raised peak has less room to act. {\f1 --bitres-mode} can reduce or disable the reservoir regardless of the size set here. {\f1 --max-bits-frame} caps individual frames after all of this, so a tight cap makes a large reservoir pointless. With {\f1 --unlock-bitrate} the reservoir becomes more important, since overshoot is the normal case there.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Measured: {\f1 0} is treated as unset and produces no effect. Values at or above {\f1 6144 * channels} minus the average bits per frame collapse to the ceiling. {\f1 --verbose} prints the reservoir budget block that gives you both numbers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 {\f1 --peak-bitrate <bps>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Allows short term bitrate peaks up to the value you give while the average is still held at the {\f1 -b} target.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: every profile, in CBR mode.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: bits per second, above the {\f1 -b} target; default: {\f1 -1}, off\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reservoir decides how long a frame may stray; this decides how high it may go. Set it above your target and the encoder is allowed to let difficult frames reach that rate momentarily, while the rate loop continues to steer the average towards {\f1 -b}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Below the target it is ignored, which is deliberate: a peak ceiling under the average makes no sense and the encoder does not act on the contradiction. The useful range is therefore above {\f1 -b} only, and the natural way to express it is as a multiple: half again the target is cautious, double is generous.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The ceiling of 6144 bits per channel still applies, so asking for a peak beyond what the frame can physically hold gives you the frame limit instead. At 48 kHz stereo that is about 1150 kbps worth of frame, so for ordinary targets you have plenty of room before the format limit binds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild, higher peak: {\f1 -b 128000 --peak-bitrate 150000}. A small amount of headroom. Climaxes come out slightly less compressed.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong, higher peak: {\f1 -b 128000 --peak-bitrate 200000}. The practical setting, usually paired with a wide reservoir. Difficult frames reach well above the target and the average is kept.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated, higher peak: {\f1 -b 128000 --peak-bitrate 1100000}. Close to the format frame limit. The encoder may spend almost a whole frame's physical capacity on one frame, after which the rate loop must claw all of it back, so you get a dramatic version of the very dimming the quasi-VBR settings exist to prevent.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Downward there is no useful direction: any value at or below the {\f1 -b} target is ignored, so {\f1 --peak-bitrate 100000} with {\f1 -b 128000} behaves as if the switch were absent.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: the natural companion of {\f1 --vbr-reservoir}; both are needed for the "do not choke after a peak" behaviour, since one grants height and the other grants time. {\f1 --max-bits-frame} is applied after this switch, so an explicit frame cap wins over the peak. {\f1 --bitres-mode 2} makes this irrelevant by removing the reservoir that the peak would be drawn from.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Measured: the tip configuration in {\f1 --help} for quasi-VBR at about 128 kbps stereo is {\f1 -b 128000 --peak-bitrate 200000 --vbr-reservoir 12000}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 {\f1 --max-bits-frame <bits>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b A hard ceiling on the number of bits any single frame may use.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: every profile, in CBR mode.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: bits, at least the average bits per frame and at most {\f1 6144 * channels}; default: {\f1 -1}, off\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is the blunt instrument of the group. Where {\f1 --peak-bitrate} expresses a rate that the engine may reach, this expresses an absolute per frame cap that is applied afterwards and wins. If you give both, the explicit cap is what binds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 It is a constraint, not a quality knob, and its main use is meeting an external requirement: a transport that cannot carry a frame above a certain size, or a test that needs the maximum frame bounded. A reasonable cap is around one and a half times the average, which still allows real breathing while bounding the worst case.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Set it too low and loud frames are starved, which is audible as exactly the dimming you were presumably trying to avoid. Below the average it fights the target outright, and the encoder cannot both honour your cap and hit the bitrate you asked for.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild, higher cap: at 128 kbps stereo (about 2730 bits per frame average), {\f1 --max-bits-frame 8000}. Well above anything the engine would normally produce, so effectively a safety net.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong, higher cap: {\f1 --max-bits-frame 12288}. The stereo format maximum. The cap is present but never binds, which is the right setting when you want the switch documented in a command line without changing behaviour.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated, higher cap: {\f1 --max-bits-frame 100000}. Above the format limit, so it is clamped to {\f1 6144 * channels}. Harmless, and a useful reminder that no switch here lifts the frame limit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild, lower cap: {\f1 --max-bits-frame 4000} at 128 kbps stereo. Peaks trimmed, average untouched; close to the useful 1.5x guideline.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong, lower cap: {\f1 --max-bits-frame 3000} at the same target. Loud frames are clearly starved, since the cap sits barely above the average.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated, lower cap: {\f1 --max-bits-frame 2000} at 128 kbps stereo, below the average. The cap and the target now contradict each other every frame; quality drops across the board and nothing is gained, which is why the rule is to stay at or above the average.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: applied after {\f1 --peak-bitrate}, so the explicit cap wins when both are given. A tight cap makes a large {\f1 --vbr-reservoir} pointless, since the reservoir's bits have nowhere to be spent. Read the average bits per frame from the {\f1 --verbose} reservoir budget block before choosing a value.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Measured: AAC-LC 128 kbps on attacks48. Stock largest frame is 3816 payload bits. With {\f1 --max-bits-frame 3000} the largest becomes 2888; with {\f1 4000} it becomes 3440.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 {\f1 --min-bits-frame <bits>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b A hard floor on the number of bits every frame must use.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: every profile, in CBR mode.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: bits, from {\f1 0} up to the average bits per frame; default: {\f1 -1}, off\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The mirror of the ceiling, and the less useful half. A floor forces the encoder to spend at least this much on every frame, including frames that genuinely need less, such as silence or a sustained quiet passage. Those bits are not saved for later, they are spent, so the floor converts easy frames into wasted budget.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason it exists is constant rate transport and experiments: some downstream equipment prefers a predictable minimum frame, and sometimes you want to see what the quantiser does when it is told to spend more than it wants.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Keep it at {\f1 0} unless you have such a reason. Above the average it fights the target the same way a too low ceiling does, and the encoder cannot honour both.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild, higher floor: {\f1 --min-bits-frame 1000} at 128 kbps stereo. Silent and very easy frames get padded; the average has to be recovered from the harder frames.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong, higher floor: {\f1 --min-bits-frame 2000} at the same target, close to the average. Almost every frame is now floored, so the engine loses most of its ability to redistribute.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated, higher floor: {\f1 --min-bits-frame 2730} at 128 kbps stereo, that is the average itself. Combined with the average as a target, this is close to forcing a rigid per frame rate, except that {\f1 --bitres-mode 2} does that properly and without fighting the rate loop.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Mild, lower floor: {\f1 --min-bits-frame 200}. Barely a constraint; easy frames stay cheap.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Strong, lower floor: {\f1 --min-bits-frame 1}. Effectively no floor, the same intent as leaving the switch off.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Exaggerated, lower floor: {\f1 --min-bits-frame 0}. The documented off value for the floor; identical in effect to not setting it, and the setting to return to when an experiment is over.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: the opposite end of {\f1 --max-bits-frame}, and the two are usually discussed together as constrained VBR. A high floor wastes the bits that {\f1 --vbr-reservoir} exists to redistribute, so the two work against each other. {\f1 --bitres-mode 2} is the correct way to get rigid per frame behaviour.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Measured: AAC-LC 128 kbps on attacks48. Stock output 97056 bytes; with {\f1 --min-bits-frame 1000} 97111 bytes; with {\f1 2900} 97709 bytes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 {\f1 --bitres-mode <n>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Selects how much of the bit reservoir mechanism is active, from full breathing to a rigid per frame bitrate.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: every profile, in CBR mode.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: {\f1 0} full, {\f1 1} reduced, {\f1 2} disabled; default: {\f1 -1}, leave it to the encoder\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: whole number\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Where {\f1 --vbr-reservoir} sets the size of the buffer, this selects the policy. Full is the normal behaviour and the same as stock. Reduced narrows the reservoir's operation. Disabled removes it, so each frame has to fit its own share of the budget, which is the closest this encoder gets to a rigid frame by frame constant bitrate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The one clearly useful setting is {\f1 2}, and the use case is a transport or a test that needs each frame to be about the same size. The cost is real: a transient or a dense passage cannot borrow, so it is coded coarsely instead.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Values:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 -1}: leave it to the encoder (default).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 0}: full reservoir, the same as stock behaviour.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 1}: reduced reservoir. On our material this gave the same stream as {\f1 2}, so treat the distinction between the two as untested rather than established.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 2}: reservoir disabled. Frames become near constant in size and difficult passages lose their escape route.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Example in context:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 fdkaac -p 2 -b 128000 --bitres-mode 2 -o rigid.m4a in.wav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: {\f1 2} makes {\f1 --vbr-reservoir} and {\f1 --peak-bitrate} irrelevant, since there is no reservoir for them to act on. {\f1 0} is the mode in which the rest of group F is meant to be used.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Measured: AAC-LC 128 kbps on attacks48. {\f1 0} gives the stock stream; {\f1 1} and {\f1 2} gave the same stream as each other, so no difference between reduced and disabled was observed on that material.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 G. DAB+ output\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two switches, and a mode rather than a tweak. Everywhere else in this reference the encoder makes a file or a bare AAC stream; with {\f1 --dab} it makes a DAB+ superframe stream for terrestrial digital radio.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 {\f1 --dab}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Emits a DAB+ superframe bitstream per ETSI TS 102 563 instead of a file or a plain AAC stream.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: AAC-LC, HE-AAC and HE-AAC v2, mono or stereo, at 32000 or 48000 Hz. Replaces the normal output path entirely, so {\f1 -f} and the container options do not apply.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: flag; default: off (normal file or stream output)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: flag\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 DAB+ does not simply carry AAC in a different container. Three things differ. The transform is 960 samples rather than the usual 1024, so the frame length fits the radio timing grid. The audio is packed into superframes of exactly 120 ms. And because the air is hostile, with fading, tunnels and interference, the bitstream is protected twice: a firecode, which is a Fire CRC, guards the header, and Reed-Solomon RS(120,110) over GF(256) protects the payload, so a receiver can rebuild data that arrived damaged. The encoder does all of that.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The output is a raw {\f1 .dabp} stream, one superframe after another and nothing else. It is not a file for a media player; it is what a DAB+ multiplexer ingests. The usual chain is {\f1 out.dabp} into {\f1 odr-dabmux}, which combines several stations into one ensemble and emits ETI, and from there to a transmitter or to a software receiver such as welle.io or dablin for testing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Three constraints the standard imposes and the encoder enforces: the sample rate must be 32000 or 48000 Hz, nothing else is legal in DAB+; the bitrate must be a multiple of 8 kbps between 8 and 192; mono and stereo are both fine.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The bitrate convention here is its own. With {\f1 --dab}, {\f1 -b} is kilobits per second and the encoder multiplies by 1000 itself, so {\f1 -b 96} means 96 kbps. If you also pass {\f1 --unlock-bitrate}, {\f1 -b} reverts to literal bits per second, which is the one combination where the two conventions collide.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 You normally do not choose the profile. DAB+ practice, following the reference tool {\f1 odr-audioenc}, derives it from bitrate and channel count so that low bitrates automatically get the more economical tools:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab stereo at 48 kbps or below, that is a subchannel size of 6 or less, gives HE-AAC v2 with parametric stereo;\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab mono up to 64 kbps, or stereo up to 80 kbps, gives HE-AAC with SBR;\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab anything higher gives AAC-LC.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 So {\f1 -b 96} on a stereo file lands on AAC-LC, {\f1 -b 64} on HE-AAC and {\f1 -b 32} on HE-AAC v2 without you thinking about it. You can still force the profile with {\f1 -p} ({\f1 2} for LC, {\f1 5} for HE-AAC, {\f1 29} for HE-AAC v2) when you know what you want.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Values:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab omitted: normal output, and the encoder behaves exactly as stock.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 --dab}: DAB+ superframe output.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Examples in context:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 fdkaac --dab -b 96 -o out.dabp input.wav     # auto AAC-LC\line
fdkaac --dab -b 64 -o out.dabp input.wav     # auto HE-AAC\line
fdkaac --dab -b 32 -o out.dabp input.wav     # auto HE-AAC v2\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: {\f1 --dab-label} only does something in this mode. {\f1 -f} and the MP4 container switches are inapplicable, since there is no container. {\f1 --unlock-bitrate} changes how {\f1 -b} is read. The group D and F switches still act on the AAC core inside the superframes, so the detail and bitrate knobs remain available.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Measured: the streams decode with the independent faad2 decoder behind dablin, the DLS label reads back correctly, and the AAC-LC output is bit identical to what the reference {\f1 odr-audioenc} produces from the same input. Nine combinations were tested, 48 and 32 kHz against mono and stereo across the three profiles, each independently decodable.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 {\f1 --dab-label <text>}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Carries a static station label in the superframe, shown on a DAB+ receiver's display.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Applies to: {\f1 --dab} mode only. Without {\f1 --dab} the label is ignored, because there is no superframe to put it in.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Values: text, up to about 48 characters; default: no label\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Input: text string\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A DAB+ receiver shows text on its display, usually the station name and often the title of what is playing. That text travels in a channel called DLS, the Dynamic Label Segment, tucked into the superframe as X-PAD data. This encoder carries a static label: one fixed string for the whole file or stream.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Static is the operative word. The text does not change over the course of the encode. A truly dynamic label that updates track by track, and the MOT slideshow that puts artwork on the screen, are the job of {\f1 ODR-PadEnc} in the OpenDigitalRadio chain and are not part of this encoder.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The length limit of about 48 characters comes from three DLS segments packed into one PAD. Receivers vary in how much of a long label they display at once, so for a station name the useful length is shorter than the limit.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Values:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab omitted: no label in the stream.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\f1 --dab-label <text>}: the given string is carried as DLS. Quote it if it contains spaces.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Example in context:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \f1 fdkaac --dab -b 96 --dab-label "Radio DHT" -o out.dabp input.wav\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Works with / against: requires {\f1 --dab}. Independent of every other switch, since it touches the PAD and not the audio.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Appendices\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Reading {\f1 --verbose}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\f1 --verbose} prints the values the encoder actually initialised, before encoding starts. The block comes in sections. Most fields name themselves; the ones below need a word.\par}
