{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A higher {\f1 -w} than this only helps if there are bits for it: at 24..50 kbps a forced wide band is exactly where group E (holes) comes in. Above 20 kHz the core needs {\f1 --uncap-bandwidth} and a sample rate that has room for it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 The M4A container: what is mandatory and what can go\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An {\f1 .m4a} file is a tree of boxes. The playback skeleton is mandatory and has no switch: {\f1 ftyp}, {\f1 mdat} (the audio data) and {\f1 moov} with {\f1 mvhd}, {\f1 trak}, {\f1 tkhd}, {\f1 mdia} ({\f1 mdhd}, {\f1 hdlr}, {\f1 minf} with {\f1 smhd}, {\f1 dinf} and {\f1 stbl}, which holds {\f1 stsd}, {\f1 stts}, {\f1 stsc}, {\f1 stsz}, {\f1 stco}). Without it no decoder can find the audio.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Optional is the whole {\f1 udta} / {\f1 meta} / {\f1 ilst} block: the encoder identification tag ({\f1 \u169 ?too}), {\f1 iTunSMPB} (encoder delay for gapless playback) and all text tags.\par}
